INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1985,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10330654','National Cohort Study of Resource Utilization in Older Adults With Emergency General Surgery Conditions.','Hatchimonji JS, Mavroudis CL, Friedman A, Kaufman EJ, Syvyk S, Wirtalla CJ, Keele L, Reilly PM, Kelz RR.','2023','subconditions survivable fivefold thoughtfulness subconditions subcondition iwashyna elixhauser elixhauser equivalency nonmetropolitan graphing subconditions subcondition diverticular frailest hepatopancreaticobiliary elixhauser esophagogastroduodensoscopy ugi ugi intraabdominal intraabdominal hepatopancreaticobiliary esophagogastroduodenoscopy ugi 24hrs accredit designating clipping cauterizing facile miscoding espoused hcpcs nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473 r01ag060612 kelz r01ag060612 standeven nicolaou rigol antibiot havens peetz elhage schiffern paton laine laine ljungdahl sundbom nyman aboutanos crandall iwashyna odden rohde bonham e39 e43 schneeweiss lipsitz elixhauser elixhauser gems hatchimonji dowzicky scantling holena dultz canner efron canner dultz e662 olufajo brat zogg e160480 e160480 ingraham bilimoria raval hatchimonji dowzicky scantling keele wirtalla sampalis lavoie boukas tamim nikolis scalea rotondo gayed zarzaur placek korndorffer nassour spalding hynan dabrowski kauder service1 1no earley pryor hedrick kurichi ghaferi birkmeyer dimick dombrovskiy arumugam hcpcs nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473 nihms1904473','Abstract Background: Prior studies have sought to describe Emergency General Surgery (EGS) burden, but a detailed description of resource utilization for both operative and non-operative management of EGS conditions has not been undertaken. Methods: Patient and hospital characteristics were extracted from Medicare data, 2015–2018. Operations, non-surgical procedures, and other resources (i.e. radiology) were defined using current procedural terminology (CPT) codes. Results: 1,802,545 patients were included in the cohort. The mean age was 74.7 years and the most common diagnoses were upper gastrointestinal. The majority of hospitals were metropolitan (75.1%). Therapeutic radiology services were available in 78.4% of hospitals and operating rooms or endoscopy suites were available in 92.5% of hospitals. There was variability in resource utilization across EGS subconditions, with hepatobiliary (26.4%) and obstruction (23.9%) patients most frequently undergoing operation. Conclusions: Treatment of EGS diseases in older adults involves several interventional resources. Changes in EGS models, ACS training, and inter-hospital care coordination may be beneficial to the treatment of EGS patients. Keywords: emergency general surgery, medicare, resource utilization Introduction Historically, an acute abdomen was considered a life-threatening emergency with mortality rates in excess of 30%. ( 1 ) As surgical diseases were better understood and with increasing use of antibiotics through the course of the 20th century, ( 2 ) these conditions became survivable with surgery as the primary treatment modality. Nonetheless, morbidity and mortality for many surgical emergencies remain high with an estimated overall mortality rate of 12.5% (fivefold the risk in elective general surgery) and a major complication rate exceeding 30%. ( 3 ) Recently, innovations in technology and advances in minimally invasive techniques have changed care paradigms for many of these conditions. ( 4 ) Interventional gastroenterology and interventional radiology may provide primary management of some of the most common emergencies such as abscess drainage and gastrointestinal bleeding. ( 5 – 7 ) But, these conditions still fall under the umbrella of emergency surgery and may require operative management when less invasive options fail. Thus, a thorough look at emergency general surgery (EGS) requires thoughtfulness about both operative and non-operative management. The management of EGS patients and the resources needed to provide appropriate care are changing rapidly. Given the dramatic changes in management of emergency general surgery conditions over time, in order to define the modern needs of this high-risk population of patients, we sought to characterize the resources used to diagnose and treat patients with intra-abdominal general surgical emergencies in a cohort of older adults. Our hypotheses were that there would be relatively low rates of operative management and significant interhospital variability in the availability of non-surgical resources commonly used in EGS. Methods We performed a nationwide, retrospective cohort study of older adults, age 65.5 years or older enrolled in Medicare fee for service plans who were admitted via an emergency room with an International Classification of Diseases – Version 9 or 10 principal diagnosis code for an emergency general surgery condition between July 1, 2015 and June 30, 2018. Patients were included if they had an emergency general surgical diagnosis of interest based on groups outlined by Shafi et al: colorectal, general abdominal, hepatobiliary, hernia, intestinal obstruction, resuscitation (i.e. acute respiratory failure or shock), or upper gastrointestinal. We created a list of codes based on these categories, which are listed in Supplemental Digital Content Table 1 . ( 8 ) These groups contain subconditions as noted in the table. For selected conditions that often require multispecialty care such as gastrointestinal bleed patients (a subcondition), a subset analysis was performed. Patients were excluded if they had incomplete Medicare Part A or B enrollment, were enrolled in a Medicare HMO program within 6 months of the index admission, or if they had a principal diagnosis code for a Shafi condition which fell outside of the population of interest (i.e., soft tissue infection, cardiothoracic, vascular). Patient characteristics including age, sex, race, dual-eligibility, frailty index, number of comorbidities, transfer status and the presence of sepsis on hospital admission were abstracted directly from the Medicare files. Sepsis was identified using the method described by Iwashyna et al to identify “Angus-positive” patients. ( 9 ) Dual-eligibility for Medicaid was included as a measure of socioeconomic status. Frailty was defined using the claims-based frailty index (CFI), created by Kim et al and validated for use in claims datasets. ( 10 ) Comorbidities were identified using a 6-month look back period for Elixhauser comorbidity index diagnoses (a total of 38 diagnoses) included in inpatient and outpatient healthcare encounters. ( 11 ) Codes were identified using the Healthcare Cost and Utilization project (HCUP) Elixhauser Comorbidity Software Refined for ICD-10-CM, ( 12 ) in combination with CMS general equivalency mapping (GEM) to convert back to ICD-9 codes. ( 13 ) Hospital characteristics were identified directly from Medicare files and the American Hospital Association (AHA) Annual Hospital Survey. Ownership type, bed size, metropolitan vs nonmetropolitan status, critical access status, teaching hospital status and the availability of hospital services were included. The definitions of these categories are available at the AHA website. ( 14 ) The primary outcome of interest was overall rate of operative management during the index admission. We also examined the use of radiographic services, non-surgical interventions including endoscopy, interventional radiology, transfusions, and intensive care, also during the index admission. Resources were assigned based on International Classification of Diseases – Version 9 or 10, Current Procedural Terminology and Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System codes (See Supplemental Digital Content Table 2 for codes). Each outcome was examined across the overall population and within condition groups. Descriptive statistics were performed. Univariate analysis was performed using ANOVA tests for continuous outcomes and the chi-squared test for binary or categorical data. Stata version 16.1 (College Station, TX) was used for the statistical analysis. Graphpad PRISM 8.1 for Windows (Graphpad software, San Diego, CA, www.graphpad.com ) was used for graphing. Results are reported in compliance with the STROBE guidelines ( Supplemental Digital Content Table 3 ). ( 15 ) The study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of the University of Pennsylvania (Protocol #832059). Results Patient Characteristics The demographic data and baseline characteristics of the 1,802,545 patients included in the study are summarized in Table 1 overall and by EGS condition. The majority of patients were female (55.8%) and white (81.7%). Nearly one-quarter were dual eligible for Medicare and Medicaid (22.7%). The mean age was 74.7 years (standard deviation 12.7). The most frequent conditions were related to the upper gastrointestinal tract including appendicitis (26.9%). There was considerable but predictable variation among subconditions; for example, the most common subcondition within the Upper GI category was bleeding (14.3%) and the most common in colorectal was diverticular disease (8.9%). Hernias were the least common of the EGS conditions presenting for hospitalization (0.6%). Patients requiring hospitalization for resuscitation as an EGS condition were the frailest of the EGS patients, the most likely to be transferred from another hospital (2.6%), and the most likely to present with sepsis on admission (32.2%). Table 1. Patient Characteristics of Medicare Beneficiaries Admitted with Emergency General Surgery Conditions Overall General Abdominal Colorectal HPB Hernia Intestinal Obstruction Resuscitation Upper GI p-value N (%) 1,802,545 (100.0) 241,163 (13.4) 334,508 (18.6) 270,960 (15.0) 11,200 (0.6) 184,515 (10.2) 274,602 (15.2) 485,597 (26.9) -- Age, mean (SD) 74.7 (12.7) 71.8 (14.3) 76.4 (11.7) 72.9 (12.9) 75.7 (12.7) 75.6 (12.1) 74.0 (12.8) 76.0 (12.0) <0.001 Female, N (%) 1,006,