The majority of treating hospitals were metropolitan (75.1%), private, not-for-profit (45.2%), and small with less than 100 beds (45.2%). The majority of care was provided in non-teaching hospitals (54.9%). Twenty-five percent of hospitals were labeled as critical access. There were subtle, yet statistically significant differences in the types of hospitals treating patients with different EGS conditions. ( Figure 1 ) Figure 1. Open in a new tab Characteristics of Hospitals Treating Emergency General Surgery Patients There was variation across hospitals in the availability of services commonly employed in the management of EGS patients as seen in Figure 2 . Nearly all hospitals had general diagnostic radiology services (99.3%). Magnetic resonance imaging was available in 74.3% of hospitals. Therapeutic radiology services were available in 78.4% of hospitals. There were operating rooms or endoscopy suites present in 92.5% of the hospitals. Of note, 2.5% of patients in this data set were transferred to another hospital for further care. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Availability of Hospital Services in Hospitals Treating Emergency General Surgery Patients Operative Management Overall, 10.0% underwent an operation and 36.6% underwent any procedure, with the Esophagogastroduodensoscopy (EGD) being used most frequently (20.3%) and interventional radiology being used the least (1.8%). Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography (ERCP) was infrequently used in all categories; the category with the greatest use of ERCP was HPB with a 1.8% rate of ERCP. A small minority of patients (n=9,907, 0.6%) underwent both an operation and either endoscopy or an interventional radiology procedure. There were notable differences in the use of operative and procedural interventions across the EGS condition categories. For example, 26.4% of HPB patients underwent an operation while 3.1% of colorectal conditions necessitated an operation. Within the upper GI conditions, 15.8% of patients without a bleed underwent an operation compared to only 0.4% of those with a GI bleed. Conversely, 60.3% UGI patients without a bleed underwent endoscopy including a 9.0% rate of colonoscopy, as compared to 82.3% of UGI patients with a bleed undergoing endoscopy including 30.7% colonoscopy. Intraabdominal interventional radiology procedures were used most frequently for patients with HPB (4.7%) and general abdominal (4.0%) conditions. Resource Utilization Resource utilization varied significantly by EGS condition in terms of the utilization of diagnostic imaging, interventional procedures, and the use of an Intensive Care Unit. In the overall cohort, chest X-ray was the most frequently used diagnostic imaging modality (55.6%), with Computed Tomography (CT) used nearly as often (47.9%), and Gastrointestinal bleeding scan seldom used (1.8%). Chest X-rays were most often used in patients admitted for resuscitation (91.1%), and CT most often used for those admitted for obstruction (76.7%). Notably, even in those patients admitted for GI bleed, the GI bleeding scan was seldom used (4.9%). ( Figure 3 ). Overall, 32.4% of patients required an ICU stay. Resuscitation (59.5%) and GI bleed (38.2%) patients most frequently required ICU admission ( Figure 4 ). The overall length of stay differed across EGS conditions, as did the total costs of care during the index admission. ( Table 2 ). The overall median LOS was 4.0 days [2,6] ( Figure 5 ) with a total unadjusted cost of admission of $7,789 [5,186, 12,221]. Figure 3. Open in a new tab Utilization of Diagnostic Radiology Studies During Index Emergency General Surgery Admission. p<0.001 for all comparisons across emergency general surgery condition groups. Figure 4. Open in a new tab Procedure Utilization During Index Emergency General Surgery Admission. p<0.001 for all comparisons across emergency general surgery condition groups. Table 2. Resource Utilization by Medicare Beneficiaries Admitted with Emergency General Surgery 