01 Interventional radiology procedure (intraabdominal) 31,962 (1.8) 9,639 (4.0) 1,779 (0.5) 12,783 (4.7) 94 (0.8) 1,081 (0.6) 1,401 (0.5) 5,185 (1.1) <0.001 Transfusion of blood, N (%) 242,357 (13.4) 15,835 (6.6) 45,560 (13.6) 10,107 (3.7) 472 (4.2) 7,317 (4.0) 16,304 (5.9) 146,762 (30.2) <0.001 Open in a new tab Notes: (1) HPB = hepatopancreaticobiliary; CT = Computed Tomography; MRI = Magnetic Resonance Imaging. (2) EGD = Esophagogastroduodenoscopy (3) p-values represent comparisons across Emergency General Surgery Condition groups. (4) Overall and ICU length of stay imputed with mean for patients who died in the hospital. Figure 5. Open in a new tab Mean Total Length of Stay Across Index Admissions for Emergency General Surgery Conditions (*** indicates p<0.001) Discussion In this study, we aimed to better characterize the use of resources for older EGS patients, in an attempt to define the needs of a modern institution treating EGS patients and the needs of a modern surgical workforce trained to care for these patients. Using a nationwide cohort of Medicare beneficiaries, we found that patients in this age group often presented with UGI pathology and/or sepsis, and required resuscitation and ICU admission. Only 10% of these patients actually required an operation, with many others requiring other forms of advanced intervention. While there was some homogeneity among the majority of hospitals treating these patients (vast majority metropolitan, small hospitals), many do not have continuous access to the services required to treat these patients. The current study is somewhat unique in that it is an extensive evaluation of EGS patients that includes non-operative patients. The field of EGS is still emerging, and attempts at determining the best way to capture and evaluate EGS-specific performance are still very much in progress. ( 16 ) Because EGS research has thus far been performed in the same fashion and using much of the same data as elective surgery – an area in which there are often limited non-operative forms of management – work has necessarily focused on operative EGS. ( 17 – 21 ) Given that the vast majority of patients with EGS diagnoses do not undergo surgery, the inclusion of these patients in EGS studies is imperative. We have previously published on outcomes in this population and now add to this work by examining resource utilization. ( 22 ) Resource utilization studies have been performed before; not long after the publication of the Shafi definitions of EGS, Gale et al published a study quantifying the public health burden of EGS. In it, they showed that EGS admissions comprise over 7% of all hospital admissions in the U.S., that the burden was increasing over the time period in question (2001–2010), and that the majority of these patients were non-operative. It is worth noting that they showed a 28.8% rate of operative management, markedly higher than the 10.0% we have shown here. This may have to do, in part, with the difference in population; Gale et al studied the convenience sample of hospital discharges enrolled in the National Inpatient Sample, while the current study included all non-federal, hospitalizations and focused on older adults only. Even among the Medicare population, we have previously reported rates closer to 30% when examining only those patients who were seen in surgical consultation; the present study includes all patients with an EGS diagnosis. ( 22 ) The fact that many of these patients underwent other treatments (i.e. endoscopy) is important to note from the perspective of resource utilization. Clearly, the care of EGS patients in the modern era is multidisciplinary and resource-intensive, requiring capabilities beyond surgery. Non-operative patients make up a significant proportion of the EGS population and it is worth a detailed evaluation of their needs. The mismatch between these diagnoses – commonly considered surgical diseases – and the non-operative resources often employed to treat them is an indication that institutions must make some adjustments. For example, upper GI pathology, including a high proportion of GI bleeding, was common, yet endoscopy and interventional radiology was not universally available. We lack data on variation in resource availability among types of hospitals, given there were many different phenotypes of hospitals and we only have information on teaching and critical access status. Even among hospitals possessing these services, they may not be available 24hrs a day. We recognize, of course, the logistical and financial challenges to providing such resources at every hospital. ( 23 ) Thus, it may make more sense to accredit hospitals as tiered EGS “centers” in a manner similar to trauma center designation. ( 24 ) Notably, while we reported the rate of transfers in among the admissions studied here as 1.2%, 2.5% of patients were transferred out . This number is in line with prior work on the topic. ( 25 ) It is possible that some of these patients were, in fact, transferred to centers with more advanced capabilities. If we are to consider designating EGS “centers,” it may also benefit patients to delineate a set of criteria for transfer. Additionally, the data presented here may be reason to consider a change in training paradigm for acute care surgeons. Currently, increasing numbers of graduating chief residents intending to practice acute care surgery complete advanced training in surgical critical care and trauma. ( 26 , 27 ) While this pathway holds many merits, it may be time to thoughtfully consider more focused training for those surgeons that are bound to care for EGS patients. Given the frequency with which non-surgical procedures are used in EGS patients, it may benefit ACS trainees and their patients to provide formal training in these areas. General surgery residents are already required to demonstrate proficiency in endoscopy. ( 28 ) As such, it seems reasonable to add training in advanced endoscopic techniques, such as clipping or cauterizing, particularly for ACS fellows. Similarly, it is common for today’s surgical residents to be facile with ultrasound and angiography; ( 29 ) one could imagine an ACS training program in which surgeons are trained to perform basic image-guided non-surgical procedures. We recognize that such a change in training paradigm would only be helpful at centers at which EGS call is taken by ACS-trained surgeons, as opposed to other varieties of general surgeon. However, given evidence that a dedicated ACS service improves outcomes ( 30 , 31 ) and the aforementioned increase in graduating residents pursuing ACS training, one could argue that centers should strive to limit the amount of call that is taken by non-ACS surgeons. Furthermore, we recognize that such a change in paradigm may impact non-surgical services at many hospitals (i.e., GI and IR). We would encourage each center to address its individual needs; if these services are readily available at all hours and would prefer to take such cases, this would seem quite reasonable. This is a retrospective, observational study of Medicare data over the course of 3 years. Strengths include the comprehensiveness, quality, and detail of the data. Limitations include the potential for miscoding or misclassification, though both Medicare data ( 32 ) and the Shafi/AAST EGS definition ( 3 ) have been extensively used and are generally accepted as reliable. We chose to use Medicare because the data is robust and because EGS diagnoses increasingly impact the elderly population. A landmark 2014 paper on EGS burden notes the mean age of those admitted with EGS diagnoses to be 58.7 years; 49.6% were over the age of 60. ( 33 ) Nonetheless, we recognize this may both impact the rate of operative management and limit generalizability. As we used the EGS definitions espoused by the field which classify appendix pathology as an “upper” GI condition, we may report a higher rate of upper GI pathology than is accurate. Finally, the use of Medicare data necessarily requires a focus on patients over the age of 65, a population that may differ significantly from younger EGS patients. This may limit the generalizability of this data. In conclusion, common EGS diseases are often non-operative and thus require a variety of resources to adequately treat. Many hospitals lack continuous resources to provide such care, and current training paradigms do not provide surgical trainees the full breadth of experience to be able to do the same. Changes in hospital-level EGS models, ACS subspecialty training, and inter-hospital coordination of care may be beneficial to the treatment of EGS patients in the current era. Supplementary Material 1 Supplemental Digital Content Table 2. Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) and Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System (HCPCS) codes to identify EGS Resources. NIHMS1904473-supplement-1.docx (22.8KB, docx) 2 Supplemental Digital Content Table 3. STROBE Checklist.( 15 ) NIHMS1904473-supplement-2.docx (19.8KB, docx) 3 Supplemental Digital Content Table 1. EGS diseases and associated ICD-9 and ICD-10 codes, based on Shafi et al. ( 8 ) NIHMS1904473-supplement-3.docx (74.9KB, docx) 4 NIHMS1904473-supplement-4.docx (16.2KB, docx) 5 NIHMS1904473-supplement-5.docx (16.5KB, docx) Acknowledgements Research reported in this publication was supported by the National Institute on Aging of the National Institutes of Health under Award Number R01AG060612. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the National Institutes of Health. Conflicts of Interest and Source of Funding: No authors have conflicts to declare. Dr. Kelz is supported by the NIH National Institute on Aging, grant R01AG060612 Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References 1. Standeven A The Acute Abdomen in the Aged. BMJ 1955:2:1184–1187. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Nicolaou KC, Rigol S. A Brief History of Antibiotics and Select Advances in their Synthesis. J Antibiot 2018:71:153–184. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Havens JM, Peetz AB, Do WS, Cooper Z, Kelly E, et al. The excess morbidity and mortality of emergency general surgery. J Trauma Acute Care Surg 2015:78:306–311. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Arnold M, Elhage S, Schiffern L, Lauren Paton B, Ross SW, et al. Use of minimally invasive surgery in emergency general surgery procedures. Surg Endosc 2020:34:2258–2265. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Laine L CLINICAL PRACTICE. Upper Gastrointestinal Bleeding Due to a Peptic Ulcer. N Engl J Med 2016:374:2367–2376. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Laine L, Shah A. Randomized trial of urgent vs. elective colonoscopy in patients hospitalized with lower GI bleeding. Am J Gastroenterol 2010:105:2636–2641. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Eriksson LG, Ljungdahl M, Sundbom M, Nyman R. Transcatheter arterial embolization versus surgery in the treatment of upper gastrointestinal bleeding after therapeutic endoscopy failure. J Vasc Interv Radiol 2008:19:1413–1418. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Shafi S, Aboutanos MB, Agarwal SJ, Brown CVR, Crandall M, et al. Emergency general surgery: Definition and estimated burden of disease. J Trauma Acute Care Surg 2013:74:1092–1097. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Iwashyna TJ, Odden A, Rohde J, Bonham C, Kuhn L, et al. Identifying patients with severe sepsis using administrative claims: patient-leve