INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1989,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10038852','Greater patient sharing between hospitals is associated with better outcomes for transferred emergency general surgery patients.','Fernandes-Taylor S, Yang Q, Yang DY, Hanlon BM, Schumacher JR, Ingraham AM.','2022','doubles diverticulitis breakdowns pursuant statute ruca dsh allotment uncompensated 0450a igraph dsh tertiles subnetworks subnetwork subnetwork subnetworks subnetwork subnetworks subnetworks dsh dsh owed dsh dsh dsh dsh routing rerouting protocoled parsimonious ebbs meza k08144 aad5359 ctsa ul1tr002373 dxs oper bruenderman kehdy benns motameni mccrum kaafarani santry crandall e71 e77 ingraham zuckerbraun regionalize regionalize brunson laubenbacher ogola dombrovskiy arumugam e160640 holena wirtalla sarani kelz reinke santry janjua petrovick velmahos nontraumatic regionalized strachan combes luyt trouillet chastre gibert golestanian scruggs gangnon flabouris borlase kenney forse benotti intrahospital ejaz spolverato pawlik keeven joos heest widder regenbogen studdert lipsitz zinner breakdowns ricketts moye halvorson dnac slideshare dnac gittell brandeis gittell gittell spreitzer gittell mullner consum ruca ruralhealth ruca nikolaev castner ingraham godat barosso resdac 20in 20the 20outpatient 20fees 2demergency 20room kamada undirected messinger spirakis tuan exper casalino pesko nyweide iwashyna mcglone eubank csardi nepusz igraph cami ghaferi birkmeyer dimick oslock ingraham rushing baselice ccsr elixhauser sundararajan halfon burnand luthi hcaup toolssoftware ccs10 ccs10 geva mandl dugoff huntley rosengart carley e2123389 baimas schiffern paton barbat sizing lauerman bruns tesoriero vincenzo ogola crandall alagoz saucke ingraham duren burant michailidis karnovsky metscape rohe regularized neur','Abstract Background: Access to emergency surgical care has declined as the rural workforce has decreased. Interhospital transfers of patients are increasingly necessary, and care coordination across settings is critical to quality care. We characterize the role of repeated hospital patient-sharing in outcomes of transfers for emergency general surgery (EGS) patients. Methods: A multicenter study of Wisconsin inpatient acute care hospital stays that involved transfer of emergency general surgery patients using data from the Wisconsin Hospital Association (WHA), a statewide hospital discharge census for 2016-2018. We hypothesized that higher proportion of patients transferred between hospitals would result in better outcomes. We examined the association between the proportion of EGS patients transferred between hospitals and patient outcomes, including in-hospital morbidity, mortality, and length of stay. Additional variables included hospital organizational characteristics and patient sociodemographic and clinical characteristics. Results: One hundred eighteen hospitals transferred 3,197 emergency general surgery patients over the 2-year study period; 1131 experienced in-hospital morbidity, mortality, or extended length of stay (>75 th percentile). Patients were 62 years old on average, 50% female, and 5% non-white. In the mixed effects model, hospitals’ proportion of patients shared was associated with lower odds of an in-hospital complication in a mixed effects model; specifically, when the proportion of patients shared between two hospitals doubles, the relative odds of any outcome changes by 0.85. Conclusions: Our results suggest the importance of emergent relationships between hospital dyads that share patients in quality outcomes. Transfer protocols should account for established efficiencies, familiarity, and coordination between hospitals. Level of Evidence: Level III, Prognostic/Epidemiological Keywords: Interhospital transfer, emergency general surgery, relational coordination, quality of care, network analysis BACKGROUND Interhospital transfers of patients involve the complex and rapid coordination of clinical processes at two different hospitals. Most commonly, transfers occur because a patient’s needs fall outside the scope of a hospital’s level of care, requiring specialty surgical, subspecialty medical, or intensive care services 1 . Less commonly, patient preference or capacity constraints dictate the decision to transfer 2 . Both formal (transfer of medical imaging) and informal (phone calls between providers) coordinating mechanisms facilitate moving a patient from one hospital to another 3 , 4 . Much in the way that relationships between people can be strengthened through repeated interaction, hospital connections can be established and reinforced through sharing and transferring patients 5 . The frequency of these connections could serve an important role in reinforcing formal and informal coordination mechanisms, both of which could be critical determinants of patient outcomes, though this has not been specifically explored. Emergency general surgery (EGS) conditions, such as diverticulitis and appendicitis, are common and costly, and up to 13% result in interhospital transfer 6 – 8 . We chose to study EGS because these conditions have no well-defined transfer guidelines and represent a large mix of urgent medical and surgical problems requiring rapid, complex information-sharing during an acute care episode. Over 3 million patients are admitted to United States hospitals for non-trauma EGS conditions annually, 7 and access to EGS care has declined as the surgical workforce has decreased in rural areas. As a result, interhospital transfers of EGS patients are necessary, and the coordination of care across settings is a critical component of ensuring quality care for these patients. Transferred EGS patients experience more morbidity and mortality than directly admitted patients 9 – 16 . Moreover, transferred EGS patients experience higher rates of complications (56 vs 38%; p<0.001) and readmissions (13 vs 10%; p<0.001) 17 – 19 . Among communication breakdowns resulting in harm to surgical patients, 43% occurred during handoffs and 39% during transfer of physical location 20 . Wisconsin (WI) provides an ideal environment to study transfer coordination because its rurality and limited number of tertiary care centers necessitate transfers for specialty care. Between 2006 and 2011, the state of WI lost 6.5% of its general surgeon workforce; three counties lost all general surgeons, and 12 counties had no general surgeons in either 2006 or 2011 21 . This phenomenon has led providers to call for a formal system of EGS transfers which has not yet been established 11 . Using network analysis, we use data from the Wisconsin Hospital Association to map the current interhospital EGS transfer system in WI and characterize the association between the proportion of patients shared and patient outcomes 22 . We then examine the association between the proportion of patients shared (i.e., the relative number of EGS patients shared by two institutions) and patient outcomes, including in-hospital morbidity, in-hospital mortality, and length of stay. METHODS Conceptual Framework We study the interhospital transfer of EGS patients using the Relational Coordination Framework in which the coordination of transfers involves communication, relationships, and information sharing. Interactions between medical staff are reinforced by communication that is frequent, timely, accurate, and problem-solving 23 . Over time, the coordination of work can improve quality and efficiency by strengthening inter-organizational coordination networks, a process termed relational coordination 24 . To inform the development of transfer protocols to improve care for EGS patients, we characterize the association between transfers and outcomes among EGS patients using a network analytical approach. We conceptualize transfers in terms of the shared work and care coordination required by multiple health care providers and staff from different hospitals. We therefore focus our analysis on the strength of relationships between hospitals transferring EGS patients and its association with clinical outcomes 25 . Given that transfer relationships may reinforce care coordination over time, we hypothesize a positive correlation between the frequency of sharing patients and better clinical outcomes 26 . Data Source Our primary data source is the Wisconsin Hospital Association (WHA) Discharge Database for the time period Quarter 1 2016 through Quarter 2 2018. Pursuant to state statute, all WI hospitals (n=187) must submit information quarterly for all inpatient and outpatient encounters, including ED visits, providing a census of hospital discharges in the state. A partnership with the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality Healthcare Cost and Utilization Project yielded a validated algorithm to identify unique patients across facilities. Additional Data We linked WHA data to the American Hospital Association (AHA) data, which contains facility-reported information on hospital ownership, staffing, facilities, and capabilities 27 . The publicly available University of Washington Rural Health Research Center’s zip code-based Rural-Urban Commuting Area approximation (RUCA) was used to characterize each hospital as either rural or urban 28 . The hospital’s safety net status is measured with the disproportionate share hospital (DSH) allotment (a measure of hospitals’ uncompensated care) from the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services 29 . Study Population EGS patients 18 years and older were identified using the American Association for the Surgery of Trauma (AAST) Committee on Severity Assessment and Patient Outcomes consensus definitions 30 with a qualifying International Classification of Diseases (ICD-10-CM) diagnosis code as a primary diagnosis during an emergency department (ED) or acute care hospital encounter in a non-federal institution were included 30 . All patients in the cohort resided in WI at the time of their qualifying encounter. We define a transfer as a patient moving from the ED or inpatient unit at one acute care facility to the ED or inpatient unit at a different acute care facility based on discharge and admitting source codes for two separate facility records indicating that services were provided within one day of each other for the same unique patient. The transfer chain was required to have an EGS diagnosis in at least one of the two segments. The transfer chain was also required to have either a segment that included an ED visit or a segment that was characterized as urgent/emergent to ensure the emergent nature of the transfer. An ED-inclusive segment was defined as an encounter that includes revenue codes of “0450”, “0450A”, “0451”, “0452”, “0456”, “0459” (Emergency room), or “0981” (Professional fees-Emergency room) 31 . Admission type was emergency or urgent. Network Construction and Primary Explanatory Variable Using the WHA data, we mapped the network of interhospital transfers of EGS patients in WI. We transformed the list of patient transfers from WHA data into an affiliation matrix in which each row represents the originating hospital and each column represents the receiving hospital. This matrix forms the basis for the network analysis. The affiliation matrix is represented graphically to determine the arrangement of hospitals’ positions in the network using social rather than geographic distance 32 , 33 . Only hospitals with at least 10 patient transfers annually were included in quantitative analysis to ensure adequate connectivity among network components 34 , 35 . Network construction was performed in R using the igraph software package 36 . From the network, we calculated an institution-level network measure of proportion of patients shared, which served as our primary explanatory variable. Proportion of patients shared was defined as the proportion of patients shared divided by the total number of patients transferred in or out of both hospitals 37 . Primary Outcomes We created a composite outcome measure that includes in-hospital mortality, complications, and/or prolonged length of stay at the receiving hospital. We define in-hospital mortality using the WHA-provided date of death indicator. We defined complications using both primary and secondary diagnosis and procedure codes for in-hospital morbidity ICD-10-CM diagnosis and procedure codes for sepsis, septic shock, pulmonary embolism, myocardial infarction, cardiac arrest, renal failure, adult respiratory distress syndrome, and pneumonia 38 , 39 . For operative complications, patients were required to have an EGS procedure performed. For systemic complications, there was no requirement of a procedure having been performed in either segment. Prolonged length of stay was defined as a stay longer than the 75 th percentile for a given Clinical Classifications Software (CCS) category for the time from presentation at final hospital to discharge from the final hospital. CCS categories are clinically meaningful categories comprising a subset of ICD-10-CM procedure codes 40 . Predictor Variables Sociodemographic variables that influence EGS outcomes, including age, race, and payer status, were included. Using a 6-month period prior to a patient’s first visit, we adjusted for the number of comorbidities using diagnosis codes (ICD-10-CM) following the Charlson-Deyo algorithm 41 , 42 . Surgical procedures were determined by ICD-10-PCS, CPT, and Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System codes. These procedures were further categorized according to CCS Services and Procedure Categories and included in the final models to control for operative intervention 43 . Hospital facility characteristics included hospital bed size and an indicator of whether the hospital received DSH payments. We categorized the bed size variable using tertiles derived from the AHA data: 1-24 beds for small, 25-71 beds for medium, and > 71 for large. Analysis We summarized descriptive statistics for all variables and assessed bivariate associations using Chi-square tests for categorical variables and t-tests for continuous variables. We hypothesized that patients receiving care at hospitals with a higher proportion of patients shared would have better outcomes (lower in-hospital mortality, in-hospital morbidity, and total length of stay) relative to patients in hospitals with lower strength of tie measures. We assessed this with a mixed effects model with strength of tie as the primary explanatory variable. Based on the skewness of this measure, we include the log of the proportion of patients shared using base 2. Models controlled for patient severity and sociodemographic characteristics, hospital organizational factors, and included a hospital dyad-specific random intercept. To explore this association further, we evaluated the predicted probability of the composite outcome relative to strength of tie using a generalized linear regression (without the dyad-level random effect). This study was designated exempt by the Committee for the Protection of Human Subjects at our institution. Our study complies with STROBE guidelines for the reporting of cross-sectional studies. RESULTS Descriptive Statistics The EGS transfer network for 118 hospitals in WI is depicted in Figure 1 . The overall strength of tie in the statewide network was 0.07. The transfer map yielded six distinct subnetworks that approximate high volume hubs with a majority of between-subnetwork transfers occurring based on geographic proximity. The median strength of tie for each subnetwork is displayed on the map. The majority of the subnetworks had a median proportion of patients shared between 11%and 16%, with the exception of the subnetwork centered in Madison, WI, which had a median proportion of patients shared of 5%. Given its central location and the availability of tertiary care services, Madison has a greater role as a secondary transfer partner for all other state subnetworks. Figure 1. Open in a new tab Proportion of Patients Shared and Transfers of Patients with EGS Conditions for Hospital Networks in Wisconsin 2016-2018 Note: Proportion of patients shared is computed as the proportion of patients shared between two hospitals divided by the total number of patients transferred in or out of both hospitals. The median is displayed for the state overall and for each of the six identified subnetworks: North Middle (NM), Northeast (NE), Southeast (SE), South Middle (SM), Southwest (SW), Northwest (NW); EGS=Emergency General Surgery Descriptive statistics for hospital (n=118) characteristics are shown in Table 1 . For the 118 hospitals in the data, the majority were in an urban area (61.9%), 45.8% were designated critical access hospitals, and 77.1% received DSH payments. Most (43.2%) transfers involved medium sized hospitals. The mean strength of tie for hospital dyads was 0.10 (sd=0.08). Table 1. Wisconsin Hospital Characteristics (n=118) Hospital Characteristics Summary Statistic (N=118) Rural, n (%) 45 (38.1%) Critical Access, n (%) 54 (45.8%) Non-profit (vs. government or for-profit), n (%) 113 (95.8) Bed Size: Large (>71), n (%) 40 (33.9%) Bed Size: Medium (25-71), n (%) 51 (43.2%) Bed Size: Small (1-24), n (%) 27 (22.9%) Receives DSH payment, n (%) 91 (77.1%) Median Proportion of Patients Shared (IQR) 0.08 (0.03-0.19) Open in a new tab Note: Bed size is defined by the dyad Patient (n=3197) characteristics are shown in Table 2 ; 1131 (35%) patients experienced at least one outcome, including mortality (122; 4%), an operative or systemic complication (794; 25%), or prolonged length of stay (592; 19%). Twenty individuals (1%) experienced all three outcomes, and 337 (11%) experienced two. The transferred EGS patients in our cohort were, on average, 62 years old, 50% male, 95% white, and had Medicare as a primary payer (56%). They had 2 comorbid conditions on average, and 29% had surgery at one or both hospitals. On average, transferred patients who experienced at least one outcome were older (66 vs. 59 years old; p<0.001), male (54.1 vs. 48.4%; p=0.002), publicly insured (80.9 vs. 67.4%; p<0.001), and had more comorbid conditions (2.81 vs. 1.61; p<0.001). Table 2. EGS Patient Characteristics for Patients with and without Adverse Events (n=3,197) Patient Characteristics Overall (N=3197) With Adverse Event (N=1131) Without Adverse Event (N=2066) P Any adverse event, n (%) 1131 (35.4) Mortality, n (%) 122 (3.8) Complication, n (%) 794 (24.8) LOS >75 th percentile, n (%) 592 (18.5) Age, M (SD) 61.6 (18.3) 66.31 (16.7) 59.0 (18.6) <0.001 Female, n (%) 1585 (49.6) 519 (45.9) 1066 (51.6) 0.002 Male, n (%) 612 (54.1) 1000 (48.4) Non-White, n (%) 156 (4.9) 43 (3.8) 113 (5.5) 0.042 White, n (%) 1083 (96.2) 1933 (94.5) Private Payer, n (%) 890 (27.8) 216 (19.1) 674 (32.6) <0.001 Government Payer, n (%) 915 (80.9) 1392 (67.4) Comorbidities, M (SD) 2.04 (2.69) 2.81 (3.01) 1.61 (2.40) <0.001 Surgery at one or both hospitals, n (%) 936 (29.3) 343 (30.3) 593 (28.7) 0.355 No surgery, n (%) 2261 (70.7) 788 (69.7) 1473 (71.3) Open in a new tab Note: LOS=Length of stay Mixed Effects Model The results of the mixed effects model with a random effect for hospital dyad are summarized in Table 3 . Male patients, patients with greater comorbidity, and white patients were more likely to experience any outcome event after transfer. In addition, having a surgical procedure during either hospital stay (first or second segment) was positively associated with the composite outcome, likely owed to the additional risk of wound- and anesthesia-related complications. Patients with private insurance had 0.71 odds of an outcome event relative to patients with public insurance or self-payment. Organizational variables, including the originating hospital’s bed size and DSH payments, were not associated with the outcome. Table 3. Mixed Effects Model Variable Odds Ratio [95% CI] P Age 1.10 [1.07, 1.13] <0.001 Male 1.23 [1.03, 1.48] 0.021 White 1.56 [0.93, 2.64] 0.094 Private Payer 0.71 [0.56, 0.90] 0.004 Comorbidities 1.14 [1.10, 1.17] <0.001 Has Surgery 1.38 [1.13, 1.68] 0.001 Bed Size: Med 1.08 [0.84, 1.40] 0.553 Bed Size: Small 1.07 [0.77, 1.48] 0.693 DSH Payment 0.98 [0.77, 1.25] 0.857 Log Proportion of Patients Shared 0.85 [0.78, 0.92] <0.001 Intercept 0.04 [0.02, 0.08] <0.001 Open in a new tab Reference Categories: Female, Non-White Race, Public Payer, No Surgery, Bed Size: Large, No DSH Payment Greater proportion of patients shared, the parameter of interest, was associated with lower odds of the outcome; specifically, doubling the proportion of patients shared between two hospitals, or more EGS patients transferred, resulted in a 0.85 relative odds of an outcome event. The plot of predicted probabilities from the generalized model across values of proportion of patients shared are shown in Figure 2 . As the proportion of shared patients increased between two hospitals, the predicted likelihood of an event decreased from 0.43 to 0.27. Figure 2. Open in a new tab Predicted Probability of Adverse Event by Proportion of Patients Shared based on Generalized Linear Model without Random Effects DISCUSSION Overall, our analysis indicates that a greater number of patients transferred between two hospitals confers a lower odds of an in-hospital outcome event. This suggests the importance of emergent relationships between hospital dyads that share patients in quality outcomes 44 . Receipt of DSH payments and originating hospital bed size were not associated with in-hospital mortality or morbidity, indicating that any delay in care resulting from presenting at a low resourced hospital and then being transferred did not result in worse outcomes. This is unsurprising given that transferring hospitals almost always transfer to hospitals with more services and greater resources. Extant network research has largely focused on the centrality of interhospital transfer networks 45 . These studies consistently find that hospitals transfer patients to higher resourced, larger hospitals with more services 46 – 49 . However, transferring hospitals do not always send patients to the hospital with the best outcomes for their condition. Researchers have identified this as an opportunity to improve transfer outcomes by routing patients to hospitals with better outcomes 46 , 50 , 51 . However, our results suggest that the strength of existing transfer relationships also confers significant advantages to patient outcomes. In other words, altering transfer networks to improve outcomes is not as simple as rerouting patients to different hospitals; this approach would likely result in at least a short-term increase in adverse events and could have implications for access to high quality surgical care in rural communities. Rather, transfer protocols must also take into account the efficiencies, familiarity, and coordination between hospitals that have long-standing transfer relationships. The results of this study must be interpreted in the context of qualitative studies of transfers in Wisconsin using the relational coordination framework. Although the results of this analysis suggest that interactions between medical staff to coordinate transfers are reinforced by communication that is frequent, timely, accurate, and problem-solving, qualitative analysis sheds light on the nuance of these interactions and how, over time, the coordination of work can improve quality and efficiency by strengthening inter-organizational coordination networks 23 . The results of qualitative interviews with nursing staff who coordinate transfers demonstrated that professional and protocoled transfer interactions contributed to the (1) appropriateness and completeness of clinical information, (2) efficiency of communication, and (3) degree of consensus about whether to transfer 52 . In addition, these relationships helped to correct common communication bottlenecks including the accepting hospital’s understanding of the clinical capacity of the referring hospital and the facilitation of imaging transfer between electronic health records. In contrast, staff from referring hospitals who are unfamiliar with established procedure often attempt to bypass transfer protocols at the receiving institution, delaying information and decision making. Taken together, this research suggests that interhospital transfers are more dynamic and relational processes than previous research suggests. Limitations Our study results must be interpreted in the context of their limitations. We do not have longer term outcomes data, and the impact of transfer on 30-day and long-term outcomes is therefore unknown. Moreover, we were unable to include commonly studied measures of market concentration because of the rurality in our state. Therefore, our results are not generalizable to other states. In addition, we used only EGS transfers for our network construction. As a result, we likely underestimate the proportion of patients shared between hospitals that transfer patients for other conditions. Our relatively small sample size meant we needed to specify a parsimonious model and use a composite outcome despite using multiple years of data. Given the lack of variability in the proportion of patients shared measure and geographical constraints of transferring patients, it is unclear how well traditional network analysis can inform the improvement of outcomes for transferred patients for selected clinical indications. Although we included both organizational and patient variables that account for higher acuity transfers, we must acknowledge the possibility that we omitted essential variables. Therefore, there remains the small possibility that, if a small rural hospital transfers all of its EGS patients to a tertiary care center, while a medium sized rural hospital treats some locally and transfers the sicker ones, the outcome of those transferred from the second hospital will be worse than those from the first; this will be due to the higher acuity and not to any relationship. Although we did not have data to examine the role of shared information systems in facilitating transfer, future studies on this topic should examine the role of shared electronic health record systems. We completed our analysis with an eye toward informing interventions to improve interhospital transfers in WI, and the network analysis was difficult to implement with this goal in mind. Future studies may benefit from leveraging recently developed methods for inference on sparse networks 53 , 54 . CONCLUSIONS Overall, the number of patients transferred between hospitals and the emergent relationships that result matter for the care of patients and should be considered in any redesign of transfer systems. As COVID surges significantly alter the landscape, ebbs, and flows of interhospital transfers, the urgency for improved transfer systems is growing. Better protocols are essential to accommodating changes in local capacity and staffing. Our work suggests that coordination between facilities seems to matter more than individual organizational characteristics in quality outcomes of transferred EGS patients. However, the difficulty of studying these networks in policy- and clinically-relevant local contexts compromises the translation of results into redesign. The results of this work and the associated qualitative studies will be used to inform an intervention to improve the transfers of EGS patients in WI. Our research suggests the intervention could focus on standardizing clinical information required for handoffs to ensure timely and accurate communication for transfer decision-making. We anticipate that eventually there will be an evidence-based handoff template and universal transfer protocol that preserves the effective components of existing transfer relationships while facilitating better outcomes broadly. ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS We gratefully acknowledge Diana Gutierrez-Meza, MPH for her help with references. CONFLICTS OF INTEREST AND SOURCES OF FUNDING This project was supported by the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality K08144-AAD5359-539714-4. The project described was also supported by the Clinical and Translational Science Award (CTSA) program, through the NIH National Center for Advancing Translational Sciences (NCATS), grant UL1TR002373. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the NIH. Footnotes The authors have no conflicts of interest to disclose. REFERENCES 1. Fan RL, Zhao M, Peng DXS. Differentiating Interhospital Transfer Types: Varied Impacts and Diverging Coordination Strategies. Prod Oper Manag. 2021;30(10):3657–3678. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Bruenderman EH, Block SB, Kehdy FJ, Benns MV, Miller KR, Motameni A, et al. An evaluation of emergency general surgery transfers and a call for standardization of practices. Surgery. 2021;169(3):567–572. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. McCrum ML, Davis KA, Kaafarani HM, Santry HP, Shafi S, Crandall ML. Current opinion on emergency general surgery transfer and triage criteria. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2020;89(3):e71–e77. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Teng CY, Ingraham AM, Zuckerbraun BS. Emergency General Surgery-To Regionalize, or Not to Regionalize, That Is the Question. JAMA Surg. 2020;155(9):849–850. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Brunson JC, Laubenbacher RC. Applications of network analysis to routinely collected health care data: a systematic review. J Am Med Inform Assoc. 2017. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Ogola GO, Gale SC, Haider A, Shafi S. The financial burden of emergency general surgery: National estimates 2010 to 2060. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2015;79(3):444–448. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Gale SC, Shafi S, Dombrovskiy VY, Arumugam D, Crystal JS. The public health burden of emergency general surgery in the United States: A