2019;76(1):115–128. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. DuGoff EH, Fernandes-Taylor S, Weissman GE, Huntley JH, Pollack CE. A scoping review of patient-sharing network studies using administrative data. Transl Behav Med. 2018;8(4):598–625. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Teng CY, Davis BS, Rosengart MR, Carley KM, Kahn JM. Assessment of Hospital Characteristics and Interhospital Transfer Patterns of Adults With Emergency General Surgery Conditions. JAMA Netw Open. 2021;4(9):e2123389. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Baimas-George M, Schiffern L, Yang H, Paton L, Barbat S, Matthews B, et al. Emergency general surgery transfer to lower acuity facility: The role of right-sizing care in emergency general surgery regionalization. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2022;92(1):38–43. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Lauerman MH, Herrera AV, Albrecht JS, Chen HH, Bruns BR, Tesoriero RB, et al. Interhospital Transfers with Wide Variability in Emergency General Surgery. Am Surg. 2019;85(6):595–600. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Di Vincenzo F. Exploring the networking behaviors of hospital organizations. BMC Health Serv Res. 2018;18(1):334. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 50. Ogola GO, Crandall ML, Richter KM, Shafi S. High-volume hospitals are associated with lower mortality among high-risk emergency general surgery patients. J Trauma Acute Care Surg. 2018;85(3):560–565. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 51. Davis KA. Providing High-Quality Care for Emergency General Surgery Patients. J Am Coll Surg. 2022;234(2):225–226. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 52. Alagoz E, Saucke M, Arroyo N, Fernandez Taylor S, Ingraham A. Communication During Interhospital Transfers of Emergency General Surgery Patients. J Patient Saf. 2022. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 53. Basu S, Duren W, Evans CR, Burant CF, Michailid