INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1992,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8278360','The association between self-declared acute care surgery services and critical care resources: Results from a national survey.','Tameron AM, Ricci KB, Oslock WM, Rushing AP, Ingraham AM, Daniel VT, Paredes AZ, Diaz A, Collins CE, Heh VK, Baselice HE, Strassels SA, Santry HP.','2020','viscus uniting cmos cohorted cohorted sicu deteriorate cohorted cohorted investor cohorted rozycki regionalized catastrophes strangulated expeditious gajic regionalized idealize originators laudable nihms1705349 supplementary_material catarina kiefe r01hs022694 hps heena santry heh santry lerro sariego feurer riskin brundage dabrowski gosnell weireter havens peetz hoth brenneman cothren hoyt endorf jurkovich velmahos jurkovich ingraham ayturk kiefe khubchandani ingraham ahadataviewer 20instruments aha_annual_survey_fy11 mayr yende dombrovskiy havens olufajo kassin pastores rozycki s405 seabury bognar aparicio kasner e001877 mada salowich felland s25 rivara jurkovich celso tepas langland orban thakkar glickman weygandt kamdar e27 battistella bukowiec nontrauma prabhakaran farshid allada chandrasekhar lecky mcclelland bypassing e016355 gajic afessa masud tyc methodist debakey leapfrog nihms1705349 supplementary_material','Abstract Purpose: We examined differences in critical care structures and processes between hospitals with Acute Care Surgery (ACS) versus general surgeon on call (GSOC) models for emergency general surgery (EGS) care. Methods: 2,811 EGS-capable hospitals were surveyed to examine structures and processes including critical care domains and ACS implementation. Differences between ACS and GSOC hospitals were compared using appropriate tests of association and logistic regression models. Results: 272/1,497 hospitals eligible for analysis (18.2%) reported they use an ACS model. EGS patients at ACS hospitals were more likely to be admitted to a combined trauma/surgical ICU or a dedicated surgical ICU. GSOC hospitals had lower adjusted odds of having 24-hour ICU coverage, in-house intensivists or respiratory therapists, and 4/6 critical-care protocols. Conclusions: Critical care delivery is a key component of EGS care. While harnessing of critical care structures and processes varies across hospitals that have implemented ACS, overall ACS models of care appear to have more robust critical care practices. Keywords: Acute Care Surgery, Surgical Critical Care, Emergency Surgery, ICU best practices Introduction: Acute Care Surgery (ACS) was proposed as a new specialty by the American Association for the Surgery of Trauma in 2005.[ 1 ] This new care model was, in part, designed to address a shortage in emergency general surgery (EGS) coverage nationally. EGS patients with diseases as diverse as appendicitis, cholecystitis, perforated viscus and soft-tissue infections had traditionally been cared for by a local general surgeon on call (GSOC). However, a workforce crisis had resulted in 37% of emergency rooms nationally lacking a GSOC.[ 2 , 3 ] Like trauma patients, EGS patients require surgical care at all hours and many have a high risk of systemic critical illness.[ 4 – 10 ] Therefore, uniting the preoperative, intraoperative, and postoperative care of both EGS and trauma patients under a specialty with added certification in surgical critical care and existing mandate for 24/7 availability was a pragmatic solution which simultaneously increased the operative volume of trauma surgeons.[ 11 – 14 ] In the absence of guidelines for ACS programs, more and more hospitals have been adopting ACS with variable implementation of structures and processes for the care of EGS patients compared to hospitals with GSOC models of care.[ 15 – 19 ] Importantly, there is no consensus on what critical care resources should be present at hospitals that implement ACS. In light of these variations, and the substantial risk of critical illness among EGS patients, we sought to describe features of critical care delivery between hospitals with a self-declared ACS model and those with a traditional GSOC model. For example, the presence or absence of an ACS model might be associated with whether the critically ill EGS patient is managed by a primary surgeon without critical care board certification versus a 24-hour in-house board-certified critical care physician or cared for in a mixed Medical/Surgical ICU versus a Surgical/Trauma ICU. We hypothesized that ACS models would be associated with more robust critical care structures and processes. Materials and Methods: We conducted a national survey of acute care general hospitals in the US to identify hospital-level EGS structures and processes in a number of domains. In this manuscript we specifically focus on structures and processes targeting critically ill patients. Our dependent variable of interest is a self-declared ACS model as a dedicated team for the care of EGS patients. While the original proponents of ACS models intended to integrate trauma, critical care, and EGS, for the analyses that follows, ACS is any dedicated team for EGS patients with or without trauma, elective general surgery, or burn patients. Data Source. The methodology of the survey reported in this study has been previously described.[[ 15 , 16 ]] Briefly, first we queried the American Hospital Association (AHA) Annual Survey of Hospitals database for hospitals that care for adult patients (≥ 18 years old) with 24 hour emergency room (ER) access and ≥ 1 operating room (OR).[ 17 ] The ability for EGS patients to receive care at these hospitals was confirmed by direct review of hospital websites and/or contact with hospitals regarding general surgery availability resulting in a final survey sample of 2,811 acute care hospitals that met the inclusion criteria. A hybrid mail and electronic survey was sent to respondents at these hospitals in two waves between August and December 2015. The first wave was sent to senior surgeons who would be familiar with EGS coverage at their hospital based on review of hospital and practice websites. The second wave was sent to similarly knowledgeable surgeons if there was no response after the first round of the survey, or Chief Medical Officers at locations with a single surgeon only who did not respond in the first round. At the completion of both rounds of the survey the overall response rate was 60.1%. More than 95% of the hospitals (n = 1,610) in our sample had data from surgeons while 4.7% (n = 80) had data from CMOs. The 68-item survey questionnaire ( Appendix 1 ) queried the hospital’s critical care structures and processes including where the patient was cared for, physician and staff availability, and protocols to guide management of deteriorating or critically ill patients. Also included was a question regarding the hospital’s overall approach to EGS care (“my hospital’s overall approach to EGS is [best described as]: (1) A dedicated clinical team whose scope encompasses EGS (±trauma, ±elective general surgery, and ± burns), (2) a traditional approach with an ad hoc general surgeon on call schedule, or (3) other”). For the purposes of the following analyses, #1 is labeled ACS, #2 is labeled GSOC, and #3 is excluded. The rationale for excluding #3 was to ensure that we were truly comparing the traditional model of ad hoc on call surgeons versus using a dedicated team-based approach. Including hospitals with “other” models of care would have biased our results in a direction that we would not be otherwise able to ascertain. Statistical Analysis. Associations between critical care structures and processes and model of care were measured. Categorical variables between ACS and GSOC hospitals were analyzed using X 2 tests of association. Normally distributed continuous variables were analyzed using student t-tests. Non-normally distributed variables were analyzed using Wilcoxon rank-sum tests. Unadjusted and adjusted logistic regression models were constructed to measure the odds of the critical care structure and process variables being in place based on self-declared ACS model vs. GSOC model. Adjusted models included hospital ownership, location, in-patient bed capacity, teaching status, medical school affiliation and trauma center designation as reported to the AHA Annual Survey. Hospitals with missing values due to lack of response were excluded from these multivariable analyses. To determine analytical appropriateness, sample size and standard model fit statistics were used. Tolerance statistics were used to assess collinearity between all hospital-level characteristics including self-reported model of EGS coverage. Tolerance statistics (all >0.2) were acceptable across all variables thus all were kept in the models. This study was deemed exempt by the University of Massachusetts Medical School institutional review board. Survey data were stored in a REDCap database and analyzed using SAS 9.4 (SAS, Cary, NC). Results: Among the 1,690 hospitals represented in the completed surveys (60.1% overall response rate), 94 did not respond to the question describing EGS model of care and 99 responded “other” leaving 1,497 hospitals for analysis (53.3% of our original sample of EGS capable hospitals). 272/1497 (18.2%) reported they had implemented an ACS model of care. Table 1 shows the frequency of critical care structure and process variables between hospitals based on the model of EGS care in place at the hospital. Likelihood of 24-hour critical care coverage in the ICU was significantly higher in ACS hospitals compared to GSOC hospitals. Among ACS hospitals, this coverage was more likely to be provided by an in-house critical care specialist compared to GSOC hospitals where on-call critical care specialists were more often utilized. Tele-ICU services were uncommon in both groups (4–11%) but were more common among GSOC hospitals. Admission to a mixed medical-surgical ICU was the most common option in both groups, but this was less frequent among ACS hospitals (35% ACS versus 69% GSOC) because 32% of ACS hospitals cohorted critically ill EGS patients in a combined trauma/surgical ICU (versus 3% GSOC) while 22% cohorted them in a dedicated surgical ICU (versus 6% GSOC). The majority of hospitals did not report the percentage of EGS surgeons with subspecialty certification in Surgical Critical Care or with clinical roles providing critical care coverage. However, respondents from ACS hospitals were more likely to report if their surgeons were board certified/eligible in surgical critical care or provided critical care coverage. Critical care for EGS patients was more likely to be provided by a surgical critical care intensivist at ACS hospitals than at GSOC hospitals where fewer than 10% of hospitals reported presence of surgical intensivists. While the majority of both types of hospitals had 24-hour respiratory therapists available, this was also more likely in ACS hospitals compared to GSOC hospitals. In addition, every critical care process we queried (i.e., specific protocols and guidelines) was more likely to be in place at ACS hospitals vs. GSOC hospitals. The least frequent critical care process overall was a protocol to identify EGS patients in need of critical care post-operatively where presence ranged from 21–31%. Notably, there were many structures and processes where our respondents did not answer a specific critical care query in the survey as shown in the table. Critical care training and roles of surgeons were most likely to not be reported followed by critical care processes to varying degrees. Among all of these areas of critical care resources, respondents based at GSOC hospitals were more likely than those at ACS hospitals to not provide an answer. Table 1. Differences in Critical Care Structures and Processes at Hospitals with Acute Care Surgery (ACS) Models Compared to General Surgeon on Call (GSOC) Models for Emergency General Surgery Coverage (N= 1497) 