f a surgical intensivist among critical care providers (0.51 [0.34–0.77]) and having a protocol to urgently mobilize blood products (0.34 [0.25–0.48]), yet had higher odds of having a respiratory therapist present 24/7 (1.81 [1.20–2.72]). <200 bed hospitals compared to ≥ 500 bed hospitals predicted higher odds that EGS patients were cared for in an open ICU (3.14 [1.77 – 5.58]) and lower odds of 6 critical structures and processes: 24-hour critical care coverage (0.13 [0.05–0.35]), 24-hour in house intensivist (0.16 [0.08–0.30]), EGS patients cared for in surgical ICU (0.10 [0.05–0.23]), surgical intensivist (0.15 [0.08–0.28]), and protocols for responding to patient deterioration (0.26 [0.08–0.85] and 0.37 [0.16–0.89]). Compared to non-governmental hospitals, governmental hospitals had lower odds of 24-hour critical care coverage (0.60 [0.41–0.86]), protocol to ensure surviving sepsis guidelines (0.45 [0.30–0.69]), and graded response strategy to identify patients at risk for clinical deterioration (0.49 [0.33–0.73]). Compared to urban hospitals, rural hospitals had higher odds of an open ICU model (1.47 [1.06–2.05]) and lower odds of 24-hour critical care coverage (0.16 [0.10–0.26]), 24-hour presence of respiratory therapists (0.20 [0.13–0.310]), and the following protocols in place: adherence to surviving sepsis guidelines (0.57 [0.35–0.90]), identifying deteriorating patients (0.27 [0.18–0.40]), escalating care for deteriorating patients (0.65 [0.44–0.99]), and mobilizing blood products urgently (0.58 [0.39–0.86]). Hospitals without major teaching status had lower odds of having 24-hour critical care coverage (minor: 0.21 [0.06–0.74] non-teaching: 0.15 [0.04–0.56]) as well as having a surgical intensivist (minor: 0.25 [0.15–0.44] non-teaching: 0.24 [0.10–0.56]) compared to those with major teaching. Hospitals lacking medical school affiliation had lower odds of having EGS patients cohorted in surgical ICUs (0.49 [0.24–0.99]) as well as having a surgical intensivist among the critical care providers for EGS patients (0.48 [0.27–0.88]). Figure 1: Open in a new tab Adjusted and Unadjusted Odds Ratio (95% Confidence Intervals) of Having a Specific Critical Care Structure and Process in Place at Hospitals with General Surgeon on Call Model vs. Hospitals with Self-Reported Implementation of an Acute Care Surgery Model Discussion: In this national assessment of critical care resources available to EGS patients, we evaluated how processes and structures differed based on whether or not a hospital has implemented the ACS model of care first proposed in 2006 but still lacking in specific guidelines for implementation or any larger certifying body. We demonstrated that there is no single critical care structure or process that is uniformly adopted by self-declared ACS hospitals. However, when compared to GSOC hospitals in aggregate, ACS hospitals had more robust critical care structures and processes in place that would presumably impart benefit to EGS patients experiencing hemodynamically instability, airway compromise, respiratory distress, or other manifestations of critical illness. EGS patients are at high risk for systemic critical illness. Previous research suggests that 13–50% of all EGS patients require intensive care and 12–14% have septic shock.[ 4 – 10 ] Among all patients in the US requiring intensive care for sepsis, approximately 16% are septic due to EGS disease.[ 18 ] Overall, as many as 1.2 million Americans will be admitted annually with EGS-attributable critical illness.[ 19 ] Critically ill EGS patients are at higher risk than those presenting without systemic illness. They experience longer lengths of stay (LOS) (25 vs 14 days), more complications (13 vs 4.5%), higher mortality (41 vs 7.7%), and more readmissions than lower acuity EGS patients.[ 9 , 10 , 20 , 21 ] Availability of critical care beds in the US declined by 21% nationally from 2000 to 2010.[ 22 ] In a 2010 paper, Davis and Rozycki discussed the evolution of ACS as initially proposed in the context of EGS patients’ critical care needs.[ 23 ] They noted that due to the unscheduled nature of EGS disease presentation, round-the-clock critical care resources are required posing a challenge to resource constrained hospital systems.[ 23 ] We found that ACS hospitals, even when adjusting for other factors that might be associated with more robust critical care resources (e.g., simultaneous trauma center status or medical school affiliation) have higher odds of providing round the clock critical care resources. Thus, our findings suggest that ACS models were they to be implemented within a larger regionalized approach to EGS care present an opportunity to overcome local critical care restraints through defined processes for triage and transfer. When the same approach has been taken for patients with other unexpected need for emergency care due to traumatic injury, suspected myocardial infarction, and possible cerebrovascular accident, outcomes have improved.[ 24 – 34 ] A study from the Michigan Quality Collaborative with overall 30-day mortality of 4.1% reported that an ACS model of care, compared to a GSOC model, was associated with a 31% decrease in EGS mortality.[ 35 ] Our findings of disparate critical care structures and processes between ACS and GSOC hospitals suggest that lack of necessary critical care resources might explain this notable mortality difference seen in Michigan EGS patients. While most trained general surgeons would likely be confident performing an array of basic and complex EGS procedures, it appears that lack of robust critical care resources for peri-operative care should be an indication for transfer to more resourced hospitals for the 33–36% of EGS patients who present with intra-abdominal catastrophes (e.g., gastrointestinal perforation, strangulated hernia, ischemic bowel) and the 4–22% who present with necrotizing soft tissue infections as they are at high risk of developing profound systemic inflammation.[ 4 , 19 , 36 , 37 ] This is not unlike that which is required for the triage of early stroke victims to centers with TPA capability, ST-segment myocardial infarction patients to centers with cardiac catheterization labs, and head injured patients to centers with board-certified neurosurgeons.[ 38 – 40 ] For example, EGS patients identified to be at high risk of sepsis may experience mortality benefit with triage to ACS hospitals that assure Surviving Sepsis Guidelines and provide prompt initiation of antibiotics, adequate resuscitation and monitoring, and expeditious source control. Similarly, patients with life-threatening gastrointestinal hemorrhage not amenable to endoscopic or catheter-based treatment may experience mortality benefit with triage to ACS hospitals with massive transfusion protocols that expedite receipt of blood products in appropriate ratios to minimize the risks of progressing to the lethal triad of hypothermia, coagulopathy, and acidosis. Additional research is needed to examine the relationship between critical care structures and processes at the hospital-level and disease-specific outcomes at the patient-level in order to identify which ones truly improve outcomes. Intensivist staffing is a specific critical structure that has been the source of much debate with a majority of providers favoring intensivist led ICUs.[ 41 ] We identified significant variability in the availability of 24-hour surgical critical care coverage across all survey respondents. There are a number of studies which demonstrate intensivist staffed ICUs are associated with decreased length of stay and lower rates of complications and mortality.[ 42 , 43 ] Gajic et al demonstrated a reduction in ICU complications from 11% to 7% per patient day with onsite critical care specialists; mortality, however, was not affected. Similarly, closed model ICUs, meaning patients are admitted under full responsibility of a trained intensivist have been noted to have better outcomes and better quality of care in both medical and subspecialty ICUs and higher rates of formalized critical care processes such as daily sedation interruption and insulin treatment.[ 44 , 45 ] Notably many respondents, the vast majority of whom were surgeons responsible for EGS oversight at their hospital, did not comment on the level of critical care provided by surgeons. Given the high rates of systemic critical illness among EGS patients, the high engagement with surgeon involved in critical care strategic planning and implementation is likely critical to optimize quality. Moving forward, it will be important to examine the role of surgeons in ICU staffing given that the original proposals for ACS and current ACS training models require additional training and certification in critical care.[ 1 , 46 ] Our results also highlight substantial variability in the critical care resources EGS patients will have available to them not just between ACS and GSOC hospitals and care but also between ACS hospitals. The significant variability in the availability of 24-hour surgical critical care coverage, the commitment to a consistent ICU model, and adherence to several key critical care protocols even within self-declare ACS models is cause for concern because any attempt to assure access to high quality EGS care within a regionalized hub-and-spoke type system must have a set of agreed upon criteria that must be met to be considered an ‘ACS centers of excellence’ (i.e., a hub). Guidelines as have been established for trauma care are needed for ACS and would idealize the goals initially described by the originators of this novel model of care to optimize access to and quality of care for EGS patients.[ 47 ] The findings from our survey must be interpreted in the context of several limitations. First, like all surveys, our was subject to 1) social desirability bias in that respondents replied with what they thought we as researchers wanted to see, though they were told their responses would be de-identified and 2) generalizability bias given that, while 60% is a laudable survey response rate from a surgeon survey, 40% of hospitals in the US where a patient with an EGS disease might receive care were not represented in our responses. Furthermore, as noted there are some areas with missing data. While the majority of questions regarding critical care resources and processes had a response rate >90%, a majority of respondents did not report what proportion of EGS surgeons have certifications in surgical critical care and a plurality did not report what proportion of EGS surgeons practice surgical critical care. Lack of response to critical care protocols were less prevalent. This difference may be due to a need to look up the answer to these questions rather than immediately available (i.e., ‘off the top of one’s head’ knowledge of clinical practices and protocols). Conclusion: As we hypothesized, our national survey demonstrates that hospitals that have implemented ACS have more robust critical care resources in place compared to hospitals with a GSOC model of care for EGS patients. Given the continuing annual increases of EGS patients in the US who have a high risk of requiring perioperative critical care, these findings are a starting point for developing consensus around which critical care structures and processes are necessary for an ideal ACS model of care to best serve the needs of EGS patients from an institutional and regional perspective. Further investigation is needed to determine whether or not implementation of ACS compared to the traditional ad hoc GSOC approach to EGS coverage, and which critical care resources within each paradigm, affect outcomes for critically ill EGS patients. Supplementary Material Supplementary Material NIHMS1705349-supplement-Supplementary_Material.pdf (484.2KB, pdf) Acknowledgement The authors would like the acknowledge Dr. Catarina Kiefe, PhD MD, Professor and Chair of Population & Quan