ication Not applicable Competing interests The authors declare that they have no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. Contributor Information Jadwiga Dworak, Email: jadwiga.dworak@doctoral.uj.edu.pl. Michał Wysocki, Email: m.wysocki@doctoral.uj.edu.pl. Anna Rzepa, Email: rzepetto90@gmail.com. Michał Natkaniec, Email: michal.natkaniec@uj.edu.pl. Michał Pędziwiatr, Email: michal.pedziwiatr@uj.edu.pl. Andrzej Budzyński, Email: andrzej.budzynski@uj.edu.pl. Piotr Major, Email: piotr.major@uj.edu.pl. References 1. Gagner M, Lacroix A, Bolte E. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy in Cushing’s syndrome and pheochromocytoma. N Engl J Med. 1992;327:1003. doi: 10.1056/NEJM199210013271417. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Maccabee DL, Jones A, Domreis J, et al. Transition from open to laparoscopic adrenalectomy: the need for advanced training. Surg Endosc. 2003;17:1566–1569. doi: 10.1007/s00464-002-8746-5. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Natkaniec M, Pedziwiatr M, Wierdak M, et al. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy for pheochromocytoma is more difficult compared to other adrenal tumors. Wideochir Inne Tech Maloinwazyjne. 2015;10(3):466–471. doi: 10.5114/wiitm.2015.52869. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Pędziwiatr M, Wierdak M, Ostachowski M, et al. Single center outcomes of laparoscopic transperitoneal lateral adrenalectomy - Lessons learned after 500 cases: A retrospective cohort study. Int Surg. 2015;20:88–94. doi: 10.1016/j.ijsu.2015.06.020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Zhang X, Wang B, Ma X, et al. Laparoscopic adrenalectomy for beginners without open counterpart experience: initial results under staged training. Urology. 2009;73:1061–1065. doi: 10.1016/j.urology.2008.11.058. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Sefr R, Penka I, Olivero R, et al. The impact of laparoendoscopic surgery on the training