INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2011,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8571114','Showcasing a General Surgery Residency Program During the COVID-19 Pandemic.','Lund S, MacArthur T, Keune J, Enger T, Martinez-Jorge J, McKenzie T, Nelson M, Olson A, Que F, Stulak J, Rivera M.','2021','narrated showcasing mingle preinterview narrated registrants themed narrated narrated preinterview touring theorize greet narrated showcasing mingle themed breaker favorite synchronously asynchronously narrated cafeteria gym narrated pausing anecdotes registrants registrants narrated narrated voiceover fantastic commentate sped showcasing luckily preinterview narrated narrated r38hl150086 gaetke udager cohan pletcher bruss durr distanced mehtsun qadan hause raut fairweather vining schuitevoerder izreig torabi kasle rahmati manes kenigsberg khouri kuprasertkul ganesan lemack mecham menapace bowe qadan delman roggin phitayakorn heitkamp aljamal prabhakar stulak','Abstract OBJECTIVE The virtual interview season has challenged general surgery residency programs to recruit applicants through the loss of visiting clerkships, tours, and time with residents. Webinars, increased informal resident and faculty sessions, and live-narrated video tours are potential solutions. This study aimed to assess the effectiveness of these elements in virtually showcasing a residency program during the virtual interview season. DESIGN/SETTING Prospective applicants to one general surgery residency program (Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota) were invited to attend six webinars: Program Overview, Simulation Education, Diversity, Resident Life, Mingle with Residents, and Last-Minute Q&A. An anonymous survey was sent to all registered participants of the webinars. Interviewees participated in a preinterview social hour with resident and faculty and a live-narrated video tour of our facilities during their interview. A second anonymous survey was sent to all interviewees. PARTICIPANTS Webinars – 33% of 159 unique registrants surveyed participated. Interviews – 46% of 109 interviewees surveyed participated. RESULTS Average satisfaction with the webinars was 9.4/10. Overall, 98% of attendees felt that the webinars gave them a “feel” for the program. Attendees found the Last-Minute Q&A webinar and Program Overview to be most useful. For resident-led webinars, 100% of attendees felt that themed break-out rooms were effective. Average satisfaction with the interviews was 4.4/5. Interviewees rated access to faculty and residents highly (4.4/5 and 4.5/5, respectively). 98% of interviewees found the live-narrated video tour helpful. CONCLUSIONS A webinar curriculum can be effective in virtual residency recruitment, as prospective applicants developed a good understanding of the resident program after participating. Further, live-narrated video tours and purposefully incorporating several avenues for informal conversations with residents and faculty can successfully address applicant concerns about virtual interviews. Key Words: postgraduate training, interviews, COVID-19, virtual, general surgery COMPETENCIES: Interpersonal and Communication Skills, Professionalism, Systems-Based Practice Introduction In March 2020 the Association of American Medical Colleges issued a statement encouraging all residency, medical school, and fellowship interviews to transition to a virtual format until the resolution of the novel coronavirus 2019 (COVID-19) pandemic. 1 This, along with the general shift to virtual interactions, primed residency programs across the nation to prepare for a virtual interview season. Applicants typically use interview days to determine compatibility based on both subjective opinions and objective details about residency programs. Many of the subjective opinions that candidates form come from spending 1 or 2 days talking to current residents, preinterview social events, interviewing with faculty, and touring facilities and program locations. With virtual interviews, decisions about compatibility will be made solely based on the interview sessions, eliminating many avenues for a future resident to determine their feel for a program. Several editorials propose that the advantages to virtual interviews include minimal disruption to medical student''s rotations and cost-savings, potentially as much as $6000 per applicant. 2 , 3 , 4 On the other hand, editorials theorize that virtual interviews result in the loss of informal interactions between applicants and program leadership, faculty, and residents that enable applicants to evaluate a program for an adequate fit. 2 , 3 Furthermore, virtual interviews do not allow applicants to view training facilities (hospitals/clinics and simulation centers) or experience the city a training program resides within. 2 , 3 Several fellowship programs underwent virtual interviews in the spring of 2020. One editorial published on the experience of surgical oncology virtual fellowship interviews at one program described their interview day process: using break-out rooms for interviews and a main room for social interactions. 5 A survey of the experiences of applicants and faculty after these virtual interviews reported improvement in the perception of the flow of the interview schedule as compared to prior in-person interview days, however, identified areas of concern including inability to experience the local city and hospital environment. 4 Furthermore, a survey of breast surgery fellows after experiencing virtual interviews in the spring of 2020 showed that both applicants and faculty overwhelming felt that the experience was seamless but still expressed a preference for live interviews (69%). 6 In contrast, a recent nationwide survey of Otolaryngology prospective applicants reported that 37% of applicants felt that the program would not have enough information about them based on virtual interviews to make an informed decision and 62% of applicants did not think they would have enough information from virtual interviews to inform their rank list. 7 Furthermore, another nationwide survey of Urology prospective applicants reported that 64% of applicants felt resident informal interactions were the most important component of the interview day and 81% of applicants felt these informal interactions could not effectively be duplicated virtually. 8 In this survey, an additional 87% of applicants felt a city visit could not be accomplished virtually. 7 Several editorials have recommended that programs address the disadvantages to virtual interviews head-on through creative multimedia and interactive virtual solutions. 9 , 10 , 11 Based on these concerns about limited informal interactions between applicants and programs prior to the interview cycle, our general surgery residency program implemented a webinar curriculum for prospective applicants. To address concerns reported in the literature of limited informal interactions with faculty and residents during the interview day, interviewees were invited to an informal meet-and-greet with surgical faculty and current residents the evening before their interview and spent time in break-out rooms throughout the interview day with current residents. Finally, to provide a sense of the surrounding city and hospital facilities, interviewed applicants were given a live narrated, video tour by current residents during their interview day. After implementing these components to our recruitment before and during virtual interviews, we aimed to assess the effectiveness of these virtual recruitment methods in showcasing our general surgery residency program. Material and Methods Webinar Curriculum Prospective applicants to Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota''s general surgery residency program in 2020-2021 cycle were invited via social media and email to attend a webinar curriculum in August to October of 2020. Four webinars were held as a 1- to 1.5-hour long live video conference led by program leadership, in which program leadership gave a brief (15-20 minute) presentation regarding a topic specific to our general surgery resident program. An informal question and answer session followed. The topics for these webinars are Program Overview, Simulation Education, Diversity, and Last-Minute Question & Answer (Q&A) with Program Leadership. Each session started with a 20- to 30-minute presentation on the webinar topic led by a faculty leader in that area. These presentations were followed by a 45 minute to 1 hour session of informal question and answer with numerous faculty and resident panelists. Two additional webinars (Mingle with Residents and Resident Life) allowed prospective applicants to interact with current general surgery residents to ask questions and gain insight about the program. At these resident-led webinars, break-out rooms with selected residents were available for prospective applicants to join in 15-minute rotations throughout 1.5-hour webinar. Each resident-led webinar had four themed break-out rooms, using the themes: Clinical Experience, Residency Tracks (Preliminary, Cardiothoracic Integrated, Vascular Integrated), Life in Minnesota/Residency Culture, and Education & Research Experience. After the last webinar, all registered attendees were sent an anonymous, optional online survey about their webinar experience. Virtual Interviews Virtual interviews at Mayo Clinic in Rochester, Minnesota included several components. The night before the interviews, interviewees attended a 1.5-hour resident and faculty social. Interviewees were rotated randomly through five 15-minute break-out rooms. Ice-breaker questions (i.e., What is your favorite vacation destination?) were used to facilitate conversation. Every interviewee spent one rotation in a break-out room with program leadership, one rotation in a break-out room with only residents, then three rotations in break-out rooms that contained a mix of faculty and residents. During the virtual interview day, interviewees rotated through two 20-minute traditional interviews with surgical faculty and senior residents, a 20-minute virtual simulated skills mini-multiple interview that assessed technical and non-technical skills through six synchronously and asynchronously assessed short stations (described in a previous study), 12 a 60-minute informal question and answer video-call with current residents, and a 60-minute live-narrated video tour. The video tour included clinical (floor, outpatient, and inpatient) and nonclinical (cafeteria, gym, etc.) areas of the hospital and pertinent areas of the surrounding city. Several current residents live-narrated this tour for interviewees during a video call on the interview day, pausing the video to insert anecdotes and answer interviewees’ questions. All components were conducted over a virtual platform, using the break-out room functionality to seamlessly rotate residents through sessions. After the conclusion of the interview day, interviewees were sent an anonymous, voluntary online survey about their virtual interview experience at our institution. This study was deemed exempt by the Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board. Results Webinar Curriculum We had 159 unique registrants for all webinars, with several participants attending more than one webinar. Approximately 100-120 prospective applicants registered for each webinar, with approximately 50-80 attendees at each session. Of the 159 registrants, 33% (53) participated in the survey. Participants attended a variety of webinar sessions, with the largest number attending the Last-Minute Q&A (33 participants) and the Program Overview (29 participants) sessions ( Fig. 1 ). The majority of participants felt that the webinars should be offered in-person in future years (62%), but 34% of participants felt a virtual component to the webinars should continue ( Fig. 2 ). Participants felt that the Program Overview and Last-Minute Q&A sessions gave them the best subjective feel for the residency program ( Fig. 3 ). Almost all of the participants (98%) felt they developed a subjective feel for the residency program after the webinars and 72.5% of participants developed a unique program understanding from attending the webinars ( Fig. 2 ). When asked what the participants felt they learned solely from participating in the webinar curriculum, participants responded: “details about rotations,” “diversity of the program,” “atmosphere of training,” “what the program values in their residents,” “character and personality of faculty and residents which I could never have learned otherwise,” and “learning about resident''s relationships with their PD and faculty.” Overall satisfaction with the webinars was, on average, 9.2 out of 10 ( Table 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Survey respondents’ webinar session attendance. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab (A) Proportion of participants who felt webinars should be offered in-person, virtual, or both in-person and virtual in future years. (B) Proportion of participants who felt the webinars gave them a feel for the program. (C) Proportion of participants who felt they developed a unique program understanding from attending the webinars. FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Average ranking of how well each webinar session gave applicants a subjective feel for our residency program. TABLE 1. Description of Participants Experience With the Webinar and Interview Day Webinars Satisfaction with resident-only sessions (mean [SD]) 9.3 (1.0) Overall satisfaction (out of 10) (mean [SD]) 9.2 (1.2) Interviews Overall satisfaction (out of 5) (mean [SD]) 4.4 (0.9) Understanding of the residency program (out of 5) (mean [SD]) 4.6 (0.6) Access to faculty (out of 5) (mean [SD]) 4.3 (0.8) Access to residents (out of 5) (mean [SD]) 4.5 (0.6) Applicants ability to convey themselves (out of 5) (mean [SD]) 4.2 (0.7) Percentage of applicants that experienced technical issues (count) 22% (11) Open in a new tab With respect to the resident-only webinar sessions, 100% of participants felt the break-out room format was effective and 97% of participant felt the themes used were effective ( Fig. 4 ). Overall satisfaction with the resident-led sessions was, on average, 9.3 out of 10 ( Table 1 ). Participants commented that “it was helpful to see how residents interact with one another” and that residents were “transparent” and their “answers felt honest and real.” When asked what topics or additional themes participants would have found useful, participants responded: “IMG room,” “virtual tour,” and “day in the life of a resident in Rochester.” Participants suggested that “longer break-out room time,” “more residents from different backgrounds (IMGs),” and “increasing the number of residents that participate” would improve resident-led webinars. FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab (A) Proportion of participants who felt break-out rooms were effective in the resident-led webinars. (B) Proportion of participants who felt the themes used for break-out rooms were effective in the resident-led webinars. Virtual Interview We interviewed 54 applicants for categorical positions and 55 applicants for preliminary positions. Of those 109 interviewees, 46% (50 interviewees) responded to the survey. Overall satisfaction with the interview day was, on average, 4.4 out of 5 and interviewees rated their understanding of the program after the interview day at, on average, 4.6 out of 5 ( Table 1 ). Interviewees rated their access to faculty and residents highly, at 4.5 and 4.5 out of 5 on average respectively. Interviewees rated their ability to convey themselves highly, at 4.2 out of 5 on average ( Table 1 ). Only 11 survey participants (22%) experienced technical difficulties, of which 18% (2 interviewees) felt their technical difficulties compromised the integrity of their interview ( Table 1 ). The technical difficulties most commonly encountered involved video recording of technical skills (suturing, knot tying) to upload for asynchronous assessment (8 participants). While the majority (64%) of participants would prefer in-person residency interviews, 36% of participants would prefer some virtual component to resident interviews in the future ( Fig. 5 ). FIGURE 5. Open in a new tab (A) Proportion of participants who felt the live-narrated video tour was helpful in getting to know our residency program facilities. (B) Proportion of participants who would opt for in-person, virtual, or a mixture of formats for future residency interviews at our residency program. With respect to the live narrated video tour, 98% of participants found the tour helpful ( Fig. 5 ). Participants commented that they “absolutely loved the live resident voiceover facility tour,” “it was completely beneficial and insightful,” “this was fantastic,” the tour “exceeded expectations,” and “I appreciated having a resident there to commentate and pause when applicants had questions.” Participants felt the video tour could have been improved by including a section on housing, a sped-up video showing a day in the life of a surgery resident, and more time and residents dedicated to this session. Discussion The abrupt transition to a virtual interview cycle in 2020-2021 due to the COVID-19 pandemic challenged general surgery programs to recruit applicants without showcasing their programs in person. By considering pre-existing literature about potential concerns, we were able to successfully implement a webinar curriculum as virtual recruitment prior to the interview cycle. Prospective applicants were highly satisfied with our webinars. Their comments on the aspects of the program they could have only learned during the webinar curriculum highlights the need for events reaching out to prospective applicants when in-person rotations or visits are not possible. Interestingly, while the two resident-led webinar sessions were highly rated in terms of satisfaction, prospective applicants felt that these sessions were least able to give them a subjective feel for our residency program. Instead, prospective applicants developed that feel for our program most through sessions led directly by our Program Director (Program Overview and Last-Minute Q&A with Program Leadership). This information, paired with prospective applicant suggestions of more time and more residents for the resident-led sessions, can provide useful avenues for change to improve the utility of these resident-led sessions in the coming interview cycle. Further, this finding encourages us and other programs to ensure that prospective applicants have time to hear directly from and ask questions to program leadership throughout residency recruitment. Improving applicant ability to develop a subjective feel for our program was one of our main objectives during residency recruitment. We noted that published editorials and surveys brought up concerns that applicants would not be able to explore this feel for a program well in a virtual format. 7 , 8 , 10 Luckily, by tackling this problem head-on with a webinar curriculum and multiple aspects of the interview day, prospective applicants and interviewees reported that they were able to develop a good understanding of the program. The feedback that prospective applicants came away from the webinar curriculum feeling that they understood the character and culture of our program well, and that they were given insight into how program leadership interacts with residents, speaks to the value of these sessions. When designing our interview day, we addressed concerns from previous survey studies of prospective applicants that informal interactions with residents and city/hospital tours could not be accomplished virtually. 8 We created numerous opportunities for interviewees to interact informally with both residents and faculty throughout the interview experience, through the preinterview social hour and during the interview day. Likely because of these experiences, we saw that interviewees rated thorough access to faculty and residents highly. Additionally, we designed a live narrated video tour of our facilities and surrounding town, which interviewees rated highly as exceeding expectations. Conclusions Virtual interview cycles are fraught with recruitment challenges, as the lack of visiting clerkships and in-person interviews eliminates avenues for residency applicants to determine their fit with a program. We demonstrate a successful way to virtually showcase a general surgery residency program, through a structured webinar recruitment curriculum, a live-narrated virtual facility and city tour, and by highlighting resident and faculty informal interactions during the interview day. Footnotes This project was supported by R38HL150086 Stimulating Access to Research in Residency (TAM) from the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute (NHLBI). References 1. Association of American Medical Colleges. Conducting interviews during the coronavirus pandemic 2020. Available at: https://www.aamc.org/what-we-do/mission-areas/medical-education/conduction-interviews-during-coronavirus-pandemic . Accessed September 10, 2020. 2. Joshi A, Bloom DA, Spencer A, Gaetke-Udager K, Cohan RH. Video interviewing: a review and recommendations for implementation in the era of COVID-19 and beyond. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:1316–1322. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.05.020. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Chou DW, Pletcher SD, Bruss D, Sung CK, Diaz RC, Liang J, Durr ML. Otolaryngology residency interviews in a socially distanced world: strategies to recruit and assess applicants. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2020 doi: 10.1177/0194599820957961. Online ahead of print. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Tseng J. How has COVID-19 affected the costs of the surgical fellowship interview process? J Surg Educ. 2020;77:999–1004. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.05.018. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Molina G, Mehtsun WT, Qadan M, Hause KC, Raut CP, Fairweather M. Virtual interviews for the complex general surgical oncology fellowship: the Dana-Farber/Partners experience. Ann Surg Oncol. 2020;27:3103–3106. doi: 10.1245/s10434-020-08778-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Vining CC, Eng OS, Hogg ME, Schuitevoerder D, Silverman RS, Yao KA, et al. Virtual surgical fellowship recruitment during COVID-19 and its implications for resident/fellow recruitment in the future. Ann Surg Oncol. 2020:1–5. doi: 10.1245/s10434-020-08623-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Izreig S, Torabi SJ, Kasle DA, Rahmati RW, Manes RP. Otolaryngology match 2020-21: survey of prospective applicants in the setting of COVID-19. Ann Otol Rhinol Laryngol. 2021;130(5):450–458. doi: 10.1177/0003489420952470. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Kenigsberg AP, Khouri RK, Jr, Kuprasertkul A, Wong D, Ganesan V, Lemack GE. Urology residency applications in the COVID-19 era. Urology. 2020;143:55–61. doi: 10.1016/j.urology.2020.05.072. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Mecham JC, Menapace DC, Bowe SN, Carlson ML. Recruitment and networking with social media for the otolaryngology match in the COVID-19 pandemic. Otolaryngol Head Neck Surg. 2021;164(3):545–546. doi: 10.1177/0194599820957952. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Sell N, Qadan M, Delman KA, Roggin KK, Spain DA, Phitayakorn R, et al. Implications of COVID-19 on the general surgery match. Ann Surg. 2020;272:155–156. doi: 10.1097/SLA.0000000000004032. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Nguyen JK, Shah N, Heitkamp DE, Gupta Y. COVID-19 and the radiology match: a residency program''s survival guide to the virtual interview season. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:1294–1297. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.06.023. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. AlJamal Y, Prabhakar N, Rivera M, McKenzie T, Stulak J, Heller S, et al. A simulation-based selection process for trying to identify medical students who will become outstanding general surgery residents. Am J Surg. 2020;220:76–82. doi: 10.1016/j.amjsurg.2019.10.050. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',43,'2025-10-13 08:54:48','2025-10-13 08:54:48');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2012,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11088490','Transforming Perceptions: The Impact of a Formal Parental Leave Policy on Surgical Trainees.','Corbisiero MF, Acker SN, Bothwell S, Christian N.','2024','childrearing unsupported setbacks allowances flights procurements rearing sentiments rearing contemplating discourages discourages discourages catered perilous sentiments rearing miscommunication unsupported rearing crafting moonlighting nihms1978260 nihms1978260 nihms1978260 rearing unsupportive courageous easter knell gasior bergus beeler rialon moms tabo acfgme altieri bevilacqua stucke rosenkranz smink e85 e91 tackett smink acker gestating augustine e454 melck smink antonoff jakubowski baltzer lipa e132 humphries garza butz lemelman gaffley riera anzola changala smink marincola terhune avendano brugiavini pasini gokani xepoleas munabi auslander diderichsen verdonk lagro hamberg dason maxim gesink cawthorn oberle vandermeer khurana nihms1978260 nihms1978260 nihms1978260','Abstract Objective: Surgical trainees who welcome a new child during residency often face challenges related to appropriate parental leave. To address this, we instituted a comprehensive family medical leave policy within our training program and assessed resident perceptions before and after the policy’s introduction. We hypothesized that this new formal policy would enhance feelings of support amongst all (not just childbearing) trainees. Design: A web-based survey to gauge resident perceptions on parental leave was distributed to all residents at a single academic general surgery residency at two intervals: prior to policy implementation and one year after policy implementation. Setting: The study was conducted at a single institution, academic general surgery residency program. Participants: All general surgery residents at the institution were included (n = 95). Results: 40 out of 95 (42%) residents participated in the initial survey and 25 of 95 (26%) completed the subsequent survey. There was a significant improvement in resident reported satisfaction with the policy from pre to post: 15% pre to 68% post, p<0.001, report the policy frequently supported trainees’ needs, 20% pre to 88% post, p<0.001, perceived the policy as fair. Most residents (90.0% pre and 80.0% post) perceived pregnancy as a risk during surgical training. There were no differences in perception of the new policy between residents who were parents and residents who were not parents. Conclusions: The introduction of a comprehensive family medical leave policy improved all surgical trainees’ (including non-parents) perception of policy effectiveness and policy fairness. This is counter to the published perception that parental leave creates a burden on fellow trainees. However, pregnancy remains a stressor for the individual new parent. Surgical programs can develop supportive formal family medical leave policies; it is important to address the inherent systemic and cultural barriers surrounding childrearing during surgical training. Keywords: Surgical Training, Parental Leave Policy, Resident Wellness, Surgical Education Reform, Maternity Leave, Paternity Leave Introduction Surgical training is an intensive period that overlaps with potential childbearing years of many trainees. For many surgical residents, the dual pursuit of career and family presents significant challenges. Consequently, there is a need for effective and accommodating parental leave policies that prioritize the health and well-being of the trainee and infant, and also comply with the requirements of graduate surgical education to train competent surgeons. Recent studies highlight potential health risks when pregnant residents or surgeons maintain traditional work routines. 1 – 4 In response to these new data, the American Board of Surgery (ABS) has changed its requirements surrounding medical leave during general surgery training. In 2021, the ABS revised its policy for trainees which now allows for 6 weeks of family or medical leave without extending training once during the PGY 1–3 years and once during the PGY 4–5 years. 5 The ACGME currently requires that programs offer 6 weeks of paid leave. 6 Prior studies have found that trainees feel unsupported in their requests for parental leave. 7 This feeling is not only influenced by their peers but also by faculty. 7 Residents were challenged by a lack of universal leave policy, fear of educational setbacks, and a perceived lack of support from peers and mentors. 7 – 9 Program directors also noted the absence of clear parental leave guidelines. 8 , 9 Program directors and trainees alike seem to favor equal leave opportunities for all new parents, regardless of childbearing status. 8 , 10 Additionally, residents and program directors both support the establishment of formal leave policies. Despite these findings, there continues to be a lack of comprehensive reform. 11 The opportunity of improved leave allowances in 2021 and 2022 by national accrediting bodies was taken as a call to action by our program to establish a formal comprehensive family leave policy ( Table 1 ). This study aims to evaluate the effect of a new, formal family leave policy on trainees by assessing their attitudes and perceptions of parental leave both before and after the implementation of the new policy. This research aims to shed light on an important aspect of surgical training, which may also guide more informed, supportive policymaking. We hypothesize that the implementation of a new formal leave policy will enhance reported feelings of support among residents and be highly tolerable to all trainees in a program. Table 1. Parental leave policy highlights. Category Details Education Inclusion of national, state, local GME, board-specific policies and references Inclusion of relevant literature review Inclusion of residents in creation of policy Schedule adjustments * Call limitations after 20 weeks of pregnancy for pregnant trainees: Only 1 overnight in house call per week Call limitations after 30 weeks of pregnancy for pregnant trainees: No overnight in house call Call limitations for all new parents up to 12 weeks after birth or adoption: No overnight in house call Rotation adjustments based on trainee preference and/or OB recommendation (avoidance of intraoperative chemotherapy, avoidance of intraoperative radiation, moving night float and transplant to other times of year if scheduled after 30 weeks of pregnancy) Process details Who to contact in program and local GME to review benefits How to work with administrative chief residents Assigned faculty mentor How to approach special circumstances (infertility, miscarriage, high medical need pregnancy) Culture change Explicit language around support of program Explicit focus on equity Plans for ongoing faculty and resident education Open in a new tab * Night float was recommended to be moved based on data regarding night shifts and work hours later in training from the literature. 1 , 28 , 29 Transplant was recommended to be moved based on the frequent requirement for flights for procurements. Other rotations to consider moving based on the individual resident and their physician include rotations with exposure to ionizing radiation (such as Vascular) or intraoperative chemotherapy (such as Surgical Oncology). With early communication about pregnancies, annual rotation schedules were made in anticipation of these limitations, so since initiation of this policy, no residents have missed rotations or resulted in lost or excess opportunities for these specific rotations; we anticipate the potential need to navigate these in the future. However, creating a culture in which residents feel comfortable disclosing their pregnancies early has facilitated the ability to plan ahead appropriately. Material and methods Study design The primary goal of this research was to analyze the perceptions and impacts of a formal family medical leave policy on surgical trainees. Specifically, the study aimed to understand the attitudes and perceptions of a new parental leave policy for general surgery residents on the culture of support, burden on trainees, and attitudes towards parenthood during surgical training. To achieve this, a web-based survey was administered to surgical trainees prior to the new policy implementation in 2022 and then again after policy implementation in 2023 at a single, academic general surgery residency program. Survey development The survey employed in this study was informed by a previously published data and tailored to the current study’s objectives. 7 , 12 – 14 Questions pertained to demographic details, personal experience with and attitudes towards parental leave, experiences with child-rearing, and participants’ understanding of the program’s parental leave policy. Specifically, the survey was designed to assess the impact of the department’s new parental leave policy on several key areas: (1) residents’ perceived support from the department, (2) perceived fairness of the policy, (3) the support residents feel they receive during peripartum and postpartum periods, including aspects like breastfeeding and postpartum care, and (4) the policy’s perceived influence on their career advancement. Questions were closed ended. Survey questions were developed by principles investigators (SA and NC) in collaboration with co-investigators with expertise in survey development. Once the survey underwent pilot testing to ensure survey clarity and completeness, it was disseminated to all surgical residents. Survey population and administration The survey was distributed to all general surgery residents (n = 95) at one, academic institution. Survey responses were collected directly into the REDCap database. 15 REDCap is a secure, web-based application that supports data capture for research studies. The survey was distributed to residents at two time points: before and one year after the implementation of the parental leave policy. To maximize participation, a standardized email invitation with a web survey link was sent out three times. No incentives were offered. The survey consisted of 10 questions and took less than 5 minutes to complete ( Appendix A ). A statement of confidentiality and anonymity was included in the electronic-mail invitation. The study was reviewed and approved by the University of Colorado Institutional Review Board. Consent was obtained from all participants prior to completion of the survey, Additional data detailing parental leave usage was compiled for the individual institution’s general surgery residents both before and after the policy’s inception. This encompassed the gender distribution of the residency program, the number of new parents during an academic year, details of residents taking parental or family leave, and the residents’ perception of the policy’s influence on their colleagues’ workloads. This information was obtained from the general surgery residency program’s records and documented in a de-identified, anonymized Excel file. Data Analysis All analyses were performed using R Statistical Software (version 4.2.2, R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria, http://www.R-project.org/ ). Data presented and analyses conducted are based on available responses to each survey query. Type I error rate was set at 0.05. Analyses on primary outcomes were calculated for both unadjusted as well as sex and age adjusted models. Categorical variables were summarized as N (%) and statistical differences were tested using Fisher’s Exact tests for low cell counts, which evaluates whether the distribution of sample categorical data matches an expected distribution. Numerical variables were summarized as Mean (SD) and Median [Range] and statistical differences are tested using Kruskal-Wallis tests. This non-parametric method compares the medians across groups, which is suitable for data that do not follow a normal distribution or when sample sizes are small. Results Forty (Response rate: 42.1%) residents participated in the pre survey and 25 (Response rate: 26.3%) in the post survey. There was no evidence that age, gender, ethnicity, year of training, or childbearing status were different between the pre survey and post survey groups ( Table 2 ). Table 2. Resident demographics. 2022 Data (N=40) 2023 Data (N=25) P-Value Overall (N=65) Age Mean (SD) 30.9 (2.37) 31.3 (2.27) 0.517 31.0 (2.32) Median [Min, Max] 30.5 [27.0, 36.0] 31.0 [28.0, 36.0] 31.0 [27.0, 36.0] Gender Male 17 (42.5%) 9 (36.0%) 0.732 26 (40.0%) Female 22 (55.0%) 16 (64.0%) 38 (58.5%) Ethnicity Hispanic/Latino(a) 3 (7.5%) 2 (8.0%) 0.170 5 (7.7%) Other 11 (27.5%) 2 (8.0%) 13 (20.0%) White Non-Hispanic/Latino(a) 26 (65.0%) 20 (80.0%) 46 (70.8%) Black Non-Hispanic/Latino(a) 0 (0%) 1 (4.0%) 1 (1.5%) Year Junior clinical resident (R1, 2, or 3) 19 (47.5%) 11 (44.0%) 0.882 30 (46.2%) Research years 12 (30.0%) 7 (28.0%) 19 (29.2%) Senior clinical resident (R4 or 5) 9 (22.5%) 7 (28.0%) 16 (24.6%) Children No 33 (82.5%) 17 (68.0%) 0.295 50 (76.9%) Yes 7 (17.5%) 8 (32.0%) 15 (23.1%) Open in a new tab One year after implementation of the policy, data revealed a significant increase in the proportion of residents who said that the policy always or often met the needs of trainees, from 15.0% to 68.0% (p<0.001). There was also a significant change in sentiments regarding the perceived equity of the parental leave policy: 20.0% perceived it as fair in 2022 contrasted with 88.0% of residents who perceived the new policy as fair in 2023 (p<0.001). The proportion of residents who perceived the policy had a negative impact on a trainee’s child-rearing experience significantly decreased from 52.5% in 2022 to 4.0% in 2023. Similarly, residents reported the policy improved perceived support of breastfeeding. Prior to policy implementation, 47.5% reported the leave policy was insufficient to establish breastfeeding. This decreased to only 12.0% in 2023. Importantly, perception that the parental leave policy may serve as a deterrent for trainees contemplating childbearing during residency decreased from 52.5% to 4.0% between 2022 and 2023. Belief among respondents that pregnancy constitutes a potential risk for trainees was 90% in 2022 and 80% in 2023 (p=0.443) ( Table 3 ). Table 3. Resident opinions of parental leave before and after the implementation of a clear, comprehensive formal parental leave policy. 2022 Data (N=40) 2023 Data (N=25) P-Value Overall (N=65) Parental leave policy meets needs of trainees (N, %) Always/Often 6 (15.0%) 17 (68.0%) <0.001 23 (35.4%) Sometimes/Rarely/Never 19 (47.5%) 1 (4.0%) 20 (30.8%) I Don’t Know 15 (37.5%) 0 (0%) 15 (23.1%) Pregnancy is a risky medical condition for surgeons (N, %) Strongly Agree/Agree 36 (90.0%) 20 (80.0%) 0.443 56 (86.2%) Strongly Disagree/Disagree 4 (10.0%) 5 (20.0%) 9 (13.8%) The program’s parental leave policy is fair (N, %) Very Fair/Fair 8 (20.0%) 22 (88.0%) <0.001 30 (46.2%) Very Unfair/Unfair 20 (50.0%) 0 (0%) 20 (30.8%) The program’s parental leave policy impacts a resident’s experience with having a child (N, %) Very positively impacts/Positively impacts 4 (10.0%) 17 (68.0%) <0.001 21 (32.3%) Very negatively impacts/Negatively impacts 21 (52.5%) 1 (4.0%) 22 (33.8%) The program’s parental leave policy impacts a resident’s career advancement (N, %) Very positively impacts/Positively impacts 1 (2.5%) 12 (48.0%) <0.001 13 (20.0%) Very negatively impacts/Negatively impacts 14 (35.0%) 1 (4.0%) 15 (23.1%) The program’s parental leave policy encourages or discourages trainees from having a child during training (N, %) Strongly Encourages/Encourages 1 (2.5%) 18 (72.0%) <0.001 19 (29.2%) Strongly Discourages/Discourages 21 (52.5%) 1 (4.0%) 22 (33.8%) The program’s parental leave policy supports adequate post-partum self-care (N, %) Very Sufficient/Sufficient 3 (7.5%) 15 (60.0%) <0.001 18 (27.7%) Very Insufficient/Insufficient 19 (47.5%) 1 (4.0%) 20 (30.8%) Impact of the parental leave policy on a resident’s ability to provide excellent patient care (N, %) Very positively impacts/Positively impacts 3 (7.5%) 13 (52.0%) 0.003 16 (24.6%) Very negatively impacts/Negatively impacts 10 (25.0%) 2 (8.0%) 12 (18.5%) Open in a new tab When stratifying opinions based on resident level of training, between 30.0% to 42.1% felt the policy often catered to the needs of trainees. However, across various levels of training, a consistent majority (ranging from 75.0% to 94.7%) upheld the notion that pregnancy is perilous for surgeons. Fairness sentiments regarding the policy were diverse across resident statuses, between 31.3% to 52.6%. For additional information, please see Table 3 . Comparison of opinions between residents who were parents and residents who were non-parents was performed both pre- and post-implementation of the new policy. On the pre-implementation survey, residents who were parents (n=7) were less likely to perceive the old policy as meeting the needs of trainees compared to non-parents (n=33), with 71.4% of parents reporting the current policy sometimes/rarely/never met trainee needs compared to 42.4% of non-parents (p=0.05), although non-parents also had high levels of reporting that they did not know whether the policy met the needs of trainees. After the new policy was implemented, there were no differences between parents and non-parents in the survey; of particular note there were no differences in the perception of fairness or impact on trainees. For additional information comparing parents to non-parents’ perspectives, please see Appendices B (pre-implementation survey results) and C (post-implementation survey results). Discussion We found that implementation of a comprehensive parental leave policy improved general surgery residents’ sense of support and equity surrounding parenthood and training. These findings underscore the importance and influence of well-designed and supportive parental leave policies in surgical training programs. The study revealed significant improvements in a variety of metrics following the introduction of a comprehensive family medical leave policy including increased sense of support for postpartum care, a sense of being better accommodated by the program in meeting their specific needs, an increased perception of fairness, and an improved perception of support from the program for child-rearing. While there was an observed positive shift in perceptions surrounding pregnancy during residency after the implementation of the new formal leave policy, several areas require additional research and future action. We also demonstrate that residents who are not parents perceive the policy equally favorably to parents. Previous literature suggests that one of the barriers for trainees is the impression or perception that having a child is an increased or undue burden on their fellow trainees. 7 , 13 , 16 This is also a reported perception by program directors. 9 , 10 This study demonstrates that these concerns, while understandable, are not supported by the data. There were no differences in perception of the policy between parents and non-parents in our program, which again underlines that these types of policies are highly acceptable to all trainees. When creating our new policy, we included the perspectives of many residents, including parents and non-parents as well as male and female trainees. We believe part of the increase from 15.0% to 68.0% in the number of residents who believe the policy meets their needs (p<0.05) highlights the effectiveness of revising parental leave policy based on resident feedback and participation in the process. This process also underscores the role of communication and education surrounding a new formal paid leave policy, which suggests that merely having a policy isn’t enough: it needs to be communicated effectively. Previous research found that miscommunication was a source of feeling unsupported among residents, despite there being seemingly supportive leave policies in place. 7 , 17 , 18 Interestingly, despite a significant increase in the proportion of trainees who found the parental leave policy equitable, a majority (80%) still viewed pregnancy as a medical risk during surgical training. This perception, consistent before and after the implementation of the parental leave policy, highlights the broader challenges beyond policymaking: the cultural beliefs and traditional views entrenched within surgical training. A recent study found that female residents consider leaving medicine at twice the rate of males after becoming parents. 19 These broader challenges continue to stress trainees, which highlights the need for greater transparency and more consistent leave policies. Recent research found that pregnant surgical residents want formal maternity leave policies to combat childbearing-related stigma, desire mentorship on work-life integration, and highly value the support and understanding of faculty and colleagues. 20 Despite this, many surgical training programs in the United States lack transparency in communicating their parental leave policies, both with current and prospective residents. 21 This study helps to address this call for action by residents. The findings suggest that formal policies, which are communicated efficiently can decrease these perceptions and foster an environment that supports childbearing and rearing. One potential limitation of our study is its focus on an American cohort of surgical trainees. It is crucial to acknowledge that the challenges and perceptions of parental leave in surgical training likely vary globally. European nations, for instance, have often been at the forefront of supportive parental leave policies. 22 , 23 Research from countries in Europe could provide valuable insights into crafting policies that address the multifaceted challenges faced by trainees. Furthermore, international perspectives might offer novel solutions, such as shared parental leaves, flexible training models, or extended training periods without penalties have proven to be effective in supporting trainees. 24 , 25 Research shows that supportive parental leave policies may influence specialty selection and increase medical student interest in pursuing a career in surgery, which improves talent and reduces bias within the field. 26 , 27 Additionally, our survey had a response rate from 25–42%, which while consistent with other survey response rates of this nature, may under or over represent attitudes based on which residents responded to the survey. Of note, there was a lower response rate in the 2023 survey, and, overall, there is a higher proportion of female residents and parent residents. The demographic makeup of participants may have biased the outcome of the responses. Lastly, we recognize that the implementation of the policies discussed in this study may pose challenges for smaller residency surgical training programs with limited resident class sizes or staffing resources. As such, to address these constraints, smaller programs can explore collaborative approaches, such as partnering with other surgical programs within the department, including integrated programs or fellowships. Additionally, augmenting the workforce with Advanced Practice Providers (APPs) or moonlighting personnel may offer viable strategies to accommodate call limitations and ensure program compliance without compromising educational quality or patient care. In conclusion, our study highlights the impact of a formal family medical leave policy on general surgery trainees’ perceptions and well-being. However, the journey to ensuring holistic support for trainees does not end with policy implementation. Surgical training programs must actively endeavor to evolve and continuously assess their policies while also addressing the underlying cultural and systemic issues. In doing so, programs can improve the delicate balance that supports both the personal and professional aspirations of our future surgeons. Supplementary Material 1 NIHMS1978260-supplement-1.docx (15.4KB, docx) 2 NIHMS1978260-supplement-2.docx (15.8KB, docx) 3 NIHMS1978260-supplement-3.docx (15.7KB, docx) Highlights. A single, academic department of surgery developed and implemented a new residency parental leave policy. The new policy significantly improved the program’s ability to meet trainees’ needs during pregnancy and early parenthood, from 15.0% in 2022 to 68.0% in 2023 (p<0.001). The new policy increased residents’ perceptions of equity surrounding parenthood and leave, from 20.0% in 2022 to 88.0% in 2023 (p<0.001). After implementing the new policy, concerns about the policy negatively affecting a trainee’s child-rearing experience decreased from 52.5% in 2022 to 4.0% in 2023, and perceptions that the institution was unsupportive of breastfeeding also decreased significantly, from 47.5% in 2022 to 12.0% in 2023. Despite improved perceptions related to the policy, a majority of residents still viewed pregnancy as a risk during surgical training (90.0% in 2022 and 80.0% in 2023). Additional wellness and policy research is needed to better support residents and their transition to parenthood. Acknowledgements We extend our gratitude to all participating residents, with special recognition to the courageous parent-residents who shared their perspectives. We also acknowledge the research on this vital topic and the leadership’s proactive strides towards ensuring the well-being of residents and their growing families, which made this research possible. Footnotes Publisher''s Disclaimer: This is a PDF file of an unedited manuscript that has been accepted for publication. As a service to our customers we are providing this early version of the manuscript. The manuscript will undergo copyediting, typesetting, and review of the resulting proof before it is published in its final form. Please note that during the production process errors may be discovered which could affect the content, and all legal disclaimers that apply to the journal pertain. References 1. Rangel EL, Castillo-Angeles M, Easter SR, et al. Incidence of infertility and pregnancy complications in US female surgeons. JAMA surgery. 2021;156:905–915. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Atkinson RB, Castillo-Angeles M, Kim ES, et al. The long road to parenthood: assisted reproduction, surrogacy, and adoption among US surgeons. Annals of surgery. 2022;275:106–114. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Knell J, Kim ES, Rangel EL. The Challenges of Parenthood for Female Surgeons: The Current Landscape and Future Directions. Journal of Surgical Research. 2023. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gasior AC, Bergus KC, Beeler WH, Xi AS, Rialon KL. Pregnancy in Medical Training: A Survey of Physician Moms on Their Experiences During Residency and Fellowship. Journal of Surgical Education. 2024;81:84–92. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Surgery TABo. Leave Policy - General Surgery. 2022. 6. (ACGME) ACfGME. Institutional Application and Requirements. 2022. 7. Altieri MS, Salles A, Bevilacqua LA, et al. Perceptions of surgery residents about parental leave during training. JAMA surgery. 2019;154:952–958. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Castillo-Angeles M, Stucke RS, Rosenkranz KM, Smink DS, Rangel EL. Paternity leave during surgical training: perspectives of male residents. Journal of Surgical Education. 2022;79:e85–e91. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Sandler BJ, Tackett JJ, Longo WE, Yoo PS. Pregnancy and parenthood among surgery residents: results of the first nationwide survey of general surgery residency program directors. Journal of the American College of Surgeons. 2016;222:1090–1096. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Castillo-Angeles M, Smink DS, Rangel EL. Perspectives of general surgery program directors on paternity leave during s