ffect of the pandemic on general surgery resident operative volume and whether all case types are affected. A single-institution study found a 63.3% reduction in operative volume in March and April 2020 compared with March and April 2019. 14 However, they used overall operative volume data from the hospital as a proxy for resident operative volume and did not directly look at residents’ ACGME operative logs. In another single-institution report, the authors noted that mean total major cases logged daily by residents decreased from 21 to 10 during the pandemic. 7 This report was an early, 6-week experience at a single level 1 trauma center during which the residency schedule was restructured. In our multi-institutional study, we directly analyzed resident operative case logs throughout a 4-month period, included diverse institutions from across the county, divided residents into PGY levels, and described which operative case types were affected. Given that many hospitals were limited to performing emergent and urgent cases during the COVID-19 pandemic, our study sought to determine which case types were affected. With the exception of trauma, small intestine, liver, and pancreas, all case types in the present study declined in March to June 2020 compared with the same period in 2018 and 2019. The lack of decline in small intestine, liver, and pancreas cases were likely the result of low volume of these case types at baseline. Trauma cases, in contrast, did not decline during the pandemic, likely because they are emergent and not affected by the restriction of elective cases. In fact, a recent study from Philadelphia found that violent trauma, including penetrating trauma and gunshot wounds, significantly increased during Pennsylvania’s stay-at-home order. 17 Another study found the majority of operations still being performed during the height of the pandemic were trauma, emergency general surgery, and burn cases. 7 This is consistent with our finding that the only program factor that was somewhat protective of a decline in case volume during the pandemic was level 1 trauma center status. However, level 1 trauma center status did not completely prevent the reduction in resident operative experience, as we found that resident case volume at level 1 trauma centers still significantly declined during the pandemic. Not surprisingly, purely elective cases such as endoscopies and hernia surgeries saw the greatest declines, both greater than 50%. With the cancellation of elective operations and the restructuring of surgical residencies during the pandemic, surgical resident education has undoubtedly been negatively affected. This is highlighted by the fact that the American Board of Surgery (ABS) took immediate action in anticipation of an operative decline. For general surgery residents graduating in 2020, the ABS modified the usual graduation requirements. Traditional graduation requirements include completing 850 total major cases, including 200 during PGY5. For 2020 graduates, the ABS accepted a 10% decrease in total major cases and did not require a minimum of 200 cases during PGY5. 18 In our study, chief residents during the 2019-2020 academic year logged a mean of 272 cases during PGY5, which would be sufficient to graduate without the modifications made by the ABS. Although the ABS provided relaxed requirements for 2020 graduates, we also found that PGY1 to 4 residents experienced a significant decrease in operative volume during the first 4 months of COVID-19. One modification the ABS made for residents still in training was allowing 6 months into PGY3 to complete 250 cases, whereas traditionally, 250 cases must be completed by the end of PGY2. 18 In our study, this modification was necessary for 2019-2020 PGY2 residents, who only completed a mean of 234.6 cases by the end of PGY2 (mean of 87.3 cases during 2018-2019 PGY1 plus 147.3 cases during 2019-2020 PGY2). As the pandemic continues, it is likely residents in the 2020-2021 academic year will experience ongoing reductions in operative volume. 19 It will be important to continue to track surgical residents’ operative volumes as the pandemic rages on. The ABS may need to consider further modifications to graduation requirements for residents graduating after 2020. Hopefully a more drastic alternative, such as extending surgical training, will be unnecessary. Just as important as ensuring surgical residents meet graduation requirements is ensuring that residents are adequately trained to advance to independent practice or fellowship. Even prior to the pandemic, there have been concerns that many general surgery residency graduates may not be prepared technically for independent practice or fellowship, partly because resident autonomy in the operation room has been decreasing over time. 12 , 13 The decreased operative volume during the pandemic, as reported in our study, may exacerbate this problem. Despite the modified ABS graduation requirements, many residents are worried about their decreased operative experience during the pandemic. 14 , 15 , 16 A recent survey reported that less than half of junior residents (34%-42%) and less than two-thirds (58%-65%) of senior residents would feel comfortable graduating with less than the traditional case numbers required by the ABS. 15 This suggests that a significant number of residents may have concerns about readiness to graduate without meeting the required minimum technical experience. In the first 4 months of the pandemic, we found that residents performed a mean of 22.7 fewer cases (33.5% decrease) compared with the same period in 2018 and 2019. If cases continue to decline at the same rate for the entire 2020-2021 academic year, the absolute case reduction will grow to 113.5, which is 13.3% of the 850 cases required by the ABS, and residents may have trouble completing enough cases to feel comfortable graduating. Surgical training programs face unique challenges in identifying strategies to overcome the decrease in operative experience associated with the pandemic. Similar to other studies, we found that several programs transitioned to virtual didactic curriculums. 5 , 6 , 9 , 10 , 15 Nine programs added telehealth visits to augment residents’ clinical training. Three programs increased resident access to surgical simulators. Others have reported using surgical videos to supplement residents’ technical education. 9 There may be some positives of this virtual transition because most surgery residents are participating in more didactic education during COVID-19 than previously. 15 However, the concern is that none of these measures may fully replace the loss of operative experience because it is difficult to replicate surgery on actual patients using a virtual format. The pandemic may bring into sharper focus the need to create more realistic virtual surgery simulation capabilities to augment surgical training. It will also be important for faculty to commit to prioritizing resident participation and autonomy in the operating room once cases resume. Limitations There are several limitations to our study. First, the pandemic has affected some regions of the US more than others and at different times. Our cohort of programs is neither a random nor an all-inclusive sample of training programs. Our convenience sample of 16 residency programs does represent 7 states with broad geographic distribution: the West (California, Washington), Midwest (Illinois, Nebraska), Northeast (New York), and South (Alabama, Texas). Also, program characteristics in this study only reflect the primary institution and not the other rotation sites. Another limitation is that the major period analyzed was March to June. When data collection began in July 2020, COVID-19 cases were trending down, and many hospitals were well into resuming elective operations. However, we saw additional peaks of COVID-19 cases in late July and November 2020, and further limitations on scheduling elective cases seem inevitable. 19 Our study may not tell the whole story of the decline of surgical resident operative volume because the pandemic is not over. Given that prior pandemics have lasted 1 to 2 years, there will likely be an even greater decline in residents’ operative volumes as the pandemic continues. 20 Conclusions During the first 4 months of the COVID-19 pandemic, there was a significant reduction in general surgery residents’ operative volume. This affected all PGY levels and most case types. Level 1 trauma centers were less affected than non–level 1 trauma centers. COVID-19 has undoubtedly affected general surgery resident operative experience, and many surgical residents are concerned that they will not be eligible or prepared technically for graduation because of the pandemic. As the pandemic continues, the long-term implications of COVID-19 on general surgery resident education remains to be seen. References 1. World Health Organization . Archived: WHO timeline-COVID-19. Published April 27, 2020. Accessed October 5, 2020. https://www.who.int/news/item/27-04-2020-who-timeline---covid-19 2. FEMA . COVID-19 emergency declaration. Press release. Published March 14, 2020. Accessed October 5, 2020. https://www.fema.gov/news-release/20200726/covid-19-emergency-declaration 3. American College of Surgeons . COVID-19: recommendations for management of elective surgical procedures. Published March 13, 2020. Accessed October 5, 2020. https://www.facs.org/covid-19/clinical-guidance/elective-surgery 4. Adesoye T, Davis CH, Del Calvo H, et al. Optimization of surgical resident safety and education during the COVID-19 pandemic: lessons learned. J Surg Educ. 2021;78(1):315-320. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.06.040 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Coyan GN, Aranda-Michel E, Kilic A, et al. The impact of COVID‐19 on thoracic surgery residency programs in the US: a program director survey. J Card Surg. Published online August 16, 2020. doi: 10.1111/jocs.14954 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Juprasert JM, Gray KD, Moore MD, et al. Restructuring of a general surgery residency program in an epicenter of the coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic: lessons from New York City. JAMA Surg. 2020;155(9):870-875. doi: 10.1001/jamasurg.2020.3107 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Meneses E, McKenney M, Elkbuli A. Reforming our general surgery residency program at an urban level 1 trauma center during the COVID-19 pandemic: towards maintaining resident safety and wellbeing. Am J Surg. 2020;220(4):847-849. doi: 10.1016/j.amjsurg.2020.06.001 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Nassar AH, Zern NK, McIntyre LK, et al. Emergency restructuring of a general surgery residency program during the coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic: the University of Washington Experience. JAMA Surg. 2020;155(7):624-627. doi: 10.1001/jamasurg.2020.1219 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Chick RC, Clifton GT, Peace KM, et al. Using technology to maintain the education of residents during the COVID-19 pandemic. J Surg Educ. 2020;77(4):729-732. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.03.018 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. White EM, Shaughnessy MP, Esposito AC, Slade MD, Korah M, Yoo PS. Surgical education in the time of COVID: understanding the early response of surgical training programs to the novel coronavirus pandemic. J Surg Educ. 2021;78(2):412-421. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.07.036 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Zarzaur BL, Stahl CC, Greenberg JA, Savage SA, Minter RM. Blueprint for restructuring a department of surgery in concert with the health care system during a pandemic: the University of Wisconsin Experience. JAMA Surg. 2020;155(7):628-635. doi: 10.1001/jamasurg.2020.1386 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Mattar SG, Alseidi AA, Jones DB, et al. General surgery residency inadequately prepares trainees for fellowship: results of a survey of fellowship program directors. Ann Surg. 2013;258(3):440-449. doi: 10.1097/SLA.0b013e3182a191ca [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Napolitano LM, Savarise M, Paramo JC, et al. Are general surgery residents ready to practice? a survey of the American College of Surgeons Board of Governors and Young Fellows Association. J Am Coll Surg. 2014;218(5):1063-1072.e31. doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2014.02.001 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Collins C, Mahuron K, Bongiovanni T, Lancaster E, Sosa JA, Wick E. Stress and the surgical resident in the COVID-19 pandemic. J Surg Educ. 2021;78(2):422-430. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.07.031 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Aziz H, James T, Remulla D, et al. Effect of COVID-19 on surgical training across the United States: a national survey of general surgery residents. J Surg Educ. 2021;78(2):431-43. doi: 10.1016/j.jsurg.2020.07.037 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Pelargos PE, Chakraborty A, Zhao YD, Smith ZA, Dunn IF, Bauer AM. An evaluation of neurosurgical resident education and sentiment during the coronavirus disease 2019 pandemic: a North American Survey. World Neurosurg. 2020;140(5):e381-e386. doi: 10.1016/j.wneu.2020.05.263 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Abdallah HO, Zhao C, Kaufman E, et al. Increased firearm injury during the COVID-19 pandemic: a hidden urban burden. J Am Coll Surg. 2021;232(2):159-168.e3. doi: 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2020.09.028 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. American Board of Surgery . FAQs - 2020 hardship modifications to general surgery training requirements. Accessed October 5, 2020. https://www.absurgery.org/default.jsp?faq_gshardship 19. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention . COVID data tracker. Accessed December 2, 2020. https://covid.cdc.gov/covid-data-tracker/index.html#cases_casesinlast7days 20. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention . Past pandemics. Accessed October 25, 2020. https://www.cdc.gov/flu/pandemic-resources/basics/past-pandemics.html',20,'2025-10-13 08:55:28','2025-10-13 08:55:28');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2015,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12131605','The ARTS of being a surgeon: a qualitative study exploring supervisors'' trust decisions when granting autonomy to trainees.','Hesseldal KB, Møller JE, Paltved C, Madsen AH, Jensen RD.','2025','notss zwisch optrust intersubjective optrust salim confines conceptualise delves corbin handing builded notts nvivo14 kbh rdj reanalysed kbh ahm kbh bothers uneasy arrogant jerk yell respectfully p15 fortunate anytime handing humble hesitate drag ambidexterity acumen mile insist duller pitches elbows tackles daring patientcare kindness humble arrogant jerk yell humble ambidexterity acumen p15 incompetency fortunate anytime litmits humble hesitate drag handing mile insist duller pitches elbows ticket ahle intersubjective optrust optrust fluctuate unfold episteme techne phronesis episteme techne phronesis episteme techne phronetic phronetic phronetic imparted hafferty enactments transcends ticking adaption unspoken unarticulated exemplifying unarticulated verbalising unfold heimstra adapts navigates ticking employments whey notss kbh rdj ahm karhesse ahle gielissen blasberg blomqvist brasel grambau buyske sarosi minter carlsen lindorff funch morcke charmaz belgrave alseidi kwakye smink bengtson dalrymple crabtree swenson cristancho apramian vanstone novick charmaz poth phronetic e489 dansk kirurgisk selskab malbeskrivelse speciallægeuddannelsen kirurgi sst viden uddannelse uddannelse afspeciallaeger maalbeskrivelser kirurgiskespecialer maalbeskrivelse kirurgi ashxsclang hash 900248248b63b7fe08669eab480a8a86 darosa zwischenberger meyerson teitelbaum soper flyvbjerg cbo9780511810503 gofton unveiling hafferty mobilio brydges glatt moulton cae hiemstra kolkman wolterbeek trimbos stalmeijer seyer cristancho levstik chandok spafford schryer reznick murnaghan molm mylopoulos s46 0b013e31822a70be quillin cortez pritts hanseman workweek salim govaerts magas scally minter burdine nikolian sutzko matusko minter hawick sp2 supplement_7 znab361 sloth seyer corbin newbury corbin teman gauger tarpley minter varpio ajjawi monrouxe problematising watling woelfel strosberg villarreal harzman salani woelfel pieper meara yiu tacit forges yule flin maran rowley yule suc karhesse','Abstract Background Workplace-based learning in surgery has developed an inherent focus on competency-based assessment tools and quantifiable skills. Trust is an essential element in the framework of Entrustable Professional Activities (EPA). However, trust is difficult to quantify and assess. Procedural variation seen among residents may be influenced by supervisors’ trust in their trainee. The objective of this study was to explore how supervisors base their trust decisions when granting autonomy to surgical residents. Methods A constructivist grounded theory approach was used to guide interviews with specialists in general surgery at different teaching hospitals in Denmark. Results We recruited and interviewed 19 surgeons, all of whom were specialised within general surgery. In the interviews, the informants articulated four interconnected themes that characterise the elements that supervisors employ when making trust decisions about when to grant autonomy to surgical residents: (1) approach, (2) relational skills and competencies, (3) departmental solidarity, and (4) technical skills and knowing own limits. On the basis of these four categories, we created the acronym ARTS. Conclusion ARTS may provide a point of departure for critical discourse on the criteria that should inform decisions regarding the granting of autonomy, as well as the essential components that ought to be integrated into assessment frameworks. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1186/s12909-025-07326-0. Keywords: Surgical education, Resident autonomy, Entrustment, Assessment Introduction Trust is a crucial element in competence-based education, yet it is challenging to quantify or assess trust as a competence. For residents, it is vital that senior colleagues trust them to perform surgeries in a manner that ensures patient safety. This trust is demonstrated when senior colleagues allow the resident to carry out procedures and grant them autonomy. Entrustment has been a fundamental and extensive concept in the understanding and implementation of EPAs. EPAs reflects the all-round assessment of residents and beliefs regarding which competencies a specialist in surgery must possess [ 3 , 42 ]. Trust is an essential element in the EPA framework, but entrustability is difficult quantify or assess as a competence. Entrustment has been an essential and comprehensive concept in the introduction and understanding of EPAs [ 41 , 42 ]. Workplace-based learning within surgery has increasingly centred around competency-based assessment tools and quantifiable skills, with most curricula having quantitative aims regarding the number of specific procedures and quality assessment of trainee performance. However, there is considerable variety in the number of performed procedures when comparing the operative volume of residents both within the same department and between hospitals [ 4 , 32 , 37 ]. There exist a variety of tools to assess a trainees''performance in the operating room (OR). The broad spectrum of assessment tools used in the OR, such as the Objective Structured Assessment of Technical Skills (OSATS) and Non-Technical Skills for Surgeons (NOTSS) [ 27 , 48 , 49 ], assess both technical and non-technical skills. However, recent studies emphasize elements such as trust and relational competencies as being essential for the number of procedures that residents undertake [ 6 , 23 , 33 , 35 ]. Tools like the Zwisch Autonomy Scale, align the level of supervision with the degree of trust: from ‘show and tell’ to active help, passive help, and supervision only [ 14 ]. Whereas other tools like OpTrust includes faculty entrustment and evaluates behavioural domains during surgery including 1) types of questions asked, 2) operative plan, 3) instruction, 4) problem solving and 5) leadership [ 34 ]. The above-mentioned variation in operative experience may be influenced by intersubjective factors, such as shared understanding, experience, and communication between individuals, including agreement, empathy, and mutual recognition [ 23 ]. According to Sandhu et al. [ 34 ] OpTrust should be utilized "during the intraoperative educational interaction." However, Jensen et al. [ 23 ] highlight that behaviours outside the OR influence the relationship between supervisor and trainee intraoperative. Similarly, Salim et al. [ 33 ] importantly investigated how trust is constructed in the OR and found that pre-operative trustworthiness of the residents is key [ 33 ]. Building on this, there is a high degree of variability in how supervising surgeons construct trust. Insights gleaned from sociological studies indicate that assessment of residents continues beyond the confines of the OR [ 23 , 36 ]. As such, the concept of trust is described in various ways, all related to the exchange or implicit agreement between individuals [ 28 ]. Trust involves a social and cultural comprehension of a constructed reality [ 2 ]. Trust is seen as the basis for supervisors’ granting of autonomy to residents [ 35 , 45 ]. Compared to other clinical environments, the OR is described as a particularly complex learning environment due to the high consequences of failure [ 26 ]. Learning how to navigate in this complex environment is often challenging for both trainees and supervisors [ 47 ]. A supervisor may face conflicting identities, acting both as a supervisor and a surgeon with responsibility for patient safety and workplace efficiency [ 20 , 46 ]. Such aspects may impact when supervisors decide to trust a trainee and grant them autonomy to perform a task. While structured tools to assess residents'' competences in the OR exist, it is not clear how supervisors utilize these tools or to what extend preoperative elements influence intraoperative trust-decisions. Further definition is needed about how supervising surgeons construct trustworthiness of residents. The present study aims to explore what aspects supervisors base their trust decision on when granting operative autonomy to surgical residents. Methods To explore how supervising surgeons conceptualise trust in trainees, we used the principles of constructivist grounded theory (GT). As the research question delves into social interactions and experiences, we found GT to be an appropriate methodology. The choice was informed by GT''s objective of constructing theoretical explanations for social phenomena [ 11 ]. GT has developed since Corbin and Strauss [ 39 ] and it comes in different forms. We used a GT approach where the presented meanings were constructed among the participants, the interviewer, and the research team [ 5 ]. GT has been described as a useful methodology in medical education [ 24 , 44 ] and has previously been used to investigate phenomena such as problem-solving [ 30 ], requests for clinical support [ 25 ] and clinical uncertainty [ 9 ]. Participants All informants were specialist physicians in general surgery at different teaching hospitals in Denmark. We did not include authorities on surgical education even though they may serve as clinical supervisors. This was to capture the supervisors’ own reflections rather than the organisation’s agenda. We chose to use three different sampling strategies to recruit participants. First, we employed a mix of purposive and snowball sampling, inviting informants with a range of years of practice, sex, and geographic location [ 17 , 31 ]. To gather rich in-depth data, we chose a purposive sampling strategy to target participants who would have experience and perspectives relevant to our phenomenon. To access participants outside the authors network, we used snowball sampling. To advance the development of emerging analytical considerations, we employed theoretical sampling to both expand and challenge the theory on trust aiming to identify gaps that require further exploration [ 10 ]. Data collection We conducted in-person, individual interviews between October 2022 and May 2023. Most of the interviews were conducted in surgical departments, while two took place in domestic settings. We developed, pilot-tested and used a semi-structured interview guide to explore the supervisors’ views on our phenomena of interest (Appendix 1). The interviews were audio-recorded and transcribed verbatim. Throughout data collection, we applied a constant comparative approach to analyse the transcripts -an iterative process that guided and informed subsequent interviews [ 8 ]. During data collection, we revised the interview guide to explore new themes. We conducted interviews until no new themes emerged that related directly to our research question [ 29 , 43 ]. According to Morse, saturation occurs when enough data has been gathered to form a thorough and convincing understanding of the phenomenon being studied [ 29 ]. The informants were asked questions about topics that encompassed (1) the organisation and structure of learning, (2) handing over the knife, (3) use of assessment tools, (4) cultural dynamics within the department, and (5) aspects related to independence. The interviews were conducted in Danish and selected quotes were translated. The Danish setting General surgery specialist training consists of a one-year of internship and a five-year fixed-term residency. After the six years of specialist training, the physician may apply for a permanent position as an attending. No board examinations need to be passed to achieve specialisation. The formal education is builded around EPAs, national surgical courses and workplace-based competencies assessment with assessment tools like OSATS, Mini-Cex and NOTTS [ 13 ]. Data analysis Analysis of the interviews was supported by NVivo14 qualitative data analysis software. The first four interviews were independently coded using an open line-by-line approach by at least two authors (KBH, RDJ, JEM) [ 38 ]. This line-by-line coding was compared to create initial categories. For the subsequent six interviews, broad categories were created, the coding was constantly compared and the categories iteratively informed the ongoing data collection [ 5 ]. The transcripts were reviewed both independently and together in the team. From the group coding sessions, a framework was developed to focus the coding and ongoing data collection until no new themes that reflected our research question were identified [ 8 , 29 , 43 ]. The coding framework was then used to analyse iteratively the remaining nine interviews. To ensure a thorough approach, the interviews were reanalysed using theoretical coding, which consolidated the themes. Reflexivity The research team had expertise within surgery and clinical training (KBH, AHM), and included surgical educational scientists with expertise in qualitative methods (RD, JEM, CP). This divergence supported the comprehensive analytical process. During data collection, analysis, and team meetings, the primary coder (KBH) kept additional memos to map and clarify emerging ideas [ 16 ]. Ethical approval The research project and the included studies were approved by the Institutional Review Board at Aarhus University (R. no. 233066) and registered with the Central Denmark Region Trial Registration Service (R. no. 1–16-02158-21). Furthermore, the Central Denmark Region Committees on Health Research Ethics waived the need for approval (R. no. 1–10–721–21). Informed consent was given by participants. Results We recruited and interviewed 19 surgeons, nine women, all of whom practised within general surgery. Of them, seven were consultants, and 12 were senior registrars. In Denmark senior registrars are specialists with at least 8-years postgraduate training and often with a permanent position. The informants were from ten different surgical departments from the North, Central, and Southern Denmark Regions; five were employed at two different university hospitals and 14 were from eight different regional hospitals. The informants practised in different sub-specialities that included colorectal, acute, bile duct, and bariatric surgery, and all had daily supervisor responsibilities. The interviews lasted an average of 44 min. Through the analysis, we identified four interconnected themes that underpinned the aspects that supervisors used to base their trust decisions when granting autonomy to surgical residents: (1) approach, (2) relational skills and competencies, (3) technical skills and competencies and (4) departmental solidarity. Table 1 presents the four themes along with corresponding quote and subthemes. Table 1. The four themes with quotes and subthemes Theme Quote Subthemes Approach "Well, I figure things out when I listen to how they explain things, what they''re saying, and how they''ve evaluated patients. I quickly get a feel for it when we''re discussing whether patients need surgery or not. I can tell if what they''re saying makes sense and if they''re presenting it properly, or if it''s a bit risky. If it''s on the risky side, I pay more attention. But if it seems well thought out and reasonable and they have a good understanding of the situation, then I''m more trusting." P9 "So, I was with this junior doctor, and we were performing a diagnostic laparoscopy, where I asked,''Okay, she doesn''t have appendicitis, so what else could she possibly have?''And the person had absolutely no idea what other differential diagnoses there could be for appendicitis. That really bothers me. Because it shows a lack of preparation and a lack of effort, which kind of suggests that the person doesn''t really care about this.” P8 "If they haven''t prepared at all for the patient, and I know they have the time to do so, then I actually become a bit uneasy. I don''t know what I can trust. I don''t know what to expect." P4 "Well, personally, if I''m teaching someone something, I expect them to have done their homework on the procedure beforehand. If they haven''t, or if I sense some uncertainty about what''s going to happen, it raises red flags for me in terms of how we''re going to approach this operation."P7 Residents should: be structured be well-prepared be visible and leave a good overall impression provide meaningful suggestions Residents should be prepared in: Specific procedures Pathology Anatomy Differential diagnostics Relational skills and competencies "Well, I really want people who are going to be doctors to be, you know, kind, just nice. They should be empathetic towards patients, take care of their responsibilities, and do their job properly. So, if he''s an arrogant jerk, he might not get as many opportunities to perform surgeries as someone who shows a bit more humility and understanding of teamwork. It makes a huge difference, and if someone can''t contribute for some reason, training becomes a bit challenging. You can''t deny that they might get fewer chances and not get the knife in their hand as many times." P1 "Willingness to listen and being interested in other people and how you talk to each other are, of course, extremely important attributes. I have no respect, I really have no respect for people who yell at others just because they''re in a bad mood." P8 " Well, if I see one of the younger doctors who is appreciated — someone who contributes to a good atmosphere, also across different professional groups — then there’s no doubt that I’ll be more inclined to favor them." P13 Residents should be able to: - show an interest in achieving personal relationship - be kind - be honest - be empathetic - show respect - take responsibility - show good character - communicate respectfully Technical skills "The technical, surgical aspect is actually the easiest because I can see it with my own eyes." P15 "I''m fortunate to have many shifts, and shifts with different people, so I get to know them. It''s that feeling where you know you can trust them, that here is someone who would say,''If I''m in a tight spot, I''ll call anytime,''and I think that''s important to know about people. Whereas with others, you might wonder,''Will they call, or will they just try to handle it themselves?''before it goes completely wrong." P9 "I really don''t need many minutes to see if this resident can use the instruments properly and if they understand tissue handling and progression in the surgery." P8 "So, if I''ve seen that they have a steady hand, know what they''re doing, and think things through, then I have no problem handing over—they can just call if they run into trouble. But it''s also about those who I know will stop when there''s something, meaning they are aware of their own limitations." P16 "As a starting point, I want them to be humble about the task, so I can be sure that they ask for help even when I''m not around. They shouldn''t hesitate. I want to be sure that if they encounter any issues, they communicate rather than do something wrong or drag it out for too long." P1 Supervisors stressed that: Technical skills – are straightforward to assess, and the following are essential: - ambidexterity - hand–eye coordination - calm and secure movements - tactile expertise - visuospatial acumen Technical competencies – are complex to assess, and the following are essential: - behaviour and applying skills - knowing your own limits - knowing when to call for help/supervision - having an operative strategy "Not just doing surgery – but being a surgeon" Departmental solidarity "It would also mean being willing to go the extra mile. I mean, if you always insist on your rights and leave at 3:08 PM and never take an extra shift or such like. Personally, I believe that contributing a bit to the community is one of the things that helps us keep things together. You must contribute a bit to the community if you want to receive. There''s no doubt that if you don''t do that, it will have a negative impact on my assessment." P13 "That everyone is in it together. Both in the operating room, so there''s a shared goal that things go well, but also in the somewhat duller tasks like ward rounds and handling emergency patients that come in. That''s where everyone pitches in." P5 "It has to be fair. If someone comes in with sharp elbows, pushing the others aside, they won''t get my approval. That''s unfair." P1 "And as far as I can see, I can easily come across younger doctors who meet all those requirements but whom I would never want to have anything to do with in a workplace." P11 Residents must: - show engagement in the department - be physically visual - be helpful - take extra shifts - show solidarity with their peers, e.g. by sharing operations - be a good and fair colleague - complete tasks - understand that all task are important Open in a new tab Theme 1: approach The theme Approach was described as how a resident tackles a problem, task, or situation. The theme encompasses how the residents use different problem-solving strategies, their attitudes towards both unforeseen challenges and daily routine tasks, and techniques to achieve something or to address an issue. The supervisors noticed the importance of residents being structured and well-prepared when they build trust. This theme was articulated as a visible and overall impression of the residents. All of the informants stressed the importance of the resident''s approach and performance in interactions, such as when handling over patient information or sharing thoughts and suggestions about a treatment. One informant commented regarding approach when sharing thoughts about treatment: "Well, I figure things out when I listen to how they explain things, what they''re saying, and how they''ve evaluated patients. I quickly get a feel for it when we''re discussing whether patients need surgery or not. I can tell if what they''re saying makes sense and if they''re presenting it properly, or if it''s a bit risky. If it''s on the risky side, I pay more attention. But if it seems well thought out and reasonable, and they have a good understanding of the situation, then I''m more trusting." P9. As seen in this quote, the informant emphasizes how the residents approach the clinical event. In addition to the impression of if the residents approach the treatment by being well-prepared, daring or bold suggestions from residents could determine whether supervisors kept an eye out and did not trust the resident. On the other hand, behaviours as being well-prepared both in terms of specific procedures and knowledge of pathology, anatomy, and differential diagnostic skills were related to autonomy, and trust. Informants perceived these elements as a positive approach from the resident in regards to their interest in the speciality and showing an overall responsibility for patientcare. "Well, personally, if I''m teaching someone something, I expect them to have done their homework on the procedure beforehand. If they haven''t, or if I sense some uncertainty about what''s going to happen, it raises red flags for me in terms of how we''re going to approach this operation." P7. In this first theme supervisors describe how they base their trust decisions on how residents approach both patient treatment and the presentation of the treatment to their supervisors. This is both inside and outside the OR and it is important how residents appeared when handling over patient information, but also when they present cases during morning rounds or other academic activities in the department. Theme 2: relational skills and competencies While approach concerned the overall impression of a resident, relational skills and competencies, on the other hand, had to do with the personal relationship between the resident and supervisor, and unfolded in conversations between the resident and supervisor. Collegial relationships and personal characteristics such as humility, kindness, and honesty were also highlighted by the informants. As one explained, a resident must be nice, humble, and show respect for all healthcare professionals in the department: "Well, I really want people who are going to be doctors to be, you know, kind, just nice. They should be empathetic towards patients, take care of their responsibilities, and do their job properly. So, if he''s an arrogant jerk, he might not get as many opportunities to perform surgeries as someone who shows a bit more humility and understanding of teamwork. It makes a huge difference, and if someone can''t contribute for some reason, training becomes a bit challenging. You can''t deny that they might get fewer chances and not get the knife in their hand as many times." P1. Similar, supervisors highlighted that the trainee must show good character and communicate with respect: "Willingness to listen and being interested in other people and how you talk to each other are, of course, extremely important attributes. I have no respect, I really have no respect for people who yell at others just because they''re in a bad mood." P8. Informants recognized how residents built relationships in new environments. This included being humble and showing respect in their relations. Theme 3: technical skills and knowing own limits Informants differentiated between technical skills and technical competencies within the OR. While technical skills concern ambidexterity and hand–eye coordination, technical competencies are broader and relate to how a resident applies skills in the OR. Such competencies involve knowing one’s own limits in technical aptitude and knowing when to call for help or supervision. Important skills were characterized as calm and secure movements, tactile expertise, and visuospatial acumen. These technical skills were deemed straightforward to observe and assess. As one informant stated: "The technical, surgical aspect is actually the easiest because I can see it with my own eyes." P15 And as another informant stressed: "I really don''t need many minutes to see if this resident can use the instruments properly and if they understand tissue handling and progression in the surgery." P8 While such skills were considered straightforward to assess, competencies were described as more complicated to evaluate and held immense significance in determining the graded autonomy of residents. Informants’ reflections thus related not just to doing surgery, but to being a surgeon. Informants emphasized the critical importance of self-awareness regarding one''s competencies, highlighting awareness of one''s own incompetency and its direct impact on decision-making abilities. Regarding calling for help, one informant asserted the following: "I''m fortunate to have many shifts, and shifts with different people, so I get to know them. It''s that feeling where you know you can trust them, that here is someone who would say,''If I''m in a tight spot, I''ll call anytime,''and I think that''s important to know about people. Whereas with others, you might wonder,''Will they call, or will they just try to handle it themselves?''before it goes completely wrong." P9 To further elaborate on knowing own litmits, humility and awareness of one’s own competencies were also highlighted as important for trust. As one informant described: "As a starting point, I want them to be humble about the task, so I can be sure that they ask for help even when I''m not around. They shouldn''t hesitate. I want to be sure that if they encounter any issues, they communicate rather than do something wrong or drag it out for too long." P1 In addition to calling for help, one informant highlighted the importance of having a strategy and a steady hand. In the following quotation, the supervisor stresses that they have no problem in letting a resident perform a procedure when these apply: "So, if I''ve seen that they have a steady hand, know what they''re doing, and think things through, then I have no problem handing over - they can just call if they run into trouble. But it''s also about those who I know will stop when there''s something, meaning they are aware of their own limitations." P16 Theme 4: departmental solidarity Most informants identified engagement in the department as being crucial to the entrustment of the resident. A resident’s participation in the department’s different responsibilities was an important tool for identifying independence. Thus, autonomy was granted to those who clearly helped when needed, for example, by taking extra shifts and helping on ward rounds. One informant explained this as follows: "It would also mean being willing to go the extra mile. I mean, if you always insist on your rights and leave at 3:08 PM and never take an extra shift. Personally, I believe that contributing a bit to the community is one of the things that helps us keep things together. You must contribute a bit to the community if you want to receive. There''s no doubt that if you don''t do that, it will have a negative impact on your assessment." P13. Another participant stressed: "That everyone is in it together. Both in the operating room, so there''s a shared goal that things go well, but also in the somewhat duller tasks like ward rounds and handling emergency patients that come in. That''s where everyone pitches in." P5. Solidarity was identified as a dynamic construct that unfolded among peers. Participants emphasized tasks in the surgical unit that varied in their degree of level of interest and popularity. It was underscored that trainees should recognize the significance of sharing the more interesting and less popular tasks. Peer solidarity encompassed the principle of being a good and fair colleague who understood the need for all trainees to achieve a well-rounded operative volume. This is exemplified as important by a supervisor in the following quote: "It has to be fair. If someone comes in with sharp elbows, pushing the others aside, they won''t get my approval. That''s unfair." P1 "And as far as I can see, I can easily come across younger doctors who meet all those requirements but whom I would never want to have anything to do with in a workplace." P11 This theme unfolded across three key aspects. Firstly, there was an emphasis on the importance of completing tasks and helping each other. Secondly, an understanding of the importance of all tasks, not just the ones perceived as interesting, was stressed. Lastly, fostering solidarity and fairness among peers, encouraging an acknowledgement that all residents should have equal opportunities to engage in the more appealing aspects of the work. The components - Approach, Relational competencies, Technical skills and knowing own limits, and departmental Solidarity (ARTS) - appeared to serve as an entry ticket to the gradual granting of independence. These four interconnected themes can be represented in the acronym presented in Figure 1 . Fig. 1. Open in a new tab ARTS Discussion In this study, we interviewed 19 surgical specialists regarding how they as supervisors based their trust decisions when granting autonomy to surgical residents. Our study shows that supervisors adopt a holistic view on trust, and their perspectives on autonomy extend beyond relying solely on assessment tools. Technical skills are not the sole criterion for participation in surgical activities, as how a surgical trainee appears in all the department’s different tasks impact the granting of autonomy. Our findings underscore the importance of acknowledging learning opportunities outside the OR, particularly relational competencies, and the ability to interpret the social dynamics within a department. Thus, our findings resonate with Jensen et al. [ 23 ] that emphasize that learning surgery is not only about performing or doing surgery, but also about being a surgeon. While Jensen et al. [ 23 ] use practice theory to investigate how the person-environment fit influences surgical education, the present study adds that supervisor judgement is dependent on relationships, environment, personal characteristics, and skills. Previous studies have explored factors that impact the surgeon''s intraoperative trust. For example, [ 6 , 7 ] interviewed gynaecological supervisors on the use of entrustment evidence when determining the level of autonomy. While Chen et al . [ 6 ] used self-determination theory and previous surgical cases as factors when granting operative trust, the present study highlights a broader holistic perspective that includes departmental solidarity and approach. In a nationwide survey, [ 40 ] concluded that supervisors consider the most important factors for entrustment in the OR to be directly observed clinical skills. The findings from the present study differs from this and add the importance of competencies observed outside the OR. Ahle et al . [ 1 ] interviewed program directors who stated that they relied on a faculty network when making entrustment decisions regarding trainees. Again, our results differ and add to their results that trusting in this context is also personal and intersubjective. The findings in the present study have similarities with the OpTrust framework, particularly in how the categories "Operative Plan" and "Leadership" correspond with our categories "Approach" and "Technical Skills and knowing own limits" (1, 2). These similarities suggest a shared understanding of key competencies, where planning and strategic execution align with the approach taken during surgery, and leadership is reflected in the ability to perform technical tasks effectively while being aware of one''s limits. Where OpTrust is an intraoperative framework, the findings in the present study emphasize the informal assessment outside the OR. To sum up, while our findings align with previous literature, we have identified that granting procedural autonomy is even more complex and relates to both static and dynamic factors that fluctuate across trainees and assessment scenarios. This understanding of trust goes beyond procedural and assessment measures. Theoretically, we find inspiration to unfold this in Aristotle''s three intellectual categories of knowledge: episteme, techne, and phronesis. Episteme is often translated as "scientific" knowledge and refers to systematic, theoretical, and general knowledge that is gained through logical reasoning and empirical observations. Techne refers to practical and context-specific knowledge [ 15 ]. In the present case, these two categories relate to medical knowledge and technical skills. Phronesis, on the other hand, is knowledge regarding context-dependent choices in situations not governed by universal rules [ 15 ]. Both episteme and techne are familiar to surgeons and relate to knowledge and skills that can be trained, learned, and assessed [ 12 ]. However, it appears that the supervisors in this study evaluated phronetic knowledge in residents. Residents reflected phronetic knowledge by demonstrating departmental solidarity, recognizing the importance of a fair distribution of operations, and being able to perceive these aspects as valuable. Thus, it seems that the phronetic dimension of how supervisors build trust has been overlooked in many existing assessment tools. Another important aspect of our findings relates to the literature of the hidden curriculum. In medical education research, the hidden curriculum refers to the informal and often unintended cultural norms, values, and behaviors that are imparted to learner outside of the formal curriculum. Hafferty et al. framed this concept as a socio-cultural understanding, emphasizing how situated learning shapes students and influences their future professional enactments [ 18 , 19 ]. Our findings suggest that ARTS transcends the formal surgical curriculum by promoting autonomy supportive behaviors. Additionally, Hill et al. conducted an interview study that explored medical students'' perceptions and experiences regarding the hidden curriculum in surgery. They described how the hidden curriculum reflects a complex interplay of "ticking the boxes" (meeting formal requirements) and "walking the talk" (adaption to the surgical culture), which enables students to participate more fully in the surgical environment [ 22 ]. Another aspect of the hidden curriculums is unspoken expectations where there may be unarticulated rules about how to behave in various situations. The findings in the present study adds to the study from Hill et al. by exemplifying what the adaptation to the surgical culture could include and by putting words to the unarticulated expectations to behaviors inside and outside the OR that supervisors have to residents in the department. Hopefully the ARTS framework can be used by surgical educators to start verbalising the hidden curriculum to the learners in their department and be explicit in their expectations to the residents. To unfold the ARTS framework within a department, one could employ the use of OSATS as an example. OSATS is described as the objective assessment of surgical skills [ 27 ]. However, Heimstra et al . [ 21 ] show that this is only an intended objective, as the assessment can never be totally free from subjectivity when the assessment is based on individuals’ opinions [ 21 ]. The findings form the present study, underscores this. If a department depends solely on assessment tools and defined objectives, it may overlook the ARTS dynamics and the alignment of the person-environment fit, neglecting how a trainee adapts and navigates within a specific department. At an organizational level, it is important that the surgical department acknowledge, recognize, provide support for, and engage in discussions regarding the different environmental layers of a trainee’s development. Based on this study it cannot be decided if one or some of the themes in the ARTS framework are more important than other. In continuation, it would seem reasonable that some residents perform well without ticking all the boxes in the framework. However, a qualitative interview study does not aim to measure, compare, or weight themes against each other. There are limitations to this study. The study is based on interviews with informants from a single country. The selection of informants was purposive in order to gain in-depth knowledge about the specific surgical culture in the country rather than include a variety of contexts with different cultural expectations, healthcare regulations, and educational structures. Even though we included surgeons from different geographic employments, sex, and years of expertise, they were all from the same profession and speciality. Future research could explore the degree to which our findings resonate with other surgical specialities or other personnel in the surgical learning environment such as anaesthesiologists or OR nurses. An ethnographic approach to observing supervisor-trainee interactions outside the OR could provide information to the discussion. Due to methodological limitations, the findings do not provide clear indications of whether whey reflect current supervisory practices or represent ideal practices that we aim to achieve. The findings may provide a foundation for critical discourse on the criteria that should inform decisions regarding the granting of trust, as well as the essential components that ought to be integrated into assessment frameworks. The ARTS framework could serve as a concrete example of how the conversation in a department can be structured to accommodate such discussions. Future studies are needed to investigate if the framework can play a role in shaping a surgical curriculum, guide evaluations of trainees during residency competency committee meetings or fostering environmental alignments with new colleagues. In order to explore the integration of the ARTS framework into the daily educational responsibilities of surgical departments, future research is needed. The incorporation of ARTS into the educational discourse within a department holds the promise of cultivating a shared and common language. Such integration has the potential to enhance the likelihood of departments adhering to their educational responsibilities. Supplementary Information Supplementary Material 1. (32.1KB, docx) Acknowledgements The authors wish to thank the participants for sharing their time and stories. Abbreviations EPA Entrustable Professional Activities OR Operating room OSATS Objective Structured Assessment of Technical Skills NOTSS Non-Technical Skills for Surgeons GT Grounded theory Authors’ contributions KBH, RDJ have developed the design of the study, performed the acquisition of data collection and analysis, interpreted the data and drafted and revised the manuscript. CP, JEM and AHM have made substantial contributions to the design of the study, analysis and interpretation and the revision of the manuscript. All authors approved the submitted version. Funding None. Data availability The dataset used and analysed during the current study are not publicly available due to the relatively small number of participants and the possibility of compromising anonymity/individual privacy; however, data may be made available upon a reasonable request. Please, put in this request to corresponding author (karhesse@rm.dk). Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate The current study was conducted in full compliance with the ethical principles outlined in the Helsinki Declaration. The study was exempt from a formal review by the Central Denmark Regional Ethics Committee (J. No. 1–10-72–1-21). The project was approved by the Institutional Review Board at Aarhus University (R. No. 233066) and registered with the Central Denmark Region Trial Registration (J. No 1–16-02–158-21). Informed consent was obtained from all study participants. All methods were carried out in accordance with the relevant guidelines and regulations. Consent for publication Not applicable. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Ahle SL, Gielissen K, Keene DE, Blasberg JD. Understanding entrustment decision-making by surgical program directors. J Surg Res. 2020;249:74–81. 10.1016/j.jss.2019.12.001. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Blomqvist K. The many faces of trust. Scand J Manag. 1997;13(3):271–86. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Brasel KJ, Klingensmith ME, Englander R, Grambau M, Buyske J, Sarosi G, Minter R. Entrustable professional activities in general surgery: development and implementation. J Surg Educ. 2019;76(5):1174–86. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 4. Carlsen CG, Lindorff-Larsen K, Funch-Jensen P, Lund L, Morcke AM, Ipsen M, Charles P. Is Current Surgical training efficient? a national survey. J Surg Educ. 2014;71(3):367–74. 10.1016/j.jsurg.2013.10.002. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Charmaz K, Belgrave L. Qualitative interviewing and grounded theory analysis. The SAGE handbook of interview research: the complexity of the craft. 2012;2:347–65. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Chen XP, Sullivan AM, Alseidi A, Kwakye G, Smink DS. Assessing residents’ readiness for or autonomy: a qualitative descriptive study of expert surgical teachers’ best practices. J Surg Educ. 2017;74(6):e15–21. 10.1016/j.jsurg.2017.06.034. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Chen XP, Sullivan AM, Bengtson JM, Dalrymple JL. Entrustment evidence used by expert gynecologic surgical teachers to determine residents’ autonomy. Obstet Gynecol. 2017;130(Suppl 1):8s–16s. 10.1097/aog.0000000000002201. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Crabtree BF, Miller WL, Swenson MM. Doing Qualitative Research. Nurs Res. 1995;44(4):254. 9. Cristancho SM, Apramian T, Vanst