y Park: Sage. 1990. 39. Strauss A, Corbin J. Basics of qualitative research techniques. 1998. 40. Teman NR, Gauger PG, Mullan PB, Tarpley JL, Minter RM. Entrustment of general surgery residents in the operating room: factors contributing to provision of resident autonomy. J Am Coll Surg. 2014;219(4):778–87. 10.1016/j.jamcollsurg.2014.04.019. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 41. Ten Cate O. Competency-based education, entrustable professional activities, and the power of language. J Grad Med Educ. 2013;5(1):6–7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] 42. Ten Cate O. Entrustability of professional activities and competency-based-training. Med Educ. 2005;39:1176–7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 43. Varpio L, Ajjawi R, Monrouxe LV, O’Brien BC, Rees CE. Shedding the cobra effect: problematising thematic emergence, triangulation, saturation and member checking. Med Educ. 2017;51(1):40–50. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Watling CJ, Lingard L. Grounded theory in medical education research: AMEE Guide No. 70. Med Teach. 2012;34(10):850–61. 10.3109/0142159X.2012.704439. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Woelfel I, Smith BQ, Strosberg D, Villarreal M, Harzman A, Salani R, Cochran A. Residents’ method for gaining operative autonomy. Am J Surg. 2020;220(4):893–8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Woelfel I, Wang T, Pieper H, Meara M, Chen XP. Distortions in the balance between teaching and efficiency in the operating room. J Surg Res. 2023;283:110–7. 10.1016/j.jss.2022.10.032. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. Yiu S, Yeung M, Cheung WJ, Frank JR. Stress and conflict from tacit culture forges professional identity in newly graduated independent physicians. Adv Health Sci Educ Theory Pract. 2023;28(3):847–70. 10.1007/s10459-022-10173-z. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 48. Yule S, Flin R, Paterson-Brown S, Maran N, Rowley D. Development of a rating system for surgeons’ non-technical skills. Med Educ. 2006;40(11):1098–104. 10.1111/j.1365-2929.2006.02610.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Yule S, Paterson-Brown S. Surgeons’ non-technical skills. Surg Clin North America. 2012;92(1):37–50. 10.1016/j.suc.2011.11.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplementary Material 1. (32.1KB, docx) Data Availability Statement The dataset used and analysed during the current study are not publicly available due to the relatively small number of participants and the possibility of compromising anonymity/individual privacy; however, data may be made available upon a reasonable request. Please, put in this request to corresponding author (karhesse@rm.dk).',9,'2025-10-13 08:55:40','2025-10-13 08:55:40');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2016,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10433882','Identifying Strategies for Struggling Surgery Residents.','Santosa KB, Lussiez A, Bellomo TR, Matusko N, Gauger PG, Choi JN, Sandhu G.','2022','troublesome torbeck remediating remediate torbeck vaguely kbs provo dropdown subscribers subscribers favorship favorship declarative declarative intraop intraop declarative favorship declarative fielded remediate torbeck delegate neuropsychologist neuropsychologists neuropsychologist overseen declarative relatable repercussion taxing subjecting neuropsychologists supp1 nihms1899827 supp1 supp2 nihms1899827 supp2 robbi kupfer t32ca009672 crannell brasel tekian sanfey dunnington minter dunnington terhune lentz lyness torbeck schwind dunnington limbrick programdirectors remediating learnerdue franzese audetat laurin dory sheikhly ostlundh arayssi schwed salcedo shweikeh schwed nfonsam cogbill stolarski oriel whang kristo bongiovanni moffit kinzie brunett kratzke yefimov neurofeedback warburton goren dine guerrasio rumack aagaard remedial supp1 nihms1899827 supp1 supp2 nihms1899827 supp2','Abstract Background: Struggling residents are not uncommon in general surgery. Early identification of these residents and effective remediation remain imperfect. Materials and methods: We performed a survey of program directors (PD) across all general surgery residencies. Survey questions included the following: demographic information about the program and PD, 10 vignettes about hypothetical residents struggling in various ACGME milestones to assess how PDs would address these deficiencies, and self-reported PD preparedness and availability of resources to support struggling residents. Results: In total, we received 82 responses to our survey. All PDs who participated in our study reported having struggling residents in their program. The three most common ways struggling residents are identified were faculty word-of-mouth, formal evaluations such as milestones and ABSITE performance, and resident word-of-mouth. Over 18% of PDs reported having little to no relevant training in addressing the needs of a struggling resident, and 65.9% of PDs did not feel that their program had ‘completely adequate’ resources to address these needs. In the majority of cases, PDs offer mentorship with themselves or other faculty as a remediation strategy with infrequent use of other resources. Conclusions: Strategies to identify struggling residents and remediation strategies varied widely across programs. Diversifying remediation approaches should be considered for more effective remediation. Keywords: Struggling residents, General surgery residency, Remediation in residency Introduction Nearly all residents will struggle at some point during their training. 1 , 2 Despite how commonplace struggling residents are in any given program, early identification remains a challenge partly due to the lack of meaningful standards and clear performance evaluation strategies available. 1 , 3 , 4 Recognizing that there are many ways to define ‘struggle’, here, we refer to an academic, personal or other factors that influence the progression and/or professional development of a resident. In an attempt to address this gap with progression metrics, the Accreditation Council for Graduate Medical Education (ACGME) developed milestones to serve as a framework to assess resident physicians as they advance through their training. ACGME milestones are organized by six general competency domains: medical knowledge, patient care, professionalism, interpersonal and communication skills, systems-based practice, and practice-based learning and improvement. 5 However, despite its national implementation across surgical training programs, early identification of struggling residents continues to be troublesome as there is no uniform way to measure these milestones. 6 Further, Torbeck et al. showed that the challenge in remediating residents varied based on the milestone in which they were deficient, with few programs having strategies to remediate residents struggling with systems-based practice and practice-based learning and improvement. 7 Because of this, the responsibility and burden of identifying and subsequently developing remediation strategies for struggling residents often fall on the program director (PD) and other faculty members, and it is unclear if progress has been made toward innovative remediation strategies following the work of Torbeck et al. 8 – 10 The current model of surgical training has transformed from the apprenticeship model toward one where residents spend less time with more faculty members, making it challenging to identify struggling residents. 11 , 12 Additionally, faculty are often reluctant to make broad judgments on a resident’s clinical performance after such brief interactions. 4 Currently, there is no formal definition of the struggling resident’ in General Surgery training programs, leading to variable approaches for deciding when a resident requires remediation or other support. Moreover, many faculty are often unsure what to do after identifying a struggling resident, and interventions frequently consist of ‘more of the same’ strategies. 13 , 14 Or, for example, residents may be vaguely labeled as someone who ‘cannot operate’ with subsequent nonspecific feedback such as ‘needs more experience’. 3 Although PDs are likely to face struggling residents during their tenure as PD, there remains a paucity of literature outlining ways to identify and subsequently create remediation strategies for these individuals. We performed a cross-sectional study of PDs in general surgery to describe how PDs identify struggling residents and highlight tools and training processes for PDs that may enhance their ability to support struggling residents in general surgery. Identifying struggling residents and strategies to support them are critically important not only to the program but also more important to the affected individual, their future patients, and their eventual surgical practice. Materials and Methods Survey development The survey was drafted by three study authors (KBS, PG, and GS) and reviewed by four PDs in other surgical subspecialties at Michigan Medicine. After these PDs provided feedback regarding content, clarity, and readability of questions, the 35-question survey was revised, developed in Qualtrics (SAP, Provo, UT), and administered via an online platform. Survey questions included demographic information about the program (e.g., type, regional location, number of residents) and the responding PD (e.g., length of service as PD, gender, race/ethnicity and academic rank). We created 10 vignettes about hypothetical residents struggling in various ACGME milestones to assess how PDs would address these deficiencies. Additionally, we assessed self-reported preparedness and the availability of resources to support struggling residents. Question types included Likert scales, multiple choice, dropdown box, and free responses ( Appendix 1 ). Survey dissemination The finalized survey was submitted for review to the Association of Program Directors in Surgery (APDS) research committee and approved for distribution. The Qualtrics link was distributed through the APDS listserv along with an email, including a detailed explanation of the study, which was conducted to improve our understanding of and interventions for struggling residents. Of note, the APDS listserv is voluntary, and the exact numbers and breakdown of subscribers are unavailable. According to the APDS, the listserv includes over 400 active subscribers that include PDs, Associate PDs, and Administrators associated with General Surgery residency programs. We requested that PDs complete the following Qualtrics survey with instructions that participation was voluntary and responses would be anonymous. The survey was open from July 2020 to August 2020. Three email reminders to fill out the survey were disseminated by the APDS. This research study was reviewed and deemed exempt by the University of Michigan Institutional Review Board. Statistical analysis Sample characteristics are reported as percent and number of observations for categorical variables and median and interquartile ranges (IQR) for continuous variables. Sum scores over the ten vignettes were calculated to assess how likely PDs were to use each remediation strategy, where higher scores indicate a higher likelihood. Mann—Whitney and/or Kruskal—Wallis tests were used to assess differences in how likely PDs were to use various remediation strategies by PD training, PD resource adequacy, program resident volume, and remediation favorship. Remediation favorship was categorized based on responses to question 22 ( Appendix 1 ), in which respondents were asked, ‘What proportion of struggling residents will benefit from remediation and graduate from general surgery training?’ Responses to this question, including ‘very few,’ ‘some,’ or ‘about half’ were considered not to favor remediation; responses, including ‘more than half’ or ‘all’ were considered to favor remediation). All analyses were conducted in STATA 15 15 and R version 3.6.2, and significance was set at P < 0.05. Results Demographics of PDs and characteristics of residency program Due to the anonymity of the survey and based on the assumption that there are 336 General Surgery residency programs nationwide, we estimate a response rate of about 24.4% (82 responses out of 336 programs). More PDs who responded were male (46.3%) and white (52.4%). Most PDs held academic ranks consisting of Professor (22.0%), Associate Professor (19.5%), and Assistant Professor (17.1%), and only a minority of PDs were Chair or Section Head (7.3%). The median length of time served as PD was 5 (IQR = 7.5) y, noting that our survey included PDs who had been PD for 1 to 23 y ( Table ). Table – Demographics of 82 survey respondents included in this study. Characteristics Overall ( n = 82) Year highest level of clinical training was completed Median [Min, Max] 2000 [1980, 2020] No response 27 (32.9%) Years as program director Median [Min, Max] 5 [1, 23] No response 27 (32.9%) Academic rank Professor 18 (22.0%) Assistant professor 14 (17.1%) Associate professor 16 (19.5%) Chair or section head 6 (7.3%) No response 28 (34.1%) Gender Female 18 (22.0%) Male 38 (46.3%) No response 26 (31.7%) Race Asian American 8 (9.8%) Hispanic, or Latino or Spanish origin 1 (1.2%) White 44 (53.7%) African American 0 (0.0%) Not listed 4 (4.9%) Prefer not to answer 1 (1.2%) No response 26 (31.7%) Program type Community-based 18 (22.0%) University 40 (48.8%) University-affiliated 20 (24.4%) No response 26 (31.7%) Program demographic Rural 11 (13.4%) Suburban 13 (15.9%) Urban 54 (65.9%) No response 4 (4.9%) Program location East North Central (IL, IN, MI, OH, WI) 18 (22.0%) East South Central (AL, KY, MS, TN) 3 (3.7%) Mid Atlantic (NJ, NY, PA) 12 (14.6%) Mountain (AZ, CO, ID, MT, NM, NV, UT, WY) 4 (4.9%) New England (CT, MA, ME, NH, RI, VT) 9 (11.0%) Pacific (AK, CA, HI, OR, WA) 8 (9.8%) South Atlantic (DC, DE, FL, GA, MD, NC, SC, VA, WV) 9 (11.0%) West North Central (IA, KS, MN, MO, ND, NE, SD) 9 (11.0%) West South Central (AR, LA, OK, TX) 6 (7.3%) No response 4 (4.9%) Total number of residents in the program Median [Min, Max] 32 [10, 75] No response 5 (6.1%) Resident struggle observed Yes 78 (95.1%) No response 4 (4.9%) Open in a new tab The most common program type was University-based (48.8%), followed by University-affiliated (24.4%) and Community-Based (22.0%). There were no responses from military-based residency programs. Additionally, most PDs were at programs that were located in urban (65.9%) areas compared to suburban (15.9%) and rural (13.4%). Aside from Puerto Rico, we received responses from PDs from programs across all regions of the United States. The number of residents at programs ranged from 10 to 75, with a median of 32 (IQR = 27) residents per program. Most PDs (67.1%) reported that their program had assigned faculty mentors for incoming interns, but most programs (56.1%) did not have a mentoring program between a senior-level resident and junior-level resident. Identifying struggling residents and ACGME milestones All PDs who participated in our study reported having struggling residents in their program, and on average, PDs identified a median of 5 (IQR = 7) struggling residents during their tenure as PD. The top three ways struggling residents are identified by faculty word-of-mouth, formal evaluations (e.g., as milestones, ABSITE performance, and end of rotation evaluations,) and resident word-of-mouth. Less commonly, struggling residents are identified via self-identification, repeated QI/safety events, and medical student evaluations. Nearly half of PDs (46.1%) felt that more than half or all struggling residents would benefit from remediation. Most PDs (63.4%) felt that only some or a few struggling residents should be advised to pursue a different specialty. Of the 10 ACGME milestones, PDs reported that residents struggled the most with fund of knowledge (median = 3; IQR = 1), clinical judgment (median = 3; IQR = 1), and declarative knowledge (median = 3; IQR = 1) ( Fig. 1 ). The milestones that residents struggle with less include patient and family communication (median = 2; IQR = 0), interprofessional communication (median = 2; IQR = 1) and self-awareness and help-seeking (median = 2; IQR = 1). PDs felt most comfortable addressing deficiencies in task completion (median= 4; IQR = 2), fund of knowledge (median= 4; IQR = 1), declarative knowledge (median = 4; IQR = 1), and technical skills (median = 4; IQR = 1) ( Fig. 2 ). Milestones in which PDs were less comfortable addressing included self-awareness and help seeking (median = 3; IQR = 2) and patient and family communication (median = 3.5; IQR = 1). Fig. 1 – Open in a new tab Frequency of resident struggle in ACGME milestones. Median and IQR were plotted for each milestone with gray representing the frequency with which respondents identified struggle on a 0 to 3 scale (never, rarely, sometimes, very often). intraop decisions = intraoperative decision-making; knowledge fund = funds of knowledge; tasks = task completion; self-awareness = self-awareness and help-seeking; patient communication = patient and family communication; professional communication = interprofessional communication. Fig. 2 – Open in a new tab Comfort addressing resident struggle in ACGME milestones. Median and IQR were plotted for each milestone, with gray representing the comfort level respondents felt with addressing these struggles on a 0 to 4 scale (not at all confident, slightly confident, moderately confident, quite confident, extremely confident). intraop decisions = intraoperative decision-making; knowledge fund = funds of knowledge; tasks = task completion; self-awareness = self-awareness and help-seeking; patient communication = patient and family communication; professional communication = interprofessional, communication. Resources available to support struggling residents Most PDs (53.7%) reported that the resources at their respective institutions were adequate (moderately adequate, 17.1%; largely adequate, 31.7%; completely adequate, 4.9%) in addressing the needs of a struggling resident in their programs. The resources that are most commonly available at different programs include faculty mentorship, mental health support, and simulation skills laboratories. In contrast, the resources that are less commonly available include neuropsychiatric testing, visual testing, and video recording and feedback, but these are the resources that PDs reported would be beneficial in having to address the needs of a struggling resident. When we asked PDs how adequate the training was that they had received to address the needs of a struggling resident, over one-quarter of PDs reported having little to no relevant training. Resources that were most helpful to PDs for addressing the needs of struggling residents included advice from program director colleagues in the same specialty or in another specialty (median = 3; IQR = 1) and the Association of Program Directors in Surgery (APDS) workshop (median = 3; IQR = 1) ( Fig. 3 ). Overall, most programs had processes for residents who struggle with funds of knowledge and professionalism. However, there was less consistency across programs in the existence of formal processes for the other milestones. Fig. 3 – Open in a new tab Rating of resource quality for resident instruction. Respondents were asked to rate how adequate each resource was in addressing the needs of a struggling resident on a 1 to 3 scale (not well at all, somewhat well but not ideal, and really well). The median and IQR for each resource were plotted. prior PD advice = advice from previous program director; PD advice = advice from program director colleagues; APDS workshop = Association of Program Directors in Surgery (APDS); GME = support from Office of Graduate Medical Education (GME); personal experience = institutional faculty development ‘on the job’ personal experience; CME = participation in other continuing medical education (CME); formal policy = reading formal policies; literature = reading literature. When PDs were asked how to address the needs of struggling residents across different milestones, mentoring with faculty and mentoring with PD were the two most favored strategies used. e.g., PDs most frequently employed mentoring with faculty for five of the 10 deficiencies (i.e., intraoperative decision-making, clinical judgment, declarative knowledge, patient and family communication, and technical skills). Additionally, PDs utilized mentoring with PDs for deficiencies in interprofessional communication and professionalism. Less frequently selected strategies were utilized for deficiencies in self-awareness (refer to a specialist), funds of knowledge (develop study plan), and task completion (mentoring with residents). Comparisons by PD training, resource adequacy, program resident volume, availability of faculty and resident mentorship programs, and remediation favorship To compare the training of PDs for the position, we categorized PDs as having more training than those who self-reported having ‘moderately’, ‘largely’, or ‘completely’ adequate training to working with struggling residents. PDs who reported not having any relevant training, ‘not at all’, or ‘slightly’ adequate training were categorized as having less training. Across each of the milestone vignettes except for the one assessing ‘task completion’, we found no differences in the variety of strategies used for struggling residents between PDs with more or less training. With regards to a resident struggling with task completion, however, we found that PDs with adequate training were more likely to develop a plan (median = 3, IQR = 1 vs. median = 2, IQR = 0; P = 0.017) than those with less training. Next, we examined the adequacy of resources across the different vignettes and found no differences in the use of various strategies between PDs who had more or lesser resources available to them to deal with struggling residents. Program resident volume also did not affect the variety of remediation strategies offered by PDs for struggling residents across all the milestones. Additionally, we sought to compare how programs that assign mentors to junior residents and/or have formal mentoring programs with faculty to those without these mentorship programs support struggling residents and did not find any meaningful differences between the two groups ( Appendix 2 ). Finally, we compared remediation strategies between PDs who favored remediation and those who did not and found no differences in the variety of remediation strategies used to support struggling residents. Thoughts on remediation When PDs were asked what proportion of struggling residents would benefit from remediation, we found that the majority (69.1%) of respondents felt like more than half or all struggling residents would benefit from remediation. Moreover, when asked what proportion of struggling residents should be advised to pursue a different specialty, nearly all (94.5%) respondents felt that only very few or some struggling residents should be advised to pursue a different career path. Discussion The results of this study reveal substantial variation in the methods used to identify struggling residents, preferred remediation strategies, and availability of resources to help struggling residents in need. Residents most frequently struggled with funds of knowledge, declarative knowledge, and clinical judgment. However, despite being a domain in which residents most frequently struggle, clinical judgment was not among the areas that PDs felt most comfortable addressing. In regard to remediation strategies, we found that in the majority of cases, PDs and faculty fielded the burden of remediation despite not always receiving formal training on how to do so. These findings suggest that programs lack the infrastructure and specialized resources to support and remediate struggling residents. Alternatively, it is plausible that resources to support struggling residents exist but may not be effective. Our study extends the growing body of literature showing that although struggling residents are commonplace, effective remediation remains challenging due to lack of structure. 1 – 3 , 13 , 14 In 2006–2007, Torbeck and colleagues 7 surveyed 89 General Surgery programs and found that although the majority of programs reported having policies for resident remediation, half of these programs placed the PD in charge of developing the remediation plan. Our survey results are concordant as we show that PDs often take the task of resident remediation upon themselves by offering individual mentoring. This is concerning, however, as we show that nearly one fifth (18%) of PDs report having little to no relevant training in addressing the needs of a struggling resident. Further, our study suggests little progress has been made over the past 15 y to innovate remediation plans that best support residents. Supporting a struggling resident may be perceived by other faculty as the responsibility of the PD, which could explain the findings that PDs often lead to remediation strategies. Faculty other than PDs may feel like their responsibility of supporting a struggling resident is to report their concerns to the PD, who will then create and implement a remediation plan for the struggling resident as opposed to taking a more active role in the remediation strategy of such a resident. Future studies are necessary to evaluate the perception of other faculty regarding their role and responsibilities of supporting a struggling resident. Without effective remediation, residents are at risk for several unfortunate consequences, including attrition, patient harm, compromise to their own well-being, and/or ultimately, lack of preparedness for independent practice. 10 , 16 – 18 As such, considering remediation strategies outside of mentorship from a PD or other faculty member who may not have the bandwidth or expertise is important in order to optimize the remediation of a struggling resident. 19 , 20 Prior work shows that when clinical teachers are unsure how to address clinical reasoning deficiencies, there are delays to intervention, and moreover, the interventions tend to focus on organizational aspects of residency instead of fitting the remediation strategy to the root of the problem. 13 This highlights the need for PDs to delegate remediation when appropriate and to consider employment of remediation strategies that may seem ‘outside the box’ such as referral to specialists, including neuropsychologist, psychiatrist, or education specialist. For example, if a resident persistently struggles to identify tissue planes as one of our vignettes alluded to, PDs may want to consider referring the resident for visual testing in addition to trying other more conventional options such as mentoring with faculty and/or simulation lab experience. Moreover, residents who may struggle with their performance on the ABSITE or other equivalent in-training examination may benefit from referrals to neuropsychologists 21 or other learning specialists as the underlying issue may not simply be inadequate preparation or fund of knowledge, but an undiagnosed learning disability or performance anxiety. Finally, struggling in any milestone could be a symptom of a mental health issue, necessitating the assistance of a mental health specialist. 22 In our study, we found that these strategies were recommended much less frequently as compared to offering PD/other faculty mentorship, which may in part be due to either lack of availability or a lack of familiarity with these resources. Importantly, we recognize that partnering with specialists such as a neuropsychologist or education specialist may not be available or financially feasible. We offer lower-cost additional strategies that PDs and faculty may consider, such as peer-to-peer mentoring, mental health resources through an institution’s wellness office, and the use of a simulation center. For example, peer-to-peer mentoring can be especially helpful for residents who struggle with funds of knowledge manifesting as poor performance on the ABSITE. Struggling residents could benefit from working with other residents in the program who have previously struggled in this domain and help keep both of them accountable to adhering to a study plan that might be overseen by a PD or other faculty member. Additionally, peer-to-peer mentoring could also be leveraged for deficiencies in clinical judgment, declarative knowledge, task completion, patient and family communication, and interprofessional communication. For self-awareness and help-seeking issues, particularly as it pertains to mental health and/or substance use, we recommend partnering with an institution’s pre-existing infrastructure, such as a wellness office. Finally, encouraging struggling residents to partner with more senior residents or fellows in the program to work on technical skills and intraoperative decision-making could be beneficial and more relatable than a struggling resident who would work with a PD or other faculty member. Although our analysis did not find significant differences in how struggling residents are supported in programs that assign mentors to junior residents and/or have formal mentorship programs with faculty, we call for future qualitative and quantitative work to assess the efficacy of these mentorship programs as they likely offer the benefit that was not captured in the current scope of this work. One major benefit of remediation with nonphysician specialists is the involved personnel or services are removed from a resident’s clinical activities and evaluations, which offers an environment that can focus on the cause of the symptoms. 23 This could be ideal for struggling residents who may already be concerned about the stigma of being labeled as incompetent, which often occurs due to delays in identification of deficiencies. 4 Furthermore, it could protect those who wish to avoid the appearance of vulnerability in the workplace or those who fear repercussion if standards are not met. 20 Separately, it is crucial that we acknowledge the burden of remediation on residents as the commitment can be emotionally taxing and time-intensive. Given this load, every effort should be made for timely recognition of struggling residents and easy access to personalized, effective remediation strategies, a task that is currently difficult given the paucity of literature assessing the efficacy of various interventions. Although some strategies that we have discussed have not been tested for their efficacy, other studies have demonstrated that a multidisciplinary team can be beneficial in supporting a struggling learner. 24 , 25 For example, the University of Colorado School of Medicine created a remediation program for struggling residents composed of medical education specialists who were physicians, communication coaches, and educational psychologists that significantly improved the proportion of struggling residents who graduated and were in good standing. 25 In addition to faculty members, residents and fellows may also be successfully leveraged to coach struggling peers. 24 Given the importance of remediation and support for struggling residents and the potential impact on patient care, we believe future studies evaluating the effectiveness of various strategies are warranted. Our study must be interpreted in the context of its limitations. First, given our methodology and use of the APDS listserv, which includes PDs, associate PDs, and administrators, we were unable to calculate a response rate. Second, we realize many participants did not answer all demographic questions, thus limiting our ability to understand whether this was a nationally representative sample; however, we had multiple responses from all regions in the country and thus, believe our results are generalizable. It is possible that certain demographic information was omitted to ensure privacy as PDs are likely identifiable even with just a few variables. Third, participants were a self-selected group, thus subjecting our study to response bias. Fourth, in this survey, we do not define ‘struggling resident’, which allows PDs to create their own definition and thus, may not be consistent across PDs. Further studies are needed to identify objective measures that qualify a resident to be ‘struggling’, as well as program factors associated with higher proportions of struggling residents. Last, we acknowledge that our survey cannot provide nuanced detail on how PDs approach and manage struggling residents or how their institutional resources can be improved. However, we hope that this exploratory analysis can serve as a foundation for future studies that include a more qualitative approach. Conclusions Strategies to identify struggling residents and remediation strategies varied widely across programs. PDs frequently tend to offer mentorship with themselves or other faculty as a remediation strategy for the struggling resident with less frequent consideration of strategies such as referral to specialists, including neuropsychologists, psychiatrists, or education specialists. Consideration should be given to the use of these unique strategies in order to individualize the remediation of a struggling resident and optimize efficacy. As programs move toward competency-based medical education and entrustable professional activities, assessment programs will be helpful to identify struggling learners earlier in training. Supplementary Material supp1 NIHMS1899827-supplement-supp1.docx (14.6KB, docx) supp2 NIHMS1899827-supplement-supp2.pdf (569.6KB, pdf) Acknowledgment We would like to gratefully acknowledge all the PDs who provided their feedback and expertise to our survey, including Andrew C. Chang, MD; Dawn M. Coleman, MD; Kate H. Kraft, MD; and Robbi A. Kupfer, MD. Funding AL is supported by the National Cancer Institute (T32CA009672). Footnotes Supplementary Data Supplementary data related to this article can be found at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jss.2021.12.026 . Disclosure The authors have no disclosures and report no conflicts of interest. REFERENCES 1. Crannell WC, Brasel KJ. Dealing with the struggling learner. Surgery. 2020;167:523–527. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Park YS, Riddle J, Tekian A. Validity evidence of resident competency ratings and the identification of problem residents. Med Educ. 2014;48:614–622. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Sanfey H, Williams R, Dunnington G. Recognizing residents with a deficiency in operative performance as a step closer to effective remediation. J Am Coll Surg. 2013;216