was associated with burnout (OR, 2.03; 95% CI, 1.48-2.78) and thoughts of attrition (OR, 2.50; 95% CI, 1.61-3.88). Obstetric complications were not associated with any well-being outcomes. Postpartum depression, whether experienced by female residents or partners of male residents, was associated with resident burnout (OR, 1.93; 95% CI, 1.27-2.92), thoughts of attrition (OR, 2.32; 95% CI, 1.36-3.96), and suicidality (OR, 5.58; 95% CI, 2.59-11.99) ( Table 5 ). Table 4. Current Well-Being Among Residents Who Reported Pregnancies While Clinically Active a . Factor No. (%) P value Total Females Males Burnout No 510 (52.3) 136 (52.1) 374 (54.9) .42 Yes 432 (45.1) 125 (47.9) 307 (45.1) Unknown 15 (1.6) NA NA Thoughts of attrition No 835 (87.3) 218 (82.6) 617 (90.2) <.001 Yes 113 (11.8) 46 (17.4) 67 (9.8) Unknown 9 (0.9) NA NA Suicidality No 917 (95.8) 256 (96.6) 661 (95.5) .48 Yes 40 (4.2) 9 (3.4) 31 (4.5) Unknown 0 (0.0) NA NA Open in a new tab Abbreviation: NA, not applicable. a Statistical comparisons were made between female and male residents who reported pregnancies while clinically active. Table 5. Characteristics Associated With Burnout, Thoughts of Attrition, and Suicidality Among Residents Who Reported Pregnancies While Clinically Active. No. of residents (%) Burnout, OR (95% CI) a , b No. (%) of residents Thoughts of attrition, OR (95% CI) b No. (%) of residents Suicidality, OR (95% CI) b Overall 432 (45.1) NA 113 (11.9) NA 40 (4.2) NA Gender Male 307 (45.1) 1 [Reference] 67 (9.8) 1 [Reference] 31 (4.5) 1 [Reference] Female 125 (47.9) 0.89 (0.65-1.22) 46 (17.4) 1.53 (1.01-2.32) 9 (3.4) 0.54 (0.23-1.29) Clinical PGY 1 34 (51.5) 1.19 (0.71-2.01) 9 (13.6) 1.53 (0.72-3.25) 3 (4.5) 1.18 (0.35-3.96) 2/3 142 (47.0) 1.12 (0.81-1.55) 48 (15.9) 1.87 (1.16-3.00) 12 (4.0) 0.82 (0.38-1.76) 4/5 256 (44.6) 1 [Reference] 56 (9.7) 1 [Reference] 25 (4.3) 1 [Reference] Race and ethnicity d Asian 49 (52.1) 1.16 (0.75-1.80) 16 (17.0) 1.48 (0.79-2.78) 9 (9.4) 3.01 (1.26-7.15) Hispan