ab Abbreviations: NA, not applicable; OR, odds ratio; PGY, postgraduate year. a Burnout was defined as symptoms of either emotional exhaustion or depersonalization occurring at least weekly. b Each model excluded residents with missing data for the primary outcome and adjusted for covariates with missing data in the models. Thus, there were a total of 942 residents included in the burnout model, 948 residents included in the attrition model, and 921 residents included in the suicidal thoughts model. c Omitted for collinearity. d Options included American Indian or Alaska Native, Asian, Black or African American, Hispanic or Latinx, Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander, not Hispanic or Latinx, White, other, and prefer not to say. e Includes American Indian or Alaska Native participants, Native Hawaiian or Other Pacific Islander participants, and participants who did not identify with the racial categories provided. Sensitivity Analyses Nine female and 3 male residents were LGB+; none identified as transgender or nonbinary. Gender was not reported by 14 participants and was discordant with ABS records for 1 participant. Excluding LGB+ residents; excluding residents with LGB+, unreported, or discordant gender; or using alternative modeling strategies did not alter the directionality or significance of the results for all but 1 model. Although the point estimates were similar, the association of gender with thoughts of attrition lost significance in the mixed-effects models without any exclusions (eTables 2-6 in Supplement 1 ). Discussion This survey provides the most comprehensive assessment, to our knowledge, of the experience of pregnancy and parenthood in any specialty training program to date. 9 , 11 , 12 , 27 , 37 , 38 Gender disparities exist, with more female trainees delaying childbearing and experiencing pregnancy/parenthood-based mistreatment. Female residents are more likely to experience obstetric complications and postpartum depression than the partners of male residents. Resident mistreatment was associated with burnout and thoughts of attrition, while postpartum depression, whether in the female resident or female partner of a male resident, was associated with burnout, thoughts of attrition, and suicidality. In demonstrating the magnitude of parental discrimination and adverse maternal health outcomes in surgical training, this study provides evidence that pregnancy in procedural training carries measurable risks and that improving support is a necessary step toward gender equity. Noteworthy progress has been made over the last several decades, with pregnancy during surgical training becoming increasingly common. 39 However, the rate of childbearing remains relatively low. Only 10% of female surgical residents had a child during their clinical years of training, less than half the rate of their male peers (22%) and less than the 16% to 35% of female surgical residents reporting a pregnancy in studies with more limited cohorts. 37 , 40 Nearly 50% of female trainees reported they had delayed or were delaying having children because of residency. Given the length of procedural training and declining fecundity in the fourth decade of life, 41 delaying childbearing is likely to increase the risk of future infertility, 41 with significant future health and financial consequences. Studies estimate 20% to 25% of physicians undergo assisted reproductive technology, 38 , 42 among whom 21% are unsuccessful in conceiving. 43 Pregnancy/parenthood-based mistreatment was reported by 58% of expectant female residents, substantially more than the 34% who reported maternal discrimination in a multispecialty convenience sampling of 5244 practicing physicians. 44 Among expectant female residents, mistreatment most often manifested as being encouraged to pursue an “easier” subspecialty because of parental status and being advised against having children during training. Although perhaps well intentioned, the recommendation to choose parenthood over fulfillment of professional goals may negatively influence long-term career satisfaction and thus career longevity. 12 , 17 Moreover, such suggestions perpetuate perceptions of a “motherhood penalty” in which parenthood is an impediment to career advancement. 11 , 18 Fear of such professional repercussions may disincentivize pregnant residents from requesting clinical duty adjustments despite concerns about maternal-fetal health, 11 , 12 , 13 , 37 perhaps explaining why expectant female residents were no more likely to receive schedule modifications than their male counterparts despite the additional physical demands of pregnancy and childbirth. In this study, the prevalence of obstetric complications in female surgical residents was notably higher than those reported in large studies of women of similar socioeconomic privilege: miscarriage occurred in 25.6% (vs 12% in college-educated women), 45 preeclampsia in 6.9% (vs 3.8% in educated women with a master’s degree or higher), 46 and fetal growth restriction in 8.3% (vs 3.2% in women without poor social determinants of health). 47 Prior survey studies of surgeons and surgical trainees report a wide range of complication rates (24%-56%). However, these were conducted using convenience sampling (eg, survey recruitment on social media), precluding response rate calculations 24 , 26 , 27 , 48 , 49 and resulting in nonresponse bias. By including all US general surgery programs with a high response rate, this study addresses the critical limitation of prior work and more accurately reflects the true obstetric complication and postpartum depression rates in trainees of procedural specialties. Although excess maternal morbidity is well-described among highly educated Black women, 50 , 51 we found no association between race and ethnicity and obstetric complications. However, with Black residents representing less than 4% of expectant residents, we suspect we were underpowered to detect a difference. Unlike other studies, 12 , 52 we found no association between obstetric complications and burnout or thoughts of attrition. This difference may be attributable to our ability to survey a complete cohort (ie, minimization of selection bias) and to adjust for mistreatment, a known risk factor for poor well-being. 16 Postpartum depression affected 19% of female expectant residents, surpassing the estimated 9% to 13% prevalence in the general population. 53 , 54 Postpartum depression is a morbid condition associated with suicide, 55 long-term maternal mood disorders, 56 and adverse effects on child development such as cognitive and behavioral developmental delays. 57 Accordingly, we found postpartum depression to be associated with burnout, thoughts of attrition, and suicidality. Suicidal ideation among surgical residents who had experienced pregnancy was more than twice as common as in the general population 58 but similar to that described among the broader population of surgical trainees. 16 This finding is concerning, given that parenthood typically reduces the risk of suicide by 58%. 59 In this study, pregnancy/parenthood-based mistreatment was associated with current burnout and thoughts of attrition. These findings are consistent with prior work demonstrating the detrimental impact of mistreatment on well-being, including alterations in professional aspirations and career disengagement. 17 , 23 , 30 , 31 , 32 Despite evidence of bias in the learning environment 32 and increased voluntary and involuntary attrition among racially and ethnically minoritized residents, 60 no association between race or ethnicity and thoughts of attrition was found in our population. Again, we suspect we were underpowered for this rare outcome. Limitations First, the administration of the survey after the ABSITE may have influenced responses. However, prior data demonstrate that examination-related emotions do not impact responses regarding long-term states of distress such as burnout or suicidality. 61 We believe any theoretical response bias associated with survey timing is offset by the high response rate, far exceeding those obtained in comparable surveys using other recruitment mechanisms. 62 , 63 Second, recall bias is a risk in all surveys. This concern is likely minimal, as studies demonstrate excellent parental recall of evocative experiences like pregnancy and childbirth, 64 , 65 even among nonchildbearing partners. 66 Recall is also presumably augmented in our physician cohort, who tend to remember highly emotional (particularly negative) events 35 , 36 and whom we queried on clinical conditions with objective diagnostic criteria. Third, the study design was cross-sectional, limiting conclusions about causality. Fourth, timing of the survey during the COVID-19 pandemic may have influenced reports of well-being and pregnancy/parenthood-related mistreatment given childcare-associated strains. However, prior studies among broader surgical cohorts 16 , 23 indicated these phenomena predated the pandemic; thus their occurrences cannot be solely attributed to it. Fifth, the limited number of program-level covariates available may result in residual confounding. Sixth, we did not ask male residents to report their partners’ occupations. If some were also surgical residents, these pregnancies would have been “double-counted”; however, this would be expected to bias our results toward the null. Seventh, the association of gender with thoughts of attrition lost significance in 1 mixed-effects model, likely due to underpowering. However, the associations of mistreatment and postpartum depression on thoughts of attrition remained significant. Eighth, we used a single-pass LGB+ 29 rather than a 2-step gender/gender identity and a separate sexual orientation question. We followed recommended practices of explicitly specifying “gender/gender identity,” including nonbinary options, and allowing “select all that apply” to reflect the complexity and fluidity of the concept, 67 and we pilot-tested the item on LGB+ residents who expressed no confusion with the response options; thus we believe it is unlikely there was significant conflation between natal sex and gender/gender identity. Finally, although an important and related topic, we did not characterize the experience of those with primary infertility or adoption, which may lead to underestimation of surgical residents’ negative parenthood-related experiences. Conclusions Despite approaching gender parity, 68 gender disparities in the experience of parenthood during general surgery residency persist. Among resident parents, more female than male trainees report parenthood-related mistreatment. Female residents are more likely to report obstetric complications and postpartum depression than male residents (on behalf of their female partners). To promote and maintain gender equity in procedural specialties, it is essential to build learning environments that support parenthood and safeguard maternal-fetal health. Supplement 1. eAppendix 1. 2021 American Board of Surgery In-Training Exam Survey eAppendix 2. Survey Development eAppendix 3. Statistical Analysis Plan eFigure 1. Definition of the Cohort eFigure 2. Plot of Response Rate per Participating Residency Program eTable 1. Current Well-Being among Residents who Reported Pregnancies while Clinically Active, Compared to Those who Did Not Report Pregnancies eTable 2. Sensitivity Analyses Excluding LGB+ Residents, Using Multivariable Regression Models with Generalized Estimating Equations with Independent Correlation: Obstetric Complications, Postpartum Depression, Burnout, Thoughts of Attrition, and Suicidality eTable 3. Sensitivity Analyses Excluding LGB+ Residents and Those with Missing or Discordant Gender, Using Multivariable Regression Models with Generalized Estimating Equations with Independent Correlation: Obstetric Complications, Postpartum Depression, Burnout, Thoughts of Attrition, and Suicidality eTable 