n values. Short-dash lines represent 25th and 75th percentiles. HPB, hepatopancreatobiliary. RESULTS Attending and resident characteristics Table 1 summarizes attending and trainee characteristics. There were 4,907 resident case evaluations and 425 fellow case evaluations. Attending sex was more often female for fellow cases compared with resident cases (52% vs 30%, p < 0.001). Similarly, trainee sex was more often female for fellow cases compared with resident cases (58% vs 44%, p < 0.001). Among the senior resident case evaluations, 42% were for PGY-4 and 58% for PGY-5. Among the fellow case evaluations, 74% were for PGY-6, 12% for PGY-7, and 15% for PGY-8. Breast and soft tissue case characteristics Table 2 displays the characteristics and evaluations of the breast and soft tissue cases. Lumpectomy, simple mastectomy, and axillary lymph node excisional biopsy were the 3 most common cases among both residents and fellows. Cases missing evaluations from trainees or attendings were excluded from the reported data to allow for meaningful reporting of the statistics. Trainee evaluations were missing for 5% of resident cases and 10% of fellow cases. Attending evaluations were missing for 35% of resident cases and 33% of fellow cases. Only 39% of residents and 48% of fellows rated their performance as practice-ready or better. Attendings rated 67% of residents and 70% of fellows as practice-ready or better. Passive help or supervision only was noted by trainees in 63% of resident cases and 56% of fellow cases. Attendings noted passive help or supervision only for 79% of resident cases and 74% of fellow cases. Table 2. Breast and Soft Tissue Case Characteristics and Evaluations Characteristic and evaluation Resident case (n = 1,804) Fellow case (n = 301) p Value Case Lumpectomy 596 (33.0) 59 (19.6) <0.001 Simple mastectomy 436 (24.2) 99 (32.9) 0.002 Lumpectomy with axillary lymph node dissection 180 (10.0) 12 (4.0) <0.001 Modified radical mastectomy 90 (5.0) 24 (8.0) 0.0