rmance at practice-ready or better for 56% of resident cases and 44% of fellow cases. Passive help or supervision only was noted by trainees in 34% of resident cases and 60% fellow cases. Attendings noted passive help or supervision only for 51% of resident cases and 56% of fellow cases. Table 3. Endocrine Case Characteristics and Evaluations Characteristic and evaluation Resident case (n = 1,479) Fellow case (n = 32) p Value Case Parathyroidectomy 538 (36.4) 6 (18.8) 0.04 Thyroidectomy 650 (43.9) 17 (53.1) 0.37 Thyroidectomy with radical neck dissection 17 (1.1) 1 (3.1) 0.32 Laparoscopic adrenalectomy 274 (18.5) 8 (25.0) 0.36 Trainee evaluation Case complexity Easiest 1/3 112 (7.9) 0 (0.0) 0.17 Average 1,053 (74.6) 26 (86.7) 0.24 Hardest 1/3 246 (17.4) 4 (13.3) 0.81 Trainee performance Unprepared/critical deficiency 94 (6.6) 1 (3.3) 0.72 Inexperience with procedure 102 (7.2) 0 (0.0) 0.16 Intermediate performance 916 (64.9) 15 (50.0) 0.10 Practice-ready performance 298 (21.1) 12 (40.0) 0.03 Exceptional performance 1 (0.1) 2 (6.7) 0.001 Intraoperative supervision Show and tell 94 (6.7) 1 (3.3) 0.72 Active help 842 (59.7) 11 (36.7) 0.02 Passive help 435 (30.8) 14 (46.7) 0.12 Supervision only 40 (2.8) 4 (13.3) 0.01 Attending evaluation Case complexity Easiest 1/3 73 (8.6) 1 (6.3) >0.99 Average 546 (64.5) 14 (87.5) 0.46 Hardest 1/3 228 (26.9) 1 (6.3) 0.08 Trainee performance Unprepared/critical deficiency 44 (5.2) 3 (18.8) 0.07 Inexperience with procedure 23 (2.7) 0 (0.0) >0.99 Intermediate performance 304 (35.9) 6 (37.5) >0.99 Practice-ready performance 414 (48.9) 4 (25.0) 0.07 Exceptional performance 62 (7.3) 3 (18.8) 0.16 Intraoperative supervision Show and tell 44 (5.2) 3 (18.8) 0.07 Active help 368 (43.4) 4 (25.0) 0.15 Passive help 366 (43.2) 9 (56.3) 0.68 Supervision only 69 (8.1) 0 (0.0) 0.40 Open in a new tab Data presented as n (%). Hepatopancreatobiliary case characteristics Table 4 displays the characteristics and evaluations of HPB cases. Pancreaticoduodenectomy was the most common case by residents (46%); distal pancreatectomy was the most common case by fellows (43%). Trainee evaluations were missing for 9% of resident cases and 13% of fellow cases. Attending evaluations were missing for 38% of resident cases and 28% of fellow cases. Resident-rated performance was practice-ready or better for 11% of cases compared with 26% for fellows. Attendings rated performance at practice-ready or better for 34% of resident cases and 68% of fellow cases. Passive help or supervision only was noted by trainees in 17% of resident cases and 18% of fellow cases. Attendings noted passive help or supervision only for 26% of resident cases and 65% of fellow cases. Trainee performance and level of supervision Figure 1 displays the performance of residents and fellows by case type. Performance and supervision data were reported as arithmetic means of evaluations by both trainees and attendings. Fellows achieved practice-ready performance at significantly higher rates, though relatively few residents and fellows achieved practice-ready performance for breast and soft tissue (38% vs 48%, p = 0.001), endocrine (22% vs 41%, p = 0.03), and HPB cases (10% vs 40%, p < 0.001). Figure 2A displays the association between operative performance and level of supervision granted to trainees. Among both residents and fellows, level of supervision correlated closely with performance. Trainees who achieved practice-ready or exceptional performance more often had passive help or supervision only. Conversely, there was poor correlation between case complexity and operative supervision ( Fig. 2B ). Figure 3 displays trainee supervision during cases in which the trainee achieved practice-ready or exceptional performance. Trainees who achieved practice-ready or exceptional performance received supervision only in 17% of resident cases and 18% of fellow cases. Attendings noted supervision only for 21% of resident cases and 25% of fellow cases. Subgroup analysis of senior fellows To benchmark performance and supervision of senior fellows (defined as fellows had advanced beyond their first year of fellowship) and assess the degree to which these trainees are achieving practice-ready performance with minimal supervision, we performed a subgroup analysis cases performed by PGY-7 and −8 fellows. There were 112 cases in this subgroup analysis. Attendings completed evaluations for 81 of these 112 cases. For these 81 cases, attending evaluations indicated that 3 (3.7%) had lowest tertial complexity, 57 (70.4%) as having middle tertial complexity, and 21 (25.9%) had highest tertial complexity. Attendings rated senior fellows’ performance as critical deficiency for 4 (4.9%) cases, inexperienced for 0 cases, intermediate for 19 (23.5%) cases, practice-ready for 48 (59.3%) cases, and exceptional for 10 (12.3%) cases. Attendings rated the amount of supervision provided as show and tell for 4 (4.9%) cases, active help for 19 (23.5%) cases, passive help for 47 (58.0%) cases, and supervision only for 11 (13.6%) cases. Senior fellows completed evaluations for 101 of the 112 cases in the subgroup analysis. Fellows rated their own performance as critical deficiency for 7 (6.9%) cases, inexperienced for 2 (2.0%) cases, intermediate for 51 (50.5%) cases, practice-ready for 41 (40.6%) cases, and exceptional for 0 cases. Fellows rated the amount of supervision provided as show and tell for 7 (6.9%) cases, active help for 48 (47.5%) cases, passive help for 40 (39.6%) cases, and supervision only for 6 (5.9%) cases. DISCUSSION Results from this study indicate that there is significant variability in senior resident and fellow operative performance and level of supervision during surgical oncology cases. Overall rates of supervision only or passive help were low (between 56% and 79%, 34% and 60%, and 17% and 65% for breast and soft tissue, endocrine, and HPB cases, respectively). Fellows achieved practice-ready or exceptional performance at significantly higher rates than senior residents for all case types. Nevertheless, trainees did not achieve practice-ready or better performance in >50% of cases. Trainees achieving higher levels of performance were provided less active supervisory input, but trainees who performed at a practice-ready or exceptional level received supervision only in <25% of cases. Trainees had lower ratings of operative performance and levels of supervision compared with attendings across procedure types. This is not unique to our study. An early analysis of SIMPL data suggested discrepancies between resident and attending evaluation of operative performance. The study found 46% of trainee and faculty performance evaluations were discrepant. In 80% of those cases, the resident rating was lower than faculty. 11 Another single-center study evaluating resident performance of elective cholecystectomy found residents rated themselves at lower levels compared with attendings over multiple procedural domains. 12 Previous literature suggests that the highest-performing residents can be the most critical of their own performance compared with lower performing residents and may be a sign of self-awareness. 13 The reasons why trainees were not provided with decreased supervisory input when achieving practice-ready performance are unclear. The level of active supervisory input by attendings has not been associated with worse patient outcomes. 14 , 15 A recent study using Veterans Affairs Surgical Quality Improvement Program noted increased attending supervision for surgical residents during the past 15 years. 16 Another recent Veterans Affairs Surgical Quality Improvement Program study evaluated the association between resident supervision and patient outcomes for 1,346,461 operations. 17 After adjusting for procedure type and other patient factors, propensity matching demonstrated that cases with attending scrubbed and attending not scrubbed had similar incidence of complication and mortality. A multi-institutional study published by Fieber and colleagues 18 surveyed resident perceptions of supervision and evaluated outcomes using the NSQIP data. Greater perceived resident operative responsibility was associated with more favorable patient outcomes. These findings support the notion that trainees performing at a high level can be safely granted decreased levels of active supervisory input. There are several other barriers to decreasing trainee supervisory input. There is increasing clinical productivity demands for academic surgeons, but reimbursement regulation dictates that attendings must be present in the operating room for “key portions of the procedure” which may adversely affect trainee supervision levels. 19 , 20 There is also public concern about trainee participation in cases. A survey of patients undergoing general surgery procedures found that resident participation is a significant concern to patients, with 45% of respondents stating they would not allow a PGY-1, −2, or −3 to participate in their operation and 7% stating they would never allow any resident participation. 21 There are long-term consequences of inappropriately high levels of supervisory input. Although the short-term goal of patient safety and optimal care is paramount, surgeons and the public must understand the long-term need for a well-trained surgical work-force. 20 Surgeons should counsel patients early in their care about trainee involvement in cases to help patients understand the role of trainees and level of supervision, and the importance of trainee education in the academic surgical mission and the future of surgery as a profession. 22 , 23 Attending surgeon and resident perspectives on supervision often vary. 24 , 25 This point was also noted in the current study, with attendings perceiving lower levels of supervisory intervention than did trainees for all case types. Faculty development programs should ensure that attending surgeons are providing levels of supervision commensurate to performance in the operating room. 26 Entrustable professional activities are being incorporated into general surgery curricula and will provide a valuable tool to further assess trainees and allow for lower levels of supervisory input. 27 Limitations This study was limited by its retrospective design and inherent selection bias. Data about the training programs were not available. The number of fellow cases was much lower than resident cases. Faculty data were missing for 38% of resident cases and 33% of fellow cases. In these cases, the trainee generated an SIMPL evaluation and did not receive an attending response. As noted, there are often differences between trainee and attending perceptions of performance and supervision and this may have affected results. There is risk of selection bias by either trainees or attendings for which cases an evaluation was generated. Of all evaluations generated, 41% of resident evaluations and 57% of fellow evaluations were for women. The percentage of male and female trainees in the study is unknown and there is a risk of sex bias. In addition, the high proportion of female fellows represented in this study may reflect feedback seeking practices being more prevalent among female fellows, though we lack the data to test this hypothesis. There is a risk for rater bias between the resident and fellow cohorts. The SIMPL scoring metrics are evidence-based, though interpretations are often subjective. In addition, there were several potentially important confounding factors (eg overall case volumes for trainees and attendings, beyond those cases that were evaluated using the SIMPL OR application) and outcomes (eg patient-centered outcomes, morbidity, and mortality) that were unavailable in this analysis. Previous experiences with cases by both the faculty and trainee are unable to be accounted for in this analysis. CONCLUSIONS Senior residents and fellows from 103 training programs achieved practice-ready performance in less than half of all breast, endocrine, and HPB cases. Among trainees that did achieve practice-ready performance, <25% were allowed to independently perform critical portions of the operation. These findings suggest that attending surgeons often provide senior residents and fellows with high levels of supervisory input, even for high-performing trainees. This work highlights the importance of both trainee and attending feedback to improve resident performance an ensure decreasing levels of supervisory input. Future work should focus on faculty and trainee development to facilitate decreasing supervisory input in the operating room. CONTINUING MEDICAL EDUCATION CREDIT INFORMATION. Accreditation The American College of Surgeons is accredited by the Accreditation Council for Continuing Medical Education (ACCME) to provide continuing medical education for physicians. AMA PRA Category 1 Credits ™ The American College of Surgeons designates this journal-based activity for a maximum of 1.0 AMA PRA Category 1 Credits ™ . Physicians should claim only the credit commensurate with the extent of their participation in the activity. Of the AMA PRA Category 1 Credits ™ listed above, a maximum of 1.0 credits meet the requirements for Self-Assessment. Support: Dr Balch is supported by NIH grant [T32 GM008721–24]. Footnotes CME questions for this article available at http://jacscme.facs.org Disclosure Information: Authors have nothing to disclose. Timothy J Eberlein, Editor-in-Chief, has nothing to disclose. Ronald J Weigel, CME Editor, has nothing to disclose. Presented at the American College of Surgeons 109th Annual Clinical Congress, Boston, MA, October 2023. Contributor Information Patrick W Underwood, Department of Surgery, The Ohio State University, Columbus, OH. Jeremy A Balch, Department of Surgery, University of Florida, Gainesville, FL. Amanda C Filiberto, Department of Surgery, University of Alabama-Birmingham, Birmingham, AL. Jordan M Cloyd, Department of Surgery, Th