ura M, et al. Attitudes toward electroconvulsive therapy among involuntary and voluntary patients. J ECT. 2019;35:165–169. doi: 10.1097/YCT.0000000000000571. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Ward HB, Szabo ST, Rakesh G. Maintenance ECT in schizophrenia: a systematic review. Psychiatry Res. 2018;264:131–142. doi: 10.1016/j.psychres.2018.03.033. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Wiesing U, Fallgatter AJ. Rationality and freedom in medicine: the case of electroconvulsive therapy. Nervenarzt. 2018;89:1248–1253. doi: 10.1007/s00115-018-0564-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Witzel J, Held E, Bogerts B. Electroconvulsive therapy in forensic psychiatry—ethical problems in daily practice. J ECT. 2009;25:129–132. doi: 10.1097/YCT.0b013e318185fa55. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Zilles D, Koller M, Methfessel I, et al. Ethics, evidence and electroconvulsive therapy. Nervenarzt. 2018;89:839–840. doi: 10.1007/s00115-018-0559-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',18,'2025-10-13 09:51:03','2025-10-13 09:51:03');
